window.QUIZ_DATA={"course":"NURS 251 Introduction to Surgical Nursing","lessons":[{"id":"l01","title":"History of Surgery and Surgical Terms","emoji":"📜","recap":["Early surgery meant pain, bleeding and infection; anaesthesia (1846-47) and antiseptics/asepsis made modern surgery possible.","In Ghana, traditional healers came first, then British surgeons; the Korle-Bu medical school (1964) and local specialist training (2003) grew the workforce, and nurse anaesthetists fill a gap.","Prefixes and suffixes (e.g. -ectomy, -otomy, -plasty, -oscopy, -rhaphy) let you decode surgical words.","Surgery is classified by purpose, urgency, risk, body system, invasiveness, type of procedure and equipment."],"slides":46,"questions":[{"id":"l01-iojft","s":7,"q":"Which statement best defines surgery?","o":["Treating injury or disease mainly with drugs and dressings given after a careful clinical examination of the patient","Diagnosing disease by studying body fluids and tissue samples under a microscope in the laboratory","Preventing disease through hygiene, vaccination and health education delivered to whole communities","Treating injury or disease by cutting or manipulating body parts with special instruments"],"a":3,"e":"Surgery treats injuries or diseases by manipulation or incision of body parts using special instruments."},{"id":"l01-10ycrpl","s":7,"q":"Which three problems dominated surgery before asepsis, analgesia and anaesthesia were discovered?","o":["Severe pain, rapid healing and few infections because wounds were left open","Mild pain, heavy bleeding and allergic reactions to the instruments used","Severe pain, poor appetite and nerve damage from every surgical incision","Severe pain, heavy bleeding and infected wounds that could lead to shock"],"a":3,"e":"Pain, haemorrhage and infection were the hallmarks of early surgery, and patients often went into shock."},{"id":"l01-1h9j4y0","s":7,"q":"What mainly caused wound infection in early surgery?","o":["Strong antiseptics that damaged the skin around the incision","Operating mostly on patients whose immunity was already very low","Unsterilised equipment and no aseptic technique","Wounds closed so tightly that discharge could not drain away"],"a":2,"e":"Equipment was not sterilised and aseptic technique was unknown."},{"id":"l01-bn0nz2","s":8,"q":"Why did early surgeons in Africa, Europe and South America drill holes in skulls (trephination)?","o":["To let out evil spirits believed to be causing the illness","To drain blood that had collected beneath the skull after injury","To relieve pressure from swelling caused by a brain tumour","To place herbal medicine close to where the pain was felt"],"a":0,"e":"Diseases were linked to evil or magic, so skulls were opened to release spirits."},{"id":"l01-1cnrtsi","s":8,"q":"Which procedures did the ancient Greeks and Romans perform after studying anatomy and physiology?","o":["Suturing, amputations, bone setting and bloodletting","Organ transplants, blood transfusion and skin grafting","Skull drilling, exorcism and prayers to remove evil spirits","Caesarean section, hernia repair and cataract surgery"],"a":0,"e":"The Greeks and Romans focused on anatomy and performed suturing, amputation, bone setting and bloodletting."},{"id":"l01-1hepu79","s":10,"q":"Which methods were tried to reduce pain before anaesthesia was discovered?","o":["Ice packs, tight bandages, bloodletting and prayers said over the patient by relatives or priests","Ether, chloroform and nitrous oxide breathed in by the patient shortly before the first cut was made","Lidocaine injected near the incision site combined with spinal injections placed near the spinal cord","Alcohol, mandragora, opium, hypnotism and compression of nerves and arteries"],"a":3,"e":"These were the early attempts to dull pain before true anaesthesia."},{"id":"l01-ofduw6","s":10,"q":"In which years were diethyl ether and chloroform discovered?","o":["1846 and 1847 respectively","1867 and 1888 respectively","1953 and 1964 respectively","1987 and 1997 respectively"],"a":0,"e":"Ether was discovered in 1846 and chloroform in 1847."},{"id":"l01-1rzsgs8","s":10,"q":"What was the pain-free state produced by ether and chloroform named?","o":["Antisepsis","Asepsis","Anaesthesia","Analgesia"],"a":2,"e":"The effect of ether and chloroform was named anaesthesia, allowing pain-free surgery."},{"id":"l01-1mvmm5e","s":10,"q":"Which three developments facilitated the modernisation of surgery?","o":["Analgesics, anaesthesia and antiseptics","Antibiotics, medical imaging and patient monitors, all introduced together in the early period","Sutures, scalpels and operating tables, which were gradually improved during the same century","Opium, alcohol and hypnotism, which were already widely used by surgeons before that time"],"a":0,"e":"Analgesics, anaesthesia and antiseptics together modernised surgery."},{"id":"l01-14ydu6o","s":11,"q":"Antiseptic techniques, to which Pasteur and Lister contributed, mainly reduced which surgical problem?","o":["Intra-operative pain","Haemorrhage","Surgical infection","Anaesthetic overdose"],"a":2,"e":"Antiseptic techniques reduced surgical infection."},{"id":"l01-46bpq1","s":12,"q":"How do continuous monitoring devices help in contemporary surgery?","o":["They sterilise instruments between one operation and the next one so that infection is avoided","They locate the underlying disorder precisely before the first incision is made on the patient","They replace some members of the surgical team during long operations that last many hours","They show the patient's health status so the team can intervene promptly"],"a":3,"e":"Monitors continuously assess the patient before, during and after surgery for prompt intervention."},{"id":"l01-ihxqx6","s":14,"q":"How did native healers in Ghana operate before modern surgery arrived?","o":["Without anaesthesia and without aseptic technique","With herbal anaesthesia but without aseptic technique","With aseptic technique but without any pain relief","Only after training by British surgeons abroad"],"a":0,"e":"They worked without anaesthesia or aseptic technique."},{"id":"l01-ywdu1t","s":14,"q":"Which procedures done by traditional 'surgeons' in Ghana have persisted over the years?","o":["Appendicectomy, hernia repair and caesarean section, which were all performed by healers before doctors arrived","Skull drilling, amputation of limbs and bloodletting, which healers used to treat many different illnesses","Cleft palate repair and hare-lip repair, which healers carried out on infants soon after their birth","Circumcision, female genital mutilation and tribal marks"],"a":3,"e":"Traditional practitioners performed circumcision, female genital mutilation and tribal marks."},{"id":"l01-6wlrl3","s":15,"q":"Why is the work of Wanzams a concern now that HIV exists?","o":["Wanzams often refuse to refer infected clients for antiretroviral treatment, so the infection spreads further","Unsafe circumcision can spread the virus, so modernised practice is hoped for","Wanzams perform only adult circumcision, which carries a higher risk of infection than the infant procedure","Wanzams now need modern theatre equipment which they cannot yet afford, so they must stop practising"],"a":1,"e":"With HIV, it is hoped Wanzam activities will cease or be modernised."},{"id":"l01-1musgas","s":16,"q":"Who is believed to have introduced modern surgical operations with anaesthesia into Ghana?","o":["Ghanaian medical officers","Wanzams","British surgeons","Nurse anaesthetists"],"a":2,"e":"Modern operations are believed to have been introduced by British surgeons."},{"id":"l01-53a36s","s":17,"q":"In 1953 Ghana had only two trained surgeons. How was the shortage managed?","o":["Nurses were trained to carry out all major operations at district hospitals while the two surgeons supervised","All surgical cases were sent abroad to be treated by foreign surgeons until local training could begin","Medical officers were trained for minor cases and complex ones were referred to the surgeons","Surgery was limited to emergencies only until more surgeons could be trained locally in the new school"],"a":2,"e":"The two surgeons trained medical officers for minor cases such as hernias and caesarean sections."},{"id":"l01-1hz5yid","s":17,"q":"What has been the effect of the scarcity of surgeons in Ghana?","o":["Shorter waiting times for planned operations at district hospitals","Greater use of traditional healers for all major procedures","Closure of surgical units in most regional hospitals","Heavier workload for surgeons, and numbers still judged inadequate"],"a":3,"e":"Scarcity increased the surgeons' workload and numbers remain inadequate."},{"id":"l01-zy2sov","s":18,"q":"The first Ghanaian surgeons were trained abroad until which development?","o":["Specialist surgical training began inside Ghana in 2003 under the West African College of Surgeons","A medical school was established at Korle-Bu in 1964","Nurse anaesthetist training began at Kumasi in 1987 to fill the gap left by the shortage of doctors","A peri-operative nursing course was started in 1997 which allowed local staff to replace foreign surgeons"],"a":1,"e":"Training abroad continued until the Korle-Bu medical school opened in 1964."},{"id":"l01-9myroa","s":18,"q":"What has helped to increase the number of surgeons in Ghana in recent years?","o":["Specialist surgical training in Ghana since 2003","Recruiting surgeons from Britain since 1953","Training of nurse anaesthetists since 1987","Opening of the peri-operative nursing course in 1997"],"a":0,"e":"Specialist surgical training in Ghana since 2003 increased the numbers."},{"id":"l01-1y7oh5g","s":19,"q":"Besides private and government hospitals, who occasionally performs surgeries such as cleft palate repair in Ghana?","o":["Traditional healers' associations","Military hospitals only","International organisations","Medical school students"],"a":2,"e":"International organisations perform specific surgeries like cleft palate repair."},{"id":"l01-2f1ys9","s":19,"q":"Why were nurse anaesthetists trained in Ghana?","o":["Because surgeons preferred to work with nurses","Because anaesthetic agents became too costly for doctors","Because the law barred doctors from giving anaesthesia","Because there was a shortage of anaesthetists"],"a":3,"e":"Nurse anaesthetists were trained to fill the shortage of anaesthetists."},{"id":"l01-u4st8m","s":20,"q":"Where and when did nurse anaesthetist training begin in Ghana?","o":["Kumasi in 1987","Korle-Bu in 1964","Ridge Hospital in 2003","37 Military Hospital in 1997"],"a":0,"e":"Training of nurse anaesthetists started at Kumasi in 1987."},{"id":"l01-ujpfyh","s":20,"q":"Who trains Ghana's nurse anaesthetists?","o":["Visiting British surgeons","Specialist surgeons in teaching hospitals","Experienced theatre nurses","Specialist anaesthetists in Ghana"],"a":3,"e":"Nurse anaesthetists are trained by specialist anaesthetists in Ghana."},{"id":"l01-1lpq71s","s":21,"q":"In which year did specialist peri-operative and critical care nursing start in Ghana?","o":["1987","2003","1997","1964"],"a":2,"e":"Peri-operative and critical care nursing started in 1997."},{"id":"l01-gfdhj7","s":23,"q":"What may a prefix signify in surgical terms?","o":["The instrument the surgeon will use for the operation","A part of the body or the procedure to be performed","The urgency and expected duration of the operation","The age group and gender of the patient involved"],"a":1,"e":"Prefixes may signify a body part or the procedure."},{"id":"l01-t4j4si","s":24,"q":"Angiography is an x-ray examination of which structures?","o":["Blood vessels","Brain tissue","Vertebrae","Vaginal walls"],"a":0,"e":"Angio refers to blood vessels."},{"id":"l01-1ck1apt","s":24,"q":"Which prefix relates to the vagina, as in colporrhaphy?","o":["Colono","Lamino","Hepato","Colpo"],"a":3,"e":"Colpo means vagina; colono means colon."},{"id":"l01-lzduj3","s":24,"q":"Which pairing of the brain and vertebra prefixes with their meanings is correct?","o":["Encephal - vertebrae; lamino - brain","Encephal - brain; lamino - vertebrae","Angio - brain; colpo - vertebrae","Colpo - blood vessels; encephal - vagina"],"a":1,"e":"Encephal relates to the brain and lamino to the vertebrae."},{"id":"l01-c0mcjh","s":25,"q":"Cardiomegaly means what?","o":["Inflammation of the heart muscle","Paralysis of the heart wall","Tumour of the heart lining","Abnormal enlargement of the heart"],"a":3,"e":"Cardio means heart and -megaly means abnormal enlargement."},{"id":"l01-r0sjp9","s":25,"q":"A patient has hepatomegaly. Which organ is affected and how?","o":["The liver, which has developed a tumour","The heart, which is abnormally enlarged","The colon, which has been removed","The liver, which is abnormally enlarged"],"a":3,"e":"Hepato means liver and -megaly means enlargement."},{"id":"l01-1rh58pi","s":25,"q":"Which pairing of the colon and within prefixes with their meanings is correct?","o":["Colono - colon; endo - within or inside","Colono - vagina; endo - outside","Hepato - heart; intra - between","Cardio - liver; endo - around"],"a":0,"e":"Colono relates to the colon; endo means within."},{"id":"l01-onrnpz","s":26,"q":"Oophorectomy is surgical removal of which organ?","o":["The uterus","The ovary","The colon","The testis"],"a":1,"e":"Oophor means ovary."},{"id":"l01-1dz2hfn","s":26,"q":"Hemicolectomy removes how much of the colon?","o":["The whole of it","Half of it","One quarter of it","Only its lining"],"a":1,"e":"Hemi means half."},{"id":"l01-ppem58","s":26,"q":"Hydrocele is an accumulation of what?","o":["Pus in a localised pocket under the skin that forms after an infection of a gland","Blood in the chest cavity that collects after an injury to the ribs or the lung","Serous fluid in a cavity such as around the testis","Air in the pleural space after the lung surface ruptures, causing the lung to collapse"],"a":2,"e":"Hydro means water or fluid."},{"id":"l01-1n1ex2b","s":26,"q":"A laparotomy is which of the following?","o":["An examination of the abdomen using a small camera passed through tiny skin incisions","A surgical incision into the abdominal cavity","Removal of part of the abdominal wall muscle to repair a weakness in the area","Suturing of a tear in the abdomen's lining after a blunt injury to the area"],"a":1,"e":"Laparo means abdomen and -otomy means cutting into."},{"id":"l01-di60ir","s":27,"q":"What does the prefix 'dys' signify, as in dyspnoea?","o":["Absent or lacking","Abnormal, bad or difficult","Excessive or rapid","Half or partial"],"a":1,"e":"Dys means abnormal or bad, so dyspnoea is difficult breathing."},{"id":"l01-nfpyxe","s":27,"q":"Which pairing of timing prefixes is correct?","o":["Pre = before surgery, post = after surgery","Pre = after surgery, post = before surgery","Pre = during surgery, post = after discharge","Pre = before admission, post = before theatre"],"a":0,"e":"Pre-operative is before surgery and post-operative is after."},{"id":"l01-1yzyuze","s":29,"q":"How does the suffix -ectomy differ from -otomy?","o":["-ectomy removes a body part; -otomy only cuts into it","-ectomy cuts into a part; -otomy removes it completely","-ectomy repairs a part; -otomy examines it with a scope","-ectomy reshapes a part; -otomy sutures it closed"],"a":0,"e":"-ectomy is surgical removal (mastectomy); -otomy is cutting into (thoracotomy)."},{"id":"l01-15rhta0","s":29,"q":"Gastroscopy is which of the following?","o":["Removal of the stomach","Reshaping of the stomach","Inspection of the stomach","Suturing of the stomach wall"],"a":2,"e":"-oscopy means inspection of a cavity."},{"id":"l01-u3otuf","s":29,"q":"Rhinoplasty is which of the following?","o":["Removal of a tumour from the nose, usually followed by a period of nasal packing","Reconstruction or cosmetic reshaping of the nose","Inspection of the nasal cavity with a lighted instrument to look for polyps or growths","Suturing of a tear in the nose after an injury, to restore a normal outline"],"a":1,"e":"-plasty means to modify or reshape."},{"id":"l01-7mmpn","s":29,"q":"Which suffix means suturing, as in herniorrhaphy?","o":["-plasty","-rhaphy","-otomy","-oscopy"],"a":1,"e":"-rhaphy means suturing."},{"id":"l01-wt6rny","s":29,"q":"Hemiplegia means paralysis of what?","o":["Half of the body","Both legs only","One arm only","The face only"],"a":0,"e":"Hemi means half and -plegia means paralysis."},{"id":"l01-1f37vcy","s":30,"q":"Dysphagia combines 'dys' and 'phagia'. What does it mean?","o":["Difficulty swallowing or eating","Excessive eating","Loss of appetite","Difficulty breathing"],"a":0,"e":"-phagia refers to eating, and dys means abnormal."},{"id":"l01-cdhz0t","s":32,"q":"An abscess is which of the following?","o":["A dilatation of an artery wall","A piece of dead tissue in a wound","A blood clot lodged in a vessel","A localised collection of pus"],"a":3,"e":"An abscess is a localised collection of pus."},{"id":"l01-1wzef1q","s":32,"q":"A patient's artery has become dilated. Which term describes this?","o":["Aneurysm","Embolus","Abscess","Fistula"],"a":0,"e":"Aneurysm is dilatation of an artery."},{"id":"l01-1esx2my","s":32,"q":"Biliary relates to which structures?","o":["Bile, bile duct or gallbladder","Bladder, ureter or urethra","Liver cells only","Blood vessels of the gut"],"a":0,"e":"Biliary relates to bile, the bile duct or gallbladder."},{"id":"l01-1qnkq6c","s":32,"q":"Dysplasia is which of the following?","o":["Complete absence of a tissue or organ from birth because it never began to form","Replacement of one adult cell type by another type in response to chronic irritation","Abnormal development or growth of tissues, organs or cells","A reversible increase in the number of cells in an organ in response to a stimulus"],"a":2,"e":"Dysplasia is abnormal development or growth of tissues, organs or cells."},{"id":"l01-115s9xs","s":32,"q":"A cutaneous lesion occurs where?","o":["In the gut lining","In the armpit","On the skin","In the bile duct"],"a":2,"e":"Cutaneous means relating to the skin."},{"id":"l01-fzbqzn","s":33,"q":"Which term is a benign epithelial tumour of glandular origin?","o":["Hepatoma","Adenoma","Melanoma","Sarcoma"],"a":1,"e":"An adenoma is a benign epithelial tumour of glandular origin."},{"id":"l01-11reffk","s":33,"q":"An embolus is which of the following?","o":["A localised collection of pus in tissue that is walled off by inflamed surrounding cells","A dilated section of an artery wall that bulges outward and may eventually rupture","A blood clot lodged in a vessel, obstructing it","A fragment of dead tissue in a wound that has to be removed before healing starts"],"a":2,"e":"An embolus is a clot that becomes lodged and obstructs a vessel."},{"id":"l01-12j1kyb","s":33,"q":"A fistula is which of the following?","o":["A surgically made opening in a hollow organ to the outside of the body, created on purpose","An abnormal link between two hollow organs, or an organ and the skin","A narrow band of scar tissue joining two organs that are normally kept apart in the body","A fine thread of silk or nylon used to join the edges of tissues together during repair"],"a":1,"e":"A fistula is an abnormal communication between hollow viscera or viscus and skin."},{"id":"l01-1c7jjs1","s":34,"q":"What causes haemorrhoids?","o":["Infection of the glands around the anus which fills with pus and causes severe local pain","A tear in the lining of the lower rectum, usually caused by passing hard stools often","Narrowing of the lower bowel by scar tissue that follows repeated episodes of inflammation","Swelling of veins at or inside the anal sphincter"],"a":3,"e":"Haemorrhoids are caused by venous swelling at or in the anal sphincter."},{"id":"l01-15zq41r","s":34,"q":"A stoma is which of the following?","o":["A fine thread made of silk or nylon used to close the edges of a surgical wound","An artificial opening made in an organ","A piece of dead tissue lying in a wound that has to be removed before it heals","A blood clot that forms in a vessel and blocks the flow of blood to a limb"],"a":1,"e":"A stoma is a surgical, artificial opening in an organ."},{"id":"l01-1qsky2k","s":34,"q":"Inguinal refers to which region?","o":["The armpit","The neck","The groin","The elbow"],"a":2,"e":"Inguinal relates to the groin."},{"id":"l01-c6wr41","s":34,"q":"Ileus, as listed in the lecture, refers to what?","o":["Bile duct stones","Bleeding in the stomach","A tear in the anal lining","Intestinal obstruction"],"a":3,"e":"Ileus refers to intestinal problems such as obstruction."},{"id":"l01-42wt9a","s":35,"q":"How does resection differ from anastomosis?","o":["Resection removes part of an organ; anastomosis connects tubular structures","Resection connects tubular structures; anastomosis removes part of an organ","Resection opens a cavity; anastomosis closes it with sutures","Resection drains fluid; anastomosis removes dead tissue"],"a":0,"e":"Resection is partial removal; anastomosis is a surgical connection between tubular structures."},{"id":"l01-1x6bh3c","s":37,"q":"Which list gives the bases used to classify surgical procedures?","o":["Purpose, patient age, surgeon rank, risk, setting, theatre cost and the equipment that is used","Urgency, type of anaesthesia, duration, anatomical location, theatre size and the total cost of care","Purpose, urgency, risk, anatomical location, extent, setting and equipment","Risk, patient gender, surgeon rank, equipment, anatomical location and the duration of the operation"],"a":2,"e":"These are the seven bases listed in the lecture."},{"id":"l01-1mur0lx","s":38,"q":"Surgery done to confirm a provisional diagnosis falls under which purpose?","o":["Explorative","Curative","Restorative","Diagnostic"],"a":3,"e":"Diagnostic surgery confirms a provisional diagnosis."},{"id":"l01-1w23cfh","s":38,"q":"Which purpose aims to minimise symptoms and reduce suffering?","o":["Curative","Restorative","Cosmetic","Palliative"],"a":3,"e":"Palliative surgery minimises symptoms and suffering."},{"id":"l01-1urxu28","s":38,"q":"Which purpose focuses on improving body and self-image?","o":["Reconstructive","Restorative","Cosmetic","Palliative"],"a":2,"e":"Cosmetic surgery improves body and self-image."},{"id":"l01-qp0slk","s":38,"q":"Which purpose seeks a root cause when the problem has not yet been identified?","o":["Curative","Palliative","Explorative","Transplant"],"a":2,"e":"Explorative surgery seeks to identify problems and find a root cause."},{"id":"l01-1h2ktc1","s":39,"q":"Which description fits emergency surgery?","o":["It must be done soon but can wait for a short time","It is scheduled according to surgeon and facility availability","It is done to avoid disability with several days to prepare","It must be done immediately to save life, limb or function"],"a":3,"e":"Emergency surgery is immediate, to save a life, limb or functional capacity."},{"id":"l01-k8917t","s":39,"q":"What describes semi-elective surgery?","o":["Needed immediately to save a life, limb or functional capacity, with no time for delay","Done for non-life-threatening conditions on a date chosen by the patient and the surgeon","Performed mainly to improve appearance and body image, with no medical need for the operation","Needed to avoid permanent disability or death but can be postponed briefly"],"a":3,"e":"Semi-elective surgery can be postponed for a short time."},{"id":"l01-1srcl3r","s":40,"q":"Which pairing of surgery and risk class is correct?","o":["Heart transplant - minor; removal of an ingrowing toenail - major","Heart transplant - major; removal of an ingrowing toenail - minor","Both heart transplant and toenail removal - major","Both heart transplant and toenail removal - minor"],"a":1,"e":"Major surgery involves great risk; minor surgery minimal risk."},{"id":"l01-1tc7wt7","s":40,"q":"What typifies minor surgery?","o":["Extensive reconstruction of body parts, carrying a great risk to the life of the patient","Minimal alteration of body parts, often correcting deformities, with low risk","Only samples taken for diagnosis, without any incision being made on the skin of the patient","Surgery done solely under general anaesthesia in a theatre by a team of at least three surgeons"],"a":1,"e":"Minor surgery involves minimal alteration and minimal risk."},{"id":"l01-1moic7v","s":41,"q":"Which pairing of surgery and body system is correct?","o":["Thyroidectomy - gastrointestinal; gastrectomy - endocrine","Thyroidectomy - endocrine; gastrectomy - gastrointestinal","Thyroidectomy - respiratory; gastrectomy - endocrine","Thyroidectomy - endocrine; gastrectomy - respiratory"],"a":1,"e":"The thyroid is endocrine and the stomach is part of the GI system."},{"id":"l01-1ogncob","s":42,"q":"What makes laparoscopic surgery minimally invasive?","o":["Use of lasers instead of scalpels to cut the tissue, which avoids bleeding from the cut edges","Small skin incisions through which miniaturised instruments are inserted","Operating under a microscope on very tiny structures such as nerves and small blood vessels","Robot-controlled instruments directed from a console by the surgeon, who sits away from the table"],"a":1,"e":"Laparoscopy uses smaller outer incisions to insert small instruments."},{"id":"l01-1cmt4c0","s":42,"q":"Which are examples of highly invasive open surgery?","o":["Laparoscopic cholecystectomy and skin biopsy","Endoscopy and arthroscopy of the knee","Laparotomy and cardiac surgery","Incision and drainage and suturing of cuts"],"a":2,"e":"Laparotomy and cardiac surgery are open, highly invasive procedures."},{"id":"l01-1k6mkx1","s":43,"q":"Which definition of amputation is correct?","o":["Replacing a body part with one taken from a donor, so that normal function is restored","Rebuilding an injured or deformed body part using tissue taken from another area of the body","Removing tissue only to improve appearance, with no change in the function of the part","Cutting off a body part such as a limb or digit"],"a":3,"e":"Amputation is cutting off a body part such as a limb or digit."},{"id":"l01-1nsyl6i","s":43,"q":"How does cosmetic surgery differ from reconstructive surgery?","o":["Cosmetic improves normal structures; reconstructive rebuilds injured or deformed ones","Cosmetic rebuilds injured parts of the body; reconstructive improves normal appearance for the patient's wishes","Cosmetic replaces diseased organs with donor ones; reconstructive removes diseased tissue from the organ","Cosmetic relieves the symptoms of disease; reconstructive confirms the diagnosis by taking a biopsy"],"a":0,"e":"Cosmetic surgery improves otherwise normal structures; reconstructive repairs injured, mutilated or deformed parts."},{"id":"l01-ijdr7f","s":44,"q":"Which equipment helps surgeons see very small structures during surgery?","o":["A laparoscope, as in minimal-access surgery","A microscope, as in microsurgery","A diathermy machine","A laser beam"],"a":1,"e":"Microsurgery uses a microscope to see small structures."},{"id":"l01-1qnfpjn","s":44,"q":"In robotic surgery, who directs the instruments?","o":["The robot alone, once programmed by technicians","The surgeon, through the robotic system","The scrub nurse, using a remote control panel","The anaesthetist, from the console in the theatre"],"a":1,"e":"Robotic systems control instruments under the direction of the surgeon."},{"id":"l01-1ybupin","s":45,"q":"How should the surgical nurse use terminology?","o":["Everyday language with colleagues and surgical terms with patients and relatives, to show professionalism","Surgical terms with colleagues and everyday language with patients and relatives","Surgical terms with everyone, patients included, so that all information given stays accurate and complete","Everyday language in written notes and surgical terms when speaking, so records are easier to read"],"a":1,"e":"Nurses use correct terms among colleagues and plain language with patients and families."}]},{"id":"l02","title":"The Theatre Environment","emoji":"🏥","recap":["Theatres are located, zoned (unrestricted, semi-restricted, restricted) and equipped to protect the sterile field.","Infection prevention: scrubbing, sterile gown and gloves, cap and mask, sterile drapes; anything sterile touched by unsterile is contaminated.","The surgical team: patient, nurses (scrub and circulating), surgeon, anaesthesiologist or nurse anaesthetist, and surgical assistants.","Instrument classes: cutting/dissecting, grasping/holding, retractors and tissue-unifying; plus the table, light and anaesthetic machine."],"slides":64,"questions":[{"id":"l02-hfgrdr","s":9,"q":"Why must surgery be done in settings with the lowest possible infection risk?","o":["Anaesthesia lowers the patient's resistance to every kind of organism present in the theatre air","The skin's continuity is broken, exposing the patient to infection from the environment","Surgical instruments always carry bacteria that can never be fully sterilised away by any method","Theatre staff are the main source of infection for all patients who undergo an operation there"],"a":1,"e":"Surgery breaks the skin, which predisposes the patient to infection."},{"id":"l02-17q7jhb","s":10,"q":"Where may a minimally invasive procedure such as incision and drainage be done?","o":["Only in an operating theatre fitted with air filtration equipment and a dedicated surgical team","In a relatively clean room such as the surgeon's office or a treatment room","On the ward while other patients are present, with a screen drawn round the bed for privacy","In the recovery room next to the main operating theatre, once the list for the day is finished"],"a":1,"e":"Minor procedures are done in relatively clean treatment rooms; major ones need operating theatres."},{"id":"l02-1edfjfr","s":11,"q":"Why does the location, design and zoning of a theatre follow aseptic principles?","o":["It lowers the cost of building and equipping the theatre unit, which is its main purpose","It preserves the sterile field and supports effective team function","It lets visitors and relatives move freely through all areas of the unit at any time of day","It removes the need for team members to scrub and gown before they enter the theatre itself"],"a":1,"e":"Aseptic design preserves the sterile field and helps the team function."},{"id":"l02-12nv23c","s":12,"q":"Why are theatres sited away from busy OPDs and roads?","o":["To stop noise from disturbing patients on the surgical wards","To leave space for future expansion of the theatre unit","To reduce the risk of infection from high-traffic areas","To slow down the arrival of emergency cases at the theatre"],"a":2,"e":"Away from high traffic lowers infection risk."},{"id":"l02-6t6em0","s":12,"q":"Why are the surgical wards and ICU or recovery wards located close to the theatre?","o":["To allow wards to share sterile instruments with the theatre","To let relatives watch patients being taken in and out","To reduce patient transfer time before and after surgery","To keep the theatre quiet while patients are being admitted"],"a":2,"e":"Nearby wards shorten transfer time before and after surgery."},{"id":"l02-43h4yb","s":13,"q":"Why is the sterilisation unit placed close to the theatre?","o":["To allow instruments to be sterilised and returned while the operation is still going on in theatre","To limit equipment contamination before surgery and biohazards after it","To let the surgical team change clothes and rest without having to leave the theatre unit at all","To keep sterile supplies well away from the main theatre corridor where staff and trolleys pass"],"a":1,"e":"Proximity limits contamination of equipment pre-operatively and biohazard post-operatively."},{"id":"l02-5bzw98","s":13,"q":"Why are changing areas, offices and rest rooms within the theatre unit?","o":["To allow staff to supervise patients on the wards nearby","To keep patients' relatives out of the surgical waiting area","To reduce personnel movement in and out of the theatre","To make cleaning of the theatre floors easier after each case"],"a":2,"e":"This limits staff traffic in and out of the unit."},{"id":"l02-znp31d","s":14,"q":"What is the purpose of special air filtration devices in advanced theatres?","o":["To keep the theatre temperature steady for the surgical team","To remove anaesthetic gases from the air of the operating room","To prevent fires by diluting the oxygen present in the room","To screen out contaminating particles, dust and pollutants"],"a":3,"e":"Filters remove contaminating particles, dust and pollutants from the air."},{"id":"l02-1l7ov0u","s":15,"q":"Which three factors are controlled to reduce fire risk in the theatre?","o":["Fuel, oxygen and sources of sparks","Heat, humidity and noise","Water, light and electrical cables","Alcohol, cotton swabs and plastic drapes"],"a":0,"e":"Fire needs fuel, oxygen and an ignition source, so each is controlled."},{"id":"l02-1hga2gl","s":15,"q":"Why are theatre wall and floor tiles not highly polished?","o":["To prevent glare under the surgical light","To stop dust from sticking to the surfaces","To make cleaning between cases much faster","To reduce the risk of slips and falls"],"a":3,"e":"Highly polished tiles increase the risk of slipping."},{"id":"l02-gw5o9l","s":16,"q":"What marks the boundary of the unrestricted zone, and what dress is allowed there?","o":["Green lines on the floor; scrubs are required","A closed door; only masked staff may enter","Red lines on the floor; only sterile gowns are allowed","Red lines on the floor; outdoor clothing is allowed"],"a":3,"e":"The unrestricted zone has the lowest sterility, red-line boundaries and permits outdoor clothing."},{"id":"l02-1hreq3v","s":17,"q":"Which zone has the highest level of asepsis, where scrubs, caps, masks and footwear are required?","o":["Semi-restricted zone","Restricted zone (operating room)","Unrestricted zone","Transfer zone"],"a":1,"e":"The restricted zone is the operating room, where surgery is conducted."},{"id":"l02-5uck06","s":17,"q":"Which statement about the semi-restricted zone is correct?","o":["It lies within the theatre unit but excludes the operating room; scrubs, caps and footwear are worn","It includes the operating room itself; only masked and gowned staff are allowed to enter the area","It lies outside the red line of the unit; outdoor clothes and shoes are allowed for all who enter","It is the area immediately around the table, reserved for scrubbed team members in sterile gowns only"],"a":0,"e":"The semi-restricted zone is inside the theatre unit but excludes the OR."},{"id":"l02-7fty4e","s":21,"q":"A sterile item touches an unsterile item. How is the sterile item regarded?","o":["Contaminated","Still sterile if the contact was brief","Sterile if it is the top surface","Sterile after wiping with antiseptic"],"a":0,"e":"All sterile items touched by unsterile items are considered contaminated."},{"id":"l02-1gbigzf","s":21,"q":"When are the walls and surfaces within the operating room decontaminated?","o":["Once daily before the first case","Before and after each case","Only after infected cases","Weekly during fumigation"],"a":1,"e":"Surfaces are cleaned before and after each case."},{"id":"l02-38selc","s":22,"q":"Which items that may touch the wound or exposed tissue must be sterilised before use?","o":["Instruments and gloves only, since all other items are supplied in clean packaging from the manufacturer","Instruments and sutures only, as dressings and covers are changed later on the ward after the operation","All supplies, instruments, needles, sutures, dressings, gloves, covers and solutions","Only items that touch blood, because items touching exposed tissue are cleaned thoroughly instead of sterilised"],"a":2,"e":"Everything that may contact the wound or exposed tissue must be sterile."},{"id":"l02-9alpwx","s":22,"q":"What must team members who will touch the patient do before gowning?","o":["Rinse hands with alcohol only, then dry them in the air before the sterile gown is put on","Wash with plain water for one minute and then wear two pairs of sterile gloves for protection","Wipe hands and forearms with a dry sterile towel, then pull on the gloves without any washing","Scrub hands and arms with antiseptic soap and water"],"a":3,"e":"Those touching the patient scrub hands and arms with antiseptic soap and water."},{"id":"l02-10xxmej","s":23,"q":"How are sterile gloves worn relative to the sterile gown?","o":["Under the cuffs, tucked inside the sleeves","Over the cuffs of the gown sleeves","Over the gown only after the drapes are placed","Beside the gown, with the cuffs left open"],"a":1,"e":"Sterile gloves are worn over the gown cuffs."},{"id":"l02-23chnu","s":23,"q":"Why are surgical masks worn tightly over the mouth and nose?","o":["To control infection from the team members' respiratory tract","To protect the team members from splashes of blood and body fluids that may reach the face","To filter the anaesthetic gases that would otherwise be breathed in by the team members in theatre","To reduce noise and unnecessary talk in the operating room, so that the team can concentrate"],"a":0,"e":"Masks control infection from the team's respiratory tract, without restricting breathing, seeing or talking."},{"id":"l02-1u1fdho","s":24,"q":"Why must team members keep their fingernails short, trim and natural?","o":["To avoid puncturing the sterile gloves while scrubbing","To make the sterile gown fit better at the cuff","To reduce the risk of harbouring pathogens","To allow quicker handling of small instruments"],"a":2,"e":"Short natural nails harbour fewer pathogens."},{"id":"l02-yhig8s","s":24,"q":"What is usually done about team members with upper or lower respiratory tract infections?","o":["They work only as circulating nurses in the OR","They wear two masks and continue as normal","Their direct access to the OR is restricted","They are allowed in once they have scrubbed twice"],"a":2,"e":"Personnel with respiratory infections are usually restricted from the OR."},{"id":"l02-1x307fi","s":25,"q":"Who may touch sterile supplies, equipment or the patient during surgery?","o":["Only properly scrubbed team members in sterile gowns and gloves","Any team member who is wearing a mask, a cap and a pair of clean unsterile gloves in theatre","The circulating nurse and the surgeon only, because they are the two senior members of the team","Anyone who has washed their hands thoroughly with antiseptic soap and water before entering theatre"],"a":0,"e":"Only scrubbed, sterile gowned and gloved members may touch sterile items."},{"id":"l02-thnnom","s":25,"q":"Which part of a draped table or patient is considered sterile?","o":["Only the top surface","The top and the sides to knee level","The entire cover including the edges","The part below the level of the table"],"a":0,"e":"Only the top surface of a draped table or patient is sterile."},{"id":"l02-1ce937v","s":26,"q":"Which part of a sterile gown is considered sterile?","o":["The whole gown including the back and shoulders, because it comes out of the autoclave sterile","The front from chest to the sterile field level, and sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow to the cuff","The front from the neck down to the knees only, because the lower part touches the floor","The sleeves from the shoulder to the stockinette cuff, as the front can touch unsterile items"],"a":1,"e":"The gown front from chest to table level and the sleeves from 2 inches above the elbow are sterile."},{"id":"l02-12wonei","s":26,"q":"After a sterile package is opened, how are its edges regarded?","o":["Unsterile","Sterile for the first minute only","Sterile if not touched by the hands","Sterile if the package is dropped quickly"],"a":0,"e":"The edges of an opened sterile package are considered unsterile."},{"id":"l02-d46odn","s":28,"q":"Which list correctly names the surgical team members?","o":["Surgeon, anaesthetist, theatre porter, pharmacist, scrub nurse and the nurse who receives the patient","Patient, nurses (scrub and circulating), surgeon, anaesthesiologist or nurse anaesthetist, surgical assistants","Patient, surgeon, laboratory technician, radiologist, ward nurse and the staff who clean the theatre","Surgeon, scrub nurse, anaesthetist, relatives of the patient and the hospital manager on duty that day"],"a":1,"e":"The team includes the patient, nurses, surgeon, anaesthesia provider and assistants."},{"id":"l02-hpvajj","s":29,"q":"Why is the patient described as the focus of the surgical team?","o":["He or she signs the consent and chooses the surgeon","He or she is the one undergoing the surgical procedure","He or she directs the choice of anaesthetic agent used","He or she is the only member whose opinion matters"],"a":1,"e":"The patient is the one undergoing the procedure."},{"id":"l02-1y1kwnf","s":29,"q":"Which fears do preoperative patients commonly have?","o":["Cost of care, transport home, visitors, ward food, bed space and who will look after the family","Pain, death, loss of control, the theatre environment and the unknown","Scarring, blood tests, hospital gowns, noise from the ward and going hungry before the operation","Infection, allergies, long queues, delays in starting and the risk of the list being cancelled"],"a":1,"e":"Anxiety relates to pain, death, loss of control, the theatre and the unknown."},{"id":"l02-z0hqkd","s":30,"q":"Why are anaesthetised patients at higher risk during surgery?","o":["Anaesthetic agents always damage the heart and liver, which makes the patient more prone to infection","They cannot be monitored properly as they are unconscious, so complications go unnoticed for long","Their blood clots too quickly while they are asleep, which blocks the vessels and slows healing","Loss of senses and ability to communicate predisposes them to injury and infection"],"a":3,"e":"Anaesthesia removes senses and communication, predisposing to risks like injury."},{"id":"l02-1ep06dn","s":30,"q":"Which patients carry a higher surgical risk and need critical monitoring?","o":["Adolescents and young adults","Infants and older patients","Pregnant women only","Patients under local anaesthesia"],"a":1,"e":"Infants and older patients have a higher risk."},{"id":"l02-1ga1gdg","s":31,"q":"Except in emergencies, mental incapacity or minors, who consents to the surgical procedure?","o":["The nurse admitting the patient","The anaesthetist before induction","The patient himself or herself","The patient's employer or head of household"],"a":2,"e":"The patient consents unless an exception applies."},{"id":"l02-107shwx","s":31,"q":"How should perioperative care be planned for each patient?","o":["Standardised for all patients to ensure fairness and avoid mistakes made by individual team members","Decided by the surgical team alone, with the patient informed only after the operation has been done","Based on hospital routine and the ward timetable, with cultural wishes handled by the patient's relatives","Individualised, culturally and spiritually acceptable, with the patient informed and involved"],"a":3,"e":"Every patient is an individual who should be involved in planning care."},{"id":"l02-17jz2iz","s":32,"q":"How can the nurse help patient and family cope with the surgical experience?","o":["Discourage family visits before surgery","Reinforce healthy coping strategies","Avoid discussing the operation to prevent worry","Give sedatives to all anxious patients"],"a":1,"e":"Healthy coping strategies should be reinforced by the nurse."},{"id":"l02-13wv4wm","s":33,"q":"For how long is the surgical nurse involved in a patient's care?","o":["From the decision for surgery until no review or care is needed after discharge","From admission to the ward until the patient leaves the theatre and arrives in the recovery room","Only during the operation itself, when acting as scrub or circulating nurse for the surgical team","From induction of anaesthesia until the patient has fully woken up in the recovery room afterwards"],"a":0,"e":"The nurse is present from the decision for surgery until care ends after discharge."},{"id":"l02-ledflt","s":34,"q":"What does psychological preparation of the surgical patient involve?","o":["Nursing actions that make sure the patient is functionally fit, such as checking vital signs","Interventions carried out on the patient's body, such as shaving and gowning for theatre","Teaching the patient how to move and breathe after the operation, such as splinting and coughing","Activities that prepare the patient mentally and emotionally, such as education and reassurance"],"a":3,"e":"Psychological preparation prepares the patient mentally and emotionally, for example through patient and family education and reassurance."},{"id":"l02-834cwb","s":35,"q":"Which matching of preparation type and example is correct?","o":["Psychological - shaving and skin cleaning; physiological - reassurance; physical - vital signs and weight","Psychological - reassurance; physiological - vital signs; physical - shaving and gowning","Psychological - vital signs; physiological - gowning and consent; physical - patient education and counselling","Psychological - gowning; physiological - shaving and skin cleaning; physical - reassurance and explanation"],"a":1,"e":"Education and reassurance are psychological; vital signs physiological; shaving and gowning physical."},{"id":"l02-yh9wj4","s":35,"q":"What are the two nursing roles during surgery?","o":["Recovery nurse and ward nurse","Assisting nurse and anaesthetic nurse","Scrub (sterile) nurse and circulating (non-sterile) nurse","Scrub nurse and supervising matron"],"a":2,"e":"The nurse acts as scrub or circulating nurse."},{"id":"l02-1lj8pq","s":36,"q":"What does the nurse do postoperatively?","o":["Monitors recovery while healing wounds and preventing infection, haemorrhage and pneumonia","Takes over wound closure from the surgeon and removes all the drains on the first day after surgery","Waits for the surgeon to order observations before taking any measurements on the patient at all","Cares for the patient only until he or she leaves the recovery room and returns to the ward"],"a":0,"e":"Post-operative nursing aims to heal wounds and prevent complications."},{"id":"l02-1o4wzaf","s":37,"q":"Who heads the surgical team and bears final responsibility for the outcome?","o":["The anaesthetist","The surgeon","The scrub nurse","The theatre manager"],"a":1,"e":"The surgeon heads the team and bears final responsibility."},{"id":"l02-1nup9hz","s":38,"q":"In Ghana, who besides the anaesthesiologist may administer anaesthesia?","o":["A scrub nurse who has been trained on the job by the anaesthetist, with no formal certification","A nurse anaesthetist, a registered nurse trained and certified for it","A surgical assistant working under supervision once the surgeon has made the first incision on the patient","A circulating nurse who has completed a short course in the theatre of the teaching hospital"],"a":1,"e":"The nurse anaesthetist is a certified registered nurse."},{"id":"l02-1gwcez","s":39,"q":"Why does the anaesthetist intubate patients under general anaesthesia?","o":["To deliver muscle relaxants faster and directly into the lungs so the patient stays completely still","General anaesthesia is associated with altered respiratory function","To stop the patient from waking up during the operation, as the tube keeps the airway sedated","To measure the blood gases continuously through the tube while the operation is going on in theatre"],"a":1,"e":"Because general anaesthesia alters respiration, patients are intubated."},{"id":"l02-fbn4cx","s":40,"q":"Which set of parameters does the anaesthetist assess during surgery?","o":["Urine output, haemoglobin, wound drainage and blood glucose, recorded on the chart every fifteen minutes","Serum electrolytes, liver enzymes, platelet count and creatinine, taken from samples sent to the laboratory","Pain score, nausea, bowel sounds and wound colour, which are checked after the patient is awake","ECG, oxygen saturation, tidal volume, blood gases and alveolar gas concentrations"],"a":3,"e":"These physiological parameters are monitored throughout surgery."},{"id":"l02-17u1omj","s":41,"q":"What does a surgical assistant do?","o":["Handles unsterile items and runs errands for the team from outside the sterile field in theatre","Scrubs in, stays sterile, suctions, holds clamps and anticipates the surgeon's needs","Gives the anaesthetic and watches the patient's breathing and pulse throughout the whole operation","Counts swabs and labels specimens outside the sterile field, then records them in the theatre register"],"a":1,"e":"The assistant scrubs in and helps the surgeon."},{"id":"l02-5els7b","s":41,"q":"Who may serve as a surgical assistant?","o":["Any theatre porter who has been taught how to scrub and who has watched several operations","An assistant surgeon or physician, or a trained peri-operative nurse","A ward nurse who has observed two operations and who has been approved by the head of department","The anaesthetist, when the patient is stable and the surgeon agrees to let him or her assist"],"a":1,"e":"Assistants may be physicians or trained peri-operative nurses."},{"id":"l02-10vcz9z","s":43,"q":"Which are the general classes of surgical instruments?","o":["Cutting, suturing, sterilising and measuring instruments, each stored in its own set on the trolley","Cutting and dissecting, grasping or holding, retractors, and tissue unifying","Scalpels, forceps, drains and monitors, grouped by the speciality in which they are most often used","Diagnostic, therapeutic, cosmetic and restorative instruments, grouped by the purpose of the operation"],"a":1,"e":"The four classes are cutting/dissecting, grasping/holding, retractors and tissue unifying."},{"id":"l02-b6y9fm","s":44,"q":"Why must team members know the structure and function of instruments?","o":["To keep the perioperative phases as fluid as possible","To allow them to repair damaged instruments in theatre","To decide which instruments the patient may need to buy","To choose the type of anaesthesia used for the case"],"a":0,"e":"Knowledge of instruments keeps surgery running smoothly."},{"id":"l02-1qrr8yk","s":45,"q":"What forms the 'knife' used for surgical incisions?","o":["A pair of straight scissors with a sharp tip","A diathermy pencil used with a foot switch","A surgical blade fixed on a handle (scalpel)","A Gigli saw drawn across the skin"],"a":2,"e":"A scalpel is a blade fixed to a handle."},{"id":"l02-iwa3sy","s":46,"q":"Why do most surgeons prefer curved scissors for dissecting?","o":["They give a better field of vision of the area to be cut","They cut thicker tissue with less force, since the curved blades grip the tissue as it is cut","They are lighter and easier to sterilise because there are no corners where debris can collect","They also work as retractors in deep wounds, since the curved blades hold the wound edges apart"],"a":0,"e":"Curved patterns give a better view of the area being cut."},{"id":"l02-17jah26","s":46,"q":"When are straight scissors used?","o":["When a straight cut is wanted in sutures, nerves or vessels","When dissecting around deep structures where the surgeon has only a limited view of the area","When cutting bone during orthopaedic surgery, because the straight blades give a firmer cutting edge","When removing dressings that have become stuck to the skin of the patient after the operation"],"a":0,"e":"Straight scissors are used for straight cuts."},{"id":"l02-z76qwd","s":47,"q":"Which instruments cut bone in orthopaedic surgery?","o":["Heavy straight scissors and scalpels","Mayo-Hegar needle holders","Deaver retractors","Saws, such as orthopaedic and Gigli saws"],"a":3,"e":"Saws are mainly used to cut bones."},{"id":"l02-1h32o9f","s":48,"q":"What does a diathermy machine do?","o":["Suctions blood and other fluid from the surgical field so that the surgeon can see clearly","Uses electricity to cut and coagulate tissue and vessels","Warms intravenous fluids and blood during long operations to avoid chilling the patient in theatre","Holds the incision open for better visibility so that the surgeon can work with both hands"],"a":1,"e":"Diathermy is electrical equipment for cutting and coagulating."},{"id":"l02-zz5nvu","s":49,"q":"Why are forceps and clamps used?","o":["To grasp, hold firmly or pull on tissue when fingers are too big","To cut tissue cleanly along the line of incision when a scalpel would cause too much bleeding","To keep the wound open by pressing steadily on its edges while the surgeon works inside it","To measure the depth of the incision made, so the surgeon knows how far to go in the tissue"],"a":0,"e":"Forceps grasp, hold or exert traction on tissue or material."},{"id":"l02-1uoygai","s":50,"q":"What is the use of hemostatic forceps such as Halstead mosquito forceps?","o":["To clamp and restrict arteries or tissue and control blood flow","To secure surgical drapes to the patient's skin so that they do not slip during the operation","To hold and guide the suture needle through the tissue while the wound is being closed in layers","To grasp and handle dressings and sponges without the need to touch them with the gloved hands"],"a":0,"e":"Hemostatic forceps control bleeding by clamping vessels."},{"id":"l02-96s79a","s":51,"q":"What is the use of towel forceps such as the Backhaus?","o":["To secure drapes to the skin and handle sponges","To clamp arteries and so control blood flow in the area where the surgeon is going to cut","To hold soft tissue gently in position while the surgeon dissects around the structure in question","To retract the edges of the wound so that the deeper layers can be seen clearly by the surgeon"],"a":0,"e":"Towel forceps secure drapes and handle sponges."},{"id":"l02-e3g7vf","s":52,"q":"Dressing (thumb) forceps look like tweezers with serrated tips. What are they used for?","o":["To clamp blood vessels in the wound","To grasp and handle dressings","To hold and guide the suture needle","To secure drapes to the skin"],"a":1,"e":"Adson dressing forceps grasp and handle dressings."},{"id":"l02-zjemzs","s":53,"q":"What are tissue forceps such as Adson-Brown used for?","o":["To clamp bleeding vessels","To secure the surgical drapes","To grasp and handle soft tissue","To pass sutures through bone"],"a":2,"e":"Tissue forceps hold soft tissue."},{"id":"l02-1a34u1t","s":54,"q":"What is the purpose of retractors?","o":["To clamp vessels and control bleeding in the field so that the surgeon can see what is being cut","To hold tissue firmly in position while it is being cut or sutured by the surgeon in theatre","To suture the layers of an incision closed once the main part of the operation has been completed","To open the surgical working area for clearer visibility"],"a":3,"e":"Retractors open the surgical area."},{"id":"l02-1kjeaw3","s":54,"q":"What distinguishes a self-retaining retractor such as the Goligher?","o":["It cuts and retracts in a single movement","It stays in position without needing to be held","It is used only in obstetric and gynaecological cases","It must be held by the scrub nurse throughout"],"a":1,"e":"Self-retaining retractors do not need to be held in position."},{"id":"l02-16g3v5e","s":55,"q":"Which pairing of speculum and use is correct?","o":["Cusco's - views the cervix via the vagina; Sim's - retracts the vaginal walls","Cusco's - retracts the vaginal walls; Sim's - views the cervix through the vagina directly","Cusco's - holds the uterus steady; Sim's - opens the cervix to allow instruments to pass through","Cusco's - cuts the cervix during biopsy; Sim's - closes the vagina at the end of the repair"],"a":0,"e":"Cusco's allows cervical viewing, while Sim's retracts the vaginal walls."},{"id":"l02-1ygi57g","s":56,"q":"What are tissue-unifying instruments and materials used for?","o":["To open the wound for a better view","To separate tissue layers during dissection","To close open wounds and incisions","To grasp and hold tissue steady"],"a":2,"e":"They include needle holders, sutures and staplers to close wounds."},{"id":"l02-10gzif6","s":57,"q":"What is the Mayo-Hegar needle holder used for?","o":["To hold and guide the suture needle, with milled jaws for a non-slip grip","To clamp blood vessels and control bleeding in a part of the field that the surgeon is working in","To secure drapes to the patient's skin so that the sterile field is kept in place during surgery","To retract the edges of the wound so that deeper structures can be seen by the surgical team"],"a":0,"e":"Needle holders have milled jaws to hold the needle without slipping."},{"id":"l02-10aogx2","s":58,"q":"Which description of the surgical needle is correct?","o":["It may have a cutting body or be rounded and smooth, come in various sizes and is usually pre-attached to thread","It is always cutting-edged, comes in one standard size and is threaded by the scrub nurse in theatre","It is made of catgut and is removed from the wound about one week after the operation is done","It is a hollow tube used to inject local anaesthetic into the tissue around the wound edges"],"a":0,"e":"Needles vary in body, size and are mostly packaged attached to thread."},{"id":"l02-1wjicaj","s":59,"q":"Which suture threads are removed post-operatively?","o":["Absorbable sutures","Non-absorbable sutures","Catgut sutures only","Synthetic sutures only"],"a":1,"e":"Non-absorbable sutures do not dissolve and are removed."},{"id":"l02-pf676e","s":59,"q":"What are modern sutures made of, compared with historical ones?","o":["Synthetic material, whereas historically catgut and silk were used","Catgut and silk, whereas synthetic materials such as nylon were used in the early historical period","Steel wire, whereas plastic and nylon were used historically for closing wounds in the skin of patients","Cotton, whereas nylon and catgut were used historically for closing wounds in the deeper tissue layers"],"a":0,"e":"Historically sutures were catgut and silk; modern ones are synthetic."},{"id":"l02-cxepql","s":60,"q":"Which feature describes the theatre table?","o":["It is fixed at one level to keep the patient stable, and the surgeon adjusts by standing on a stool","It folds flat to be stored between operations, so that the theatre can be cleaned thoroughly each time","It tilts only when the surgeon pushes it by hand, so the position is changed by the whole team","It can be electrically adjusted up or down to suit the surgical team"],"a":3,"e":"The table is electrically adjustable."},{"id":"l02-1ss0b33","s":61,"q":"Why are surgical lights designed to dilute shadows?","o":["To reduce the heat falling on the surgical wound, which would otherwise dry out the exposed tissue","To minimise obstruction of the light by team members' heads","To save electricity during long operations, since many weak lights are cheaper to run than one strong","To help the camera record the operation clearly for teaching, since shadows spoil the picture quality"],"a":1,"e":"Shadow dilution minimises light blockage by the team."},{"id":"l02-3rn8ff","s":62,"q":"What is the anaesthetic machine used for?","o":["To warm the patient and the theatre during the operation so that the patient does not become cold","To give anaesthetic agent, especially in general anaesthesia, and monitor vital signs and ECG","To suction secretions from the airway of the patient before and after the tube has been placed there","To record the surgical swab count automatically and alert the team when one swab is not found"],"a":1,"e":"It delivers anaesthetic agents and has monitors for vital signs and ECG."}]},{"id":"l03","title":"Preoperative Assessment","emoji":"🩺","recap":["Pre-operative care runs from the decision for surgery until the patient is moved onto the operating table; it is the window to find and correct risks.","Assess by health history, physical examination (inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation), lab results and scans.","Check every system: fluids, nutrition, mouth, lungs, heart, liver, kidneys, glucose, immunity and all medicines.","Assess fears, coping, beliefs and understanding, then teach: deep breathing, coughing, splinting and early movement."],"slides":42,"questions":[{"id":"l03-1n7jvri","s":7,"q":"When does pre-operative care begin and end?","o":["It begins when surgery is decided and ends when the patient is moved onto the operating table","It begins on admission to the ward and ends when the anaesthetist has reviewed the patient and the notes","It begins at the first outpatient visit and ends when the operation itself has been finished by the surgeon","It begins when the consent form is signed by the patient and ends at the door of the theatre suite"],"a":0,"e":"Pre-operative care runs from the surgical decision to transfer onto the operating table."},{"id":"l03-175jj10","s":8,"q":"Which group lists risk factors for surgical complications?","o":["Good hydration, normal weight, adequate nutrition and stable vital signs","Young adulthood, recent vaccination, normal haemoglobin and good fitness","Hypovolaemia, obesity, malnutrition, pregnancy and renal disease","Adequate sleep, regular meals, normal blood pressure and no allergies"],"a":2,"e":"Fluid imbalance, extremes of age or weight, infection, pregnancy, malnutrition and organ disease raise risk."},{"id":"l03-1k3wwxq","s":9,"q":"Why is a health history collected before the physical examination?","o":["It supplies information that validates the examination findings","It allows the nurse to skip the physical examination when the patient has already been seen by the surgeon","It lets the surgeon decide which anaesthetic to use before the patient is brought to the theatre unit","It is required only for emergency cases, where the patient cannot be examined properly in the time available"],"a":0,"e":"The history confirms and supports what the examination finds."},{"id":"l03-plj0c4","s":10,"q":"Which practice is correct during the physical examination?","o":["Expose the whole body at once so that all the body systems can be seen without repeated uncovering","Start with palpation and then inspect the area, because touching first shows where to look more closely","Expose only the body part being examined, with consent and privacy","Examine the patient first and explain the findings only afterwards, to save time on a busy ward round"],"a":2,"e":"Privacy and consent matter, so only the examined part is exposed."},{"id":"l03-lru8sd","s":11,"q":"Which statement about palpation is correct?","o":["Palpate tender areas first so that the source of the problem is found early and the patient is relieved","Wear gloves only when examining the abdomen, because other body parts carry no risk of contact with fluids","Use deep pressure from the very start so that deeper organs can be felt before the patient tenses up","Keep nails short and hands warm, and palpate tender areas last"],"a":3,"e":"Palpate tender areas last, with short nails and warm hands."},{"id":"l03-1cl8zpm","s":12,"q":"Tapping the fingers sharply on the body to find organ shape and whether it is solid, fluid-filled or gas-filled is called what?","o":["Percussion","Palpation","Auscultation","Inspection"],"a":0,"e":"Percussion locates organ size and position and shows whether it is solid or contains fluid or gas."},{"id":"l03-1dznqvh","s":13,"q":"What do laboratory investigations help the nurse to assess?","o":["The shape and structure of the internal organs, which are best seen on imaging rather than on the blood tests","The patient's level of understanding about surgery, which is gathered during the pre-operative teaching session","The cleanliness of the skin at the surgical site, which is judged by swabbing before the operation starts","The physiologic status, through quantities of fluids and electrolytes"],"a":3,"e":"Lab results measure fluids and electrolytes and so show functional status."},{"id":"l03-18xro9n","s":15,"q":"Why is the time of the patient's last meal assessed?","o":["Food in the stomach raises the blood sugar before surgery, which would make anaesthetic dosing less accurate","Nil per os prevents aspiration of stomach contents during surgery","Hunger makes patients more anxious in the theatre, so the last meal is timed to keep them settled","A full stomach slows the action of the antibiotics, so they do not reach the wound in time"],"a":1,"e":"Fasting prevents intra-operative aspiration of gastric contents."},{"id":"l03-1q4scoq","s":15,"q":"Why are Glasgow Coma Scale scores recorded for subconscious patients before surgery?","o":["They serve as baseline data for later comparison","They decide whether the patient needs a general anaesthetic","They replace the need for a neurological examination later","They show how long the patient has been without food"],"a":0,"e":"Pre-operative GCS is baseline data."},{"id":"l03-1x8h0cc","s":16,"q":"How is a patient's fluid and electrolyte status checked?","o":["Body temperature and the colour of the nail beds, together with the number of hours the patient has slept","Urine smell and the number of pillows the patient uses, together with the pulse rate taken at rest","Skin turgor plus haemoglobin, haematocrit, urea nitrogen and electrolyte results","Breath sounds and the patient's stated thirst, together with the weight taken on admission to the ward"],"a":2,"e":"Skin turgor and the listed blood results reveal fluid and electrolyte balance."},{"id":"l03-1swi5c5","s":17,"q":"What is the effect of protein deficiency such as kwashiorkor on surgical patients?","o":["It speeds up healing by increasing tissue turnover","It causes excessive bleeding in theatre only","It reduces pain after the operation","It impairs postoperative wound healing"],"a":3,"e":"Protein deficiency impairs wound healing."},{"id":"l03-1k2yux2","s":18,"q":"Why are dentures and partial plates assessed before surgery?","o":["They may dislodge during intubation and obstruct the airway","They interfere with the pulse oximeter probe, which is usually clipped to a finger or the lip of the patient","They may be damaged by the anaesthetic gases, so they must be soaked in a special solution overnight","They prevent the patient from speaking clearly to staff, which delays the pre-operative checklist in the holding area"],"a":0,"e":"Oral prostheses can dislodge and obstruct the airway."},{"id":"l03-142cixg","s":19,"q":"Why is the duration of a patient's cough noted?","o":["To decide the amount of fluid to be given before surgery, since long coughing causes loss of body water","To predict how much pain the patient will feel after the operation when the wound is stretched","To help rule out respiratory infections such as tuberculosis and pneumonia","To choose the correct size of the endotracheal tube that the anaesthetist will pass through the airway"],"a":2,"e":"Cough duration helps rule out respiratory tract infections."},{"id":"l03-1dhsj20","s":20,"q":"Which pair is assessed for cardiovascular status?","o":["Skin turgor and haemoglobin level, which show how well hydrated the patient is before the operation","Urine volume and urine colour, which show how well the kidneys are working before the operation","Pulse and blood pressure and their characteristics","Cough duration and breathing depth, which show how well the lungs are working before the operation"],"a":2,"e":"The pulse and blood pressure show cardiovascular function."},{"id":"l03-es3dmn","s":21,"q":"Why is liver function assessed before surgery?","o":["The liver produces the urine that is passed during surgery, so poor function would cause retention","The liver metabolises anaesthetic agents, analgesics and antibiotics","The liver controls the patient's body temperature in theatre, so poor function would cause chilling","The liver stores the oxygen needed during anaesthesia, so poor function would cause low saturation"],"a":1,"e":"Medication metabolism depends on the liver."},{"id":"l03-1v4a7q8","s":22,"q":"What do culture and sensitivity results show?","o":["The patient's blood sugar level before surgery, which is used to adjust the insulin that is given","The amount of blood lost during the passage of urine, which suggests a hidden bleeding point in the tract","The pathogens responsible for ongoing infections","The concentration of electrolytes in the plasma, which is used to plan the intravenous fluids before surgery"],"a":2,"e":"Culture identifies the pathogens causing infection."},{"id":"l03-1ypijac","s":23,"q":"What blood glucose range is recommended before, during and after surgery?","o":["40 to 60 mg/dL","150 to 200 mg/dL","80 to 110 mg/dL","210 to 260 mg/dL"],"a":2,"e":"Keeping glucose between 80 and 110 mg/dL leads to better outcomes."},{"id":"l03-15k37fj","s":23,"q":"Why does hyperglycaemia matter for the surgical patient?","o":["It prevents the anaesthetic from working","It may delay the wound healing process","It causes immediate collapse of the lungs","It stops the kidneys making urine"],"a":1,"e":"Hyperglycaemia may delay wound healing."},{"id":"l03-s60x5j","s":24,"q":"What are patients on corticosteroids assessed for?","o":["Pulmonary oedema","Adrenal insufficiency","Hyperkalaemia","Thyrotoxicosis"],"a":1,"e":"Long-term steroids can leave the adrenal glands unable to respond to surgical stress."},{"id":"l03-57nyyd","s":25,"q":"Which list contains common hypersensitivities assessed before surgery?","o":["Cold weather, loud noise, bright light and strong smells, which can all trigger a reaction in theatre","Fasting, hospital gowns, theatre caps and surgical masks, which are routinely used in the unit during surgery","Pollen, cotton clothing, soap and warm water only, which are all substances met in the ward every day","Latex, specific drugs, plaster, blood transfusion, contrast agents and shellfish"],"a":3,"e":"Latex, drugs, plaster, transfusion, contrast agents and foods like shellfish can cause reactions."},{"id":"l03-ejsu3m","s":26,"q":"Who appraises ongoing herbal and orthodox medicines for interaction with anaesthetic agents?","o":["The anaesthesiologist","The ward pharmacist only","The scrub nurse","The patient's relatives"],"a":0,"e":"All ongoing medicines are appraised by the anaesthesiologist."},{"id":"l03-w62p2f","s":26,"q":"Which test result is assessed in patients on heparin or aspirin?","o":["Erythrocyte sedimentation rate","International Normalised Ratio (INR)","Blood urea nitrogen","White cell differential count"],"a":1,"e":"INR shows bleeding tendency in patients on these drugs."},{"id":"l03-12eu9fb","s":27,"q":"How long before surgery is aspirin stopped?","o":["At least 1 hour","At least 7 days","At least 2 months","At least 12 hours"],"a":1,"e":"Aspirin is stopped at least 7 days before to reduce excessive bleeding."},{"id":"l03-1ldj3cr","s":27,"q":"When should herbal products be discontinued before surgery?","o":["On the morning of surgery","2 to 3 weeks before","Immediately after the operation","1 to 2 hours before"],"a":1,"e":"Herbal products should stop 2 to 3 weeks before surgery."},{"id":"l03-1irde7b","s":29,"q":"How might a fearful preoperative patient behave?","o":["Sleep soundly and ask no questions about the procedure, appearing calm and detached from the whole matter","Withdraw from contact, talk excessively or repeat questions already answered","Refuse all fluids and food after the operation, saying that he or she does not trust the ward staff","Request the earliest possible theatre slot and demand to be told the exact time that the operation ends"],"a":1,"e":"Withdrawal, excessive talking and repeated questions are signs of fear."},{"id":"l03-8a9xsv","s":30,"q":"What is assessed to rule out unhealthy ways of dealing with anxiety?","o":["The patient's blood pressure alone","The patient's coping strategies","The patient's weight and height","The patient's preferred anaesthetic"],"a":1,"e":"Coping strategies are assessed to identify unhealthy ones."},{"id":"l03-1s6vx55","s":31,"q":"Why are spiritual and cultural beliefs assessed before surgery?","o":["They decide which surgeon will perform the operation, since patients are matched to staff of the same faith","They determine the dose of anaesthetic given, because belief systems alter how the body handles the drugs","They are recorded only for legal reasons, so that the hospital is protected if a complaint is made later","They affect the surgical experience and can be used to the patient's advantage"],"a":3,"e":"Beliefs shape the experience and can enhance therapeutic effect when respected."},{"id":"l03-1yi77dk","s":32,"q":"Which restriction is assessed for patients with religious or ethnic beliefs?","o":["Objection to being weighed before the operation, since the weight is shown to others in the ward","Objection to the use of the theatre gown, because it exposes parts of the body to the surgical team","Objection to shaving and to animal products such as catgut sutures","Objection to seeing the surgeon before the operation, because the surgeon is of the opposite sex or faith"],"a":2,"e":"Shaving and animal-based sutures may conflict with beliefs."},{"id":"l03-riuyga","s":34,"q":"Which teaching strategies suit the surgical patient?","o":["Verbal interaction, written documents and return demonstration for skills","Written instructions only, given on the morning of surgery so that they are remembered during the recovery","Group lectures delivered once to all the patients together, so that everyone hears the same message at once","Demonstration by the nurse alone, without the patient being asked to practise the skill under supervision"],"a":0,"e":"Teaching strategies include verbal, written and return demonstration."},{"id":"l03-lvx6a4","s":35,"q":"When does pre-operative teaching begin?","o":["On the evening before the operation, after consent is signed","When the patient is admitted onto the surgical ward","When the patient meets the surgical team, typically when the surgeon discusses the decision for surgery","After premedication has been given on the day"],"a":2,"e":"Teaching starts as soon as the patient meets the team."},{"id":"l03-b0klga","s":35,"q":"How should information be given to a surgical patient?","o":["Systematically over a period so as not to overload the patient","All at once on the day of surgery to keep it fresh in the patient's mind as the operation approaches","In writing only, in a leaflet that the patient takes home, to avoid any misunderstanding of the spoken word","Only when the patient asks a direct question, so that nothing frightening is said without being requested"],"a":0,"e":"Teach over time to avoid overload."},{"id":"l03-ek10u7","s":36,"q":"What should pre-operative explanations emphasise?","o":["The technical steps of the operation in medical terms, so that the patient is fully prepared for what happens","The sensations the patient will feel, not just a description of the procedure","The cost of the operation and the expected length of stay in hospital, which patients usually worry about most","The names of the instruments used in the operation and the order in which the surgeon is going to use them"],"a":1,"e":"Explanations should stress the sensations patients will experience."},{"id":"l03-vwe600","s":37,"q":"Which exercises help prevent post-operative pneumonia by expanding the lungs?","o":["Passive limb exercises and leg elevation, which keep the blood moving in the veins of the lower limbs","Wound splinting and abdominal tightening, which protect the incision when the patient coughs or turns in bed","Deep breathing exercises and use of an incentive spirometer","Range-of-motion exercises and early sitting up, which maintain the movement of the joints after the operation"],"a":2,"e":"Deep breathing and incentive spirometry facilitate lung expansion."},{"id":"l03-rq2ubx","s":38,"q":"How is deep breathing taught?","o":["Lie flat, hold the breath for a full minute, then cough lightly from the throat to bring up the secretions","Breathe quickly and shallowly through the mouth, then cough gently to clear the throat and the upper airway","Hold the incision firmly and cough hard without taking a deep breath first, to clear the secretions quickly","Sit up, breathe in deeply and slowly, breathe out slowly, then cough from deep in the lungs"],"a":3,"e":"The patient sits upright, breathes deeply and then coughs from deep in the lungs."},{"id":"l03-17q42ls","s":39,"q":"Why are patients taught to splint thoracic or abdominal incisions when coughing?","o":["To reduce the amount of coughing needed","To make breathing shallower and less painful","To prevent dehiscence and evisceration","To keep the dressing dry after the operation"],"a":2,"e":"Splinting protects the wound from opening or organs from protruding."},{"id":"l03-1xj04ah","s":39,"q":"How is wound splinting done?","o":["One hand pressed on the incision while the other holds the bed rail, with the knees drawn up toward the chest","Arms folded over the chest with the elbows held tight against the body, and the shoulders rolled forward","A pillow placed under the knees during the cough, and the head of the bed lowered to a flat position","Palms together with fingers interlaced, hands placed across the incision"],"a":3,"e":"Interlaced hands across the incision act as a splint."},{"id":"l03-18xju7t","s":40,"q":"Why are passive exercises and early ambulation emphasised?","o":["They reduce the need for analgesics completely","They help the wound close faster by pulling edges together","They prevent the patient from vomiting after surgery","They promote circulation and prevent venous stasis"],"a":3,"e":"Movement facilitates circulation and prevents venous stasis."}]},{"id":"l04","title":"Preoperative Nursing Care","emoji":"📝","recap":["Pre-operative care has two phases: initial care (when surgery is decided, often outpatient) and immediate care on admission.","Psychological care: reassure, correct misconceptions, orient the patient, respect faith, and make sure valid consent is obtained.","Physiological care: investigations, nil per os for 6 to 8 hours, IV fluids, vital signs, catheter or NG tube and premedication as needed.","Physical care: remove jewellery, dentures and nail polish, gown the patient, assemble records, empty the bladder, hand over to the theatre team."],"slides":31,"questions":[{"id":"l04-jgvn43","s":8,"q":"How is pre-operative care divided into phases?","o":["Admission care in the ward, and recovery care in the theatre","Initial care when surgery is decided, and immediate care for scheduled surgery on admission","Diagnostic care before the clinic visit, and discharge care with follow-up after the operation is completed at home","Day care for minor cases, and inpatient care for major cases"],"a":1,"e":"Initial care may be outpatient; immediate pre-operative care happens on admission."},{"id":"l04-oyyp3z","s":9,"q":"What are the main goals of pre-operative care?","o":["Prepare the theatre, sterilise all the instruments and check that the anaesthetic machine is working for surgery","Assess and correct risks and teach the patient and family how to avoid complications and cope","Shorten the length of stay by discharging the patient early","Reduce the surgeon's workload by handling consent and diagnosis"],"a":1,"e":"The goals are to assess and correct risks and to teach measures that prevent complications."},{"id":"l04-1dq2p0m","s":10,"q":"Which members make up the multidisciplinary pre-operative team?","o":["Surgeon, anaesthetist, radiologist, laboratory technologist and nurse","Surgeon, pharmacist, porter, physiotherapist and matron","Anaesthetist, dietician, chaplain, social worker and nurse","Surgeon, radiographer, ward clerk, theatre cleaner, hospital driver and nurse"],"a":0,"e":"The team includes surgeon, anaesthetist, radiologist, laboratory technologist and nurse."},{"id":"l04-5qke8","s":10,"q":"Which history is taken from the patient and relatives to guide the pre-operative team?","o":["Only the family's occupation and the patient's religion","Only previous admissions to other wards in the same hospital","Past surgery, allergies to drugs, tape, latex and iodine, and existing infections","Dietary likes and dislikes, the patient's usual sleep pattern and favourite hobbies at home"],"a":2,"e":"This history directs the team's activities."},{"id":"l04-fmknlc","s":12,"q":"What is the aim of psychological pre-operative care?","o":["To prepare the patient's skin and bowel for the operation and to reduce the risk of wound infection","To confirm that all laboratory results are normal","To help patient and family understand and accept the surgery and relieve fear and stress","To arrange payment and the length of stay with the family"],"a":2,"e":"Psychological care relieves fear and promotes acceptance."},{"id":"l04-omlo5b","s":13,"q":"What should the nurse reassure patients and families about?","o":["Surgery carries no risk, and complications never happen","Surgery is safe with modern technology, pain will be controlled and competent staff will care for the patient","The surgeon has performed the same operation hundreds of times","The operation will be short, the patient will go home the same day and no pain will be felt afterwards at all"],"a":1,"e":"The nurse reassures about safety, pain control and competent personnel."},{"id":"l04-1amxqx0","s":14,"q":"How should the nurse handle a question that is beyond her competence?","o":["Give a general answer so the patient is not worried, then continue with the pre-operative preparations","Tell the patient to ask after the operation","Refer to the appropriate person, such as the nurse in charge or the surgeon","Ask the patient's relatives to explain it"],"a":2,"e":"Questions beyond competence are referred to the ward in-charge or surgeon."},{"id":"l04-13twf1n","s":14,"q":"Why may the nurse introduce the patient to another who had a similar surgery successfully?","o":["It helps the nurse to save time on explanations","Their interaction helps relieve anxiety","It makes the patient eager to sign the consent form","It lets the patient compare the cost of the operation"],"a":1,"e":"Interaction with a successful patient helps relieve anxiety."},{"id":"l04-r7e5fi","s":15,"q":"Why are patients shown gadgets like monitors and oxygen devices before surgery?","o":["So they are not scared when the anaesthesia wears off","So they can learn to operate the devices themselves after they wake up","So that the devices can be tested before use","So that relatives can check that they are working"],"a":0,"e":"Familiarity reduces fear when the patient wakes after surgery."},{"id":"l04-1knpywy","s":15,"q":"Why are patients and families told about possible complications of the specific procedure?","o":["So that they cooperate in measures that prevent complications","So that they can claim compensation if any complication arises afterwards","So that they will choose another procedure","So that they will not ask further questions"],"a":0,"e":"Awareness helps them cooperate in preventing complications."},{"id":"l04-bfuuop","s":16,"q":"Why must patients not eat or drink for about 6 to 8 hours before surgery?","o":["To keep the blood sugar high enough for healing","To make the anaesthetic work more slowly","To reduce the amount of urine passed in theatre","To prevent aspiration during surgery"],"a":3,"e":"Fasting prevents aspiration of stomach contents."},{"id":"l04-vey49e","s":16,"q":"Why should the patient and family know the expected cost and length of admission?","o":["Some patients have no insurance and some operations, like cosmetic surgery, are not covered","The hospital must collect full payment from the family before any operation can be scheduled on the theatre list","Insurance companies decide which surgeon will operate","Costs determine the type of anaesthesia the patient receives"],"a":0,"e":"In Ghana some surgeries are not covered by health insurance."},{"id":"l04-pcp7wd","s":17,"q":"Who signs the consent form for a mentally impaired, heavily sedated or critically ill patient?","o":["The nurse in charge of the ward on behalf of the patient and relatives","The anaesthetist before induction","No consent is needed in these cases","A family member; for a child, the parent or guardian"],"a":3,"e":"Such patients cannot give informed consent legally, so a relative or guardian signs."},{"id":"l04-rmz4z8","s":17,"q":"Whose legal responsibility is it to explain the surgery to the patient before consent?","o":["The scrub nurse","The ward nurse","The surgeon","The anaesthetist"],"a":2,"e":"The surgeon explains and witnesses the signed consent."},{"id":"l04-1fxlj1l","s":18,"q":"What may the surgeon do in an emergency when the patient cannot consent and no relative is available?","o":["Delay surgery until a relative has been found","Ask the nurse to sign the consent form instead","Ask a hospital porter to act as a witness and sign the form on behalf of the patient","Operate without the signed consent if the condition does not permit waiting"],"a":3,"e":"In emergencies like RTA without family, surgery may proceed."},{"id":"l04-1t17ldb","s":19,"q":"Which phrases are used when writing psychological care for emergency surgery?","o":["Fully, in detail, after consent, at all times and with the family present","Briefly, if condition permits, if available, if possible","Slowly, after visiting hours, only if requested","Always, before admission, when the family arrives"],"a":1,"e":"These words reflect the limited time and context in emergencies."},{"id":"l04-fdd6fj","s":21,"q":"Which activities are part of physiological pre-operative preparation?","o":["Removing jewellery, dentures and nail polish and gowning the patient for theatre","Haemoglobin, grouping and cross-matching and chest x-ray investigations","Telling the family about the date of surgery","Dressing the patient in a theatre gown"],"a":1,"e":"Investigations such as Hb, grouping and cross-matching and chest x-ray belong here."},{"id":"l04-17jd5t3","s":22,"q":"How is urgency communicated for investigations in an emergency?","o":["The nurse takes the specimen to the laboratory herself","The word 'urgent' is written on the request form","The surgeon phones the laboratory in all cases","The patient is sent without any investigation"],"a":1,"e":"Writing 'urgent' on the form expedites the process."},{"id":"l04-s3fl6e","s":22,"q":"What is done in an emergency when the patient has recently eaten?","o":["Gastric lavage, then the patient goes to theatre without waiting 6 to 8 hours","The operation is postponed for 6 to 8 hours","An antiemetic is given and the patient is taken to theatre at once without any delay","The patient is made to vomit by drinking salt water"],"a":0,"e":"Gastric lavage empties the stomach so surgery need not wait."},{"id":"l04-k3xmn5","s":23,"q":"Why are fluid intake and output recorded accurately before surgery?","o":["To decide the patient's position on the operating list for the day","To calculate the dose of the antibiotics","To measure the amount of blood lost","To track hydration while IV fluids are being given"],"a":3,"e":"Accurate records help detect dehydration or over-hydration."},{"id":"l04-1qmee5x","s":23,"q":"Why might a patient's blood pressure be raised pre-operatively?","o":["Fasting","Premedication","Dehydration","Anxiety"],"a":3,"e":"Anxious patients may have a raised blood pressure, so vital signs must be checked carefully."},{"id":"l04-fkaumx","s":24,"q":"Why may a catheter or nasogastric tube be passed before abdominal or pelvic surgery?","o":["To give the premedication directly","To measure the patient's weight accurately","To collect a sample for culture","To decompress the bladder or stomach"],"a":3,"e":"Catheter and NG tube decompress the bladder and stomach, and must be inserted aseptically."},{"id":"l04-89vmih","s":24,"q":"Why is premedication given IV or IM in emergencies instead of orally?","o":["To make the drug work more slowly","To save the patient from swallowing tablets","To reduce the cost of treatment","To avoid intra-operative aspiration"],"a":3,"e":"Oral drugs add to stomach contents and risk aspiration."},{"id":"l04-8rbtf1","s":25,"q":"How is an anaemic patient prepared for planned surgery?","o":["With immediate blood transfusion for all anaemic patients before the operation date","With intravenous antibiotics for seven days","With bed rest and no oral intake for three days","With nutritious food or haematinics to raise the haemoglobin level"],"a":3,"e":"Diet or haematinics boost Hb in planned surgery; emergencies may be transfused."},{"id":"l04-1fuoyij","s":25,"q":"Why is the patient's weight checked before surgery?","o":["It decides the length of the incision the surgeon will make","It informs the dosage of drugs prescribed","It determines the theatre time slot","It shows how much fluid is in the lungs"],"a":1,"e":"Drug doses depend on weight."},{"id":"l04-1ffu83u","s":27,"q":"Which items are removed before the patient goes to theatre?","o":["Beads, dentures, nail polish and chains","Hearing aids and spectacles in all cases","Identification band and consent form","Surgical gown and cap"],"a":0,"e":"Items contra-indicated for surgery are removed."},{"id":"l04-kmpv6i","s":27,"q":"What is done if a patient does not want a wedding ring removed?","o":["The ring is plastered to prevent electrocution","The ring is left uncovered but recorded in the notes","The ring is tied to the finger with a thread","The ring is moved to the other hand"],"a":0,"e":"Plaster covers the ring to prevent electrocution (diathermy burns)."},{"id":"l04-j7xpxd","s":28,"q":"Why is nail polish removed before surgery?","o":["To prevent the polish from reacting with the antiseptic solution during skin preparation","To keep the hands clean for the surgeon","To allow the patient's nails to be trimmed","To help identify inadequate oxygen in the blood during surgery"],"a":3,"e":"Nail colour reveals low oxygen; polish masks it."},{"id":"l04-e4aljf","s":28,"q":"Why is the patient gowned and the female patient's hair covered?","o":["To keep the patient warm in the theatre","To prevent infection in the theatre","To identify the patient by the colour of the gown","To make the patient look neat for relatives"],"a":1,"e":"Gowning and covering hair prevent infection in theatre."},{"id":"l04-164zn74","s":29,"q":"What should patients who are not catheterised do before being sent to theatre?","o":["Drink a glass of water","Take a bath","Empty their bladder","Walk around the ward"],"a":2,"e":"Not catheterised patients empty the bladder first."},{"id":"l04-1k8uxgq","s":29,"q":"Why should the nurse not cross the area designated for patient transfer at the theatre?","o":["To enhance infection prevention","To keep the corridor clear for relatives","To save time for the theatre porters","To follow the dress code of the theatre"],"a":0,"e":"The transfer area protects the theatre from contamination."}]},{"id":"l05","title":"Intraoperative Care and Anaesthesia","emoji":"😷","recap":["Types of anaesthesia: general, regional (epidural, spinal), moderate sedation and local; each has its own risks and nursing watch-points.","The nurse is either scrub (sterile) or circulating (non-sterile): sets up, counts swabs, documents, labels specimens and keeps the patient safe.","Intra-operative observations: vitals at least every 5 minutes, hydration, consciousness, oxygen saturation, bleeding.","Precautions: infection prevention, maintained equipment, back-up power and standby equipment, emergency intubation and CPR readiness."],"slides":37,"questions":[{"id":"l05-8trdd1","s":8,"q":"Which are the main types of anaesthesia?","o":["General, spinal, topical, hypnotic and inhalation only","Local, epidural, inhalation and oral","Regional, nasal, rectal and intravenous","General, regional, moderate sedation and local"],"a":3,"e":"The four main types are general, regional, moderate sedation and local."},{"id":"l05-1osoqdj","s":9,"q":"Which statement about who gives anaesthesia is correct?","o":["The surgeon may give every type of anaesthesia, while the anaesthetist or nurse anaesthetist only watches the monitors","The surgeon may give local anaesthesia, but regional and general need the anaesthesiologist or nurse anaesthetist","The scrub nurse gives general anaesthesia on the surgeon's order","The circulating nurse gives regional anaesthesia in minor cases"],"a":1,"e":"Major surgeries need regional or general anaesthesia given by the anaesthesia provider."},{"id":"l05-o332l3","s":9,"q":"How is general anaesthesia described?","o":["A state where the patient is awake but feels no pain in one region of the body only","Complete narcosis, the patient is not arousable and cannot feel painful stimuli","A light sedation in which the patient can answer questions","Numbness at the incision site while the patient remains conscious"],"a":1,"e":"General anaesthesia gives complete narcosis, and patients need help to maintain an airway."},{"id":"l05-8z88co","s":10,"q":"At the start of general anaesthesia the patient is conscious but cannot move. Why must noise be kept low?","o":["The patient can hear instructions to move","Noise lowers the effect of the anaesthetic","Low sounds may seem loud to the patient","The anaesthetic machine alarms may fail"],"a":2,"e":"Sounds seem exaggerated at this stage."},{"id":"l05-1gvoc5t","s":10,"q":"What must the nurse do during the excitement stage of anaesthesia?","o":["Give extra anaesthetic agent at once","Wake the patient to calm him down","Leave the patient alone until it passes","Keep the patient safe from injury"],"a":3,"e":"The patient may laugh, sing or struggle, so protect from injury."},{"id":"l05-1ts5775","s":11,"q":"Which picture describes the surgical stage of anaesthesia?","o":["Awake but unable to move, with unusual sensations, ringing sounds and a very slow pulse","Struggling, laughing or singing with an irregular breathing pattern","Slow shallow breathing and pulse with developing cyanosis","Unconscious, calm and stable, feeling no pain, with normal respiration and pulse"],"a":3,"e":"In surgical anaesthesia the patient is calm and stable for surgery."},{"id":"l05-4isvuh","s":11,"q":"What happens with an excessive dose of anaesthetic agent?","o":["Return of consciousness and sudden movement during the operation, with a rapid pulse and loud breathing afterwards","Rapid pulse and fast breathing with flushing of the skin","Excitement with laughing, singing and struggling","Medullary depression, with slowing, shallow breathing and pulse until cyanosis and death if not treated"],"a":3,"e":"Overdose causes medullary depression."},{"id":"l05-tnaor9","s":12,"q":"Which are examples of inhalation anaesthetic agents?","o":["Lidocaine, bupivacaine, procaine and ropivacaine applied around the nerves","Diazepam, ketamine, propofol and midazolam","Morphine, pethidine and tramadol","Halothane, enflurane, sevoflurane and nitrous oxide with oxygen"],"a":3,"e":"These are the volatile liquids and gases named in the lecture."},{"id":"l05-2q8mni","s":12,"q":"By which routes are general anaesthetic agents given?","o":["Inhalation or intravascular routes","Oral or rectal routes using premedication drugs","Epidural or spinal routes","Topical or subcutaneous routes"],"a":0,"e":"General agents are inhaled or given intravascularly; they cross the blood-brain barrier."},{"id":"l05-22ivgd","s":13,"q":"What does regional anaesthesia involve?","o":["Spraying anaesthetic onto the skin or mucous membrane of the incision site to numb only the surface tissues of the area","Giving anaesthetic by mask to make the patient unconscious","Injecting a sedative so the patient forgets the operation","Injecting anaesthetic around major nerves to block sensation, motor and autonomic function in a region"],"a":3,"e":"Regional anaesthesia blocks the functions of the area supplied by the nerves."},{"id":"l05-hmo3zx","s":13,"q":"Why must the team guarantee a therapeutic environment during regional anaesthesia?","o":["The patient is fully unconscious and unable to respond to the team or hear them","The patient is paralysed from the neck down","The patient receives no sedation at all","The patient stays awake and is aware of the surroundings"],"a":3,"e":"Unless sedated, patients are awake and can hear noise, conversation and smell odours."},{"id":"l05-2lcanf","s":14,"q":"How do epidural and spinal anaesthesia differ?","o":["Epidural goes into the subarachnoid space below the dura; spinal stays outside the dura around the nerve roots","Epidural agent goes into the space around the dura; spinal goes through the dura into the subarachnoid space","Epidural is injected in the thoracic region only; spinal in the neck","Epidural is given by mask; spinal is given by injection into a vein"],"a":1,"e":"Spinal anaesthesia pierces the dura mater; epidural does not."},{"id":"l05-1jirg7y","s":15,"q":"Compared with epidural, which is true of spinal anaesthesia?","o":["It needs smaller doses but may cause post-operative headache","It needs larger doses and never causes headache or any post-operative complication","It needs no needle but causes paralysis of the limbs","It is used only for chest surgery under general anaesthesia"],"a":0,"e":"Direct contact with the cord allows smaller doses, but headache is common."},{"id":"l05-10sap7w","s":15,"q":"What risk occurs if spinal anaesthetic reaches the thoracic and cervical cord?","o":["Fever with severe shivering","Hypothermia","Respiratory paralysis","Itching"],"a":2,"e":"Spread upwards can paralyse the respiratory muscles."},{"id":"l05-l8n7sn","s":16,"q":"What must the nurse do for patients under moderate sedation?","o":["Leave them to rest and check the vital signs every hour until the anaesthetist returns to the ward","Never leave them alone and monitor for hypoxia, irregular heartbeat and altered vital signs","Wait for the anaesthetist to ask for observations","Monitor only the pulse until they wake"],"a":1,"e":"Sedated patients are monitored constantly."},{"id":"l05-1x8sicq","s":16,"q":"For which procedures is moderate sedation typically used?","o":["Diagnostic and therapeutic procedures such as endoscopy","Major abdominal surgery such as laparotomy or caesarean section in a theatre","Thyroidectomy and other neck surgery","Open heart surgery"],"a":0,"e":"It reduces anxiety and pain in procedures like endoscopy."},{"id":"l05-gvnsxo","s":17,"q":"Why do some local anaesthetic agents contain epinephrine?","o":["It makes the patient sleepy and relaxed during the procedure and lowers anxiety levels","It reduces the pain of the injection itself","It constricts local vessels and delays absorption into the circulation","It prevents the agent from damaging nerves"],"a":2,"e":"Epinephrine delays systemic absorption, prolonging the local effect."},{"id":"l05-1fo8w0r","s":17,"q":"Which feature makes local anaesthesia suitable for minor surgery?","o":["It always lasts more than one day, so the patient needs close ventilator support afterwards","It is simple, can be given by the surgeon, and recovery is rapid with minimal side effects","It makes the patient unconscious without an airway","It needs an anaesthetist and a ventilator"],"a":1,"e":"Local anaesthesia is simple, quick to recover from and has minimal side effects."},{"id":"l05-1jh7r2e","s":18,"q":"Which complications must the nurse continually monitor for in an anaesthetised patient, even with the anaesthetist present?","o":["Hypoxia, anaphylaxis, hypothermia, nausea and vomiting","Constipation, headache, rash and cough only, which are the commonest problems","Hypertension, jaundice, oedema and obesity","Pain, insomnia, anxiety and dehydration only"],"a":0,"e":"These are the major intra-operative complications."},{"id":"l05-1s1br2p","s":18,"q":"How are nausea and vomiting managed in an anaesthetised patient?","o":["Give extra fluids and lie the patient flat on the back to rest the stomach muscles","Stop anaesthetic agent and rouse the patient","Keep the patient head-down and give oxygen only","Give antiemetics and position the patient on the side to prevent aspiration"],"a":3,"e":"Antiemetics and side-lying positioning reduce aspiration risk."},{"id":"l05-36s5jz","s":19,"q":"Which signs may indicate anaphylaxis during surgery?","o":["Dry cough, cold hands, slow pulse and sweating","Hives, urticarial rash and itching skin","Low urine output and thirst","Blue lips and snoring"],"a":1,"e":"Skin signs like hives, rash and itching should be watched for."},{"id":"l05-x17soj","s":19,"q":"How can hypothermia be resolved during surgery?","o":["By removing the surgical drapes to let the patient warm up and giving extra oxygen","By warming IV fluids to 37 degrees Celsius and adjusting the theatre temperature","By giving cold fluids to stabilise the body","By increasing the dose of the anaesthetic"],"a":1,"e":"Warming IV fluids at 37 degrees Celsius is one measure."},{"id":"l05-12cfdan","s":21,"q":"Which pairing of nursing roles is correct?","o":["Scrub nurse - non-sterile, runs errands and handles unsterile items; circulating nurse - sterile, assists the surgeon at the table","Scrub nurse - sterile, assists the surgeon; circulating nurse - non-sterile, handles unsterile items and runs errands","Scrub nurse - monitors anaesthesia; circulating nurse - suctions the wound","Scrub nurse - labels specimens only; circulating nurse - gives drugs"],"a":1,"e":"The scrub nurse scrubs up, the circulating nurse handles unsterile items."},{"id":"l05-5rvrk5","s":22,"q":"What does the circulating nurse do to help the sterile team?","o":["Passes instruments directly to the surgeon on the sterile field and counts the swabs","Operates the diathermy using sterile gloves","Gives the anaesthetic to the patient","Assists the scrub nurse and surgeon to scrub and put on gowns and gloves"],"a":3,"e":"The circulating nurse assists with scrubbing, gowning and gloving."},{"id":"l05-txre2h","s":22,"q":"What does the nurse do when the patient is moved onto the theatre table?","o":["Starts the anaesthetic and monitors the airway while the surgeon is scrubbing up","Counts the instruments and swabs with the surgeon","Takes the patient's consent for surgery","Confirms it is the right patient and talks with him to allay anxiety"],"a":3,"e":"The nurse confirms identity and reduces anxiety."},{"id":"l05-ndj30m","s":23,"q":"Why should the patient not be left alone on the theatre table?","o":["The theatre environment is strange and may frighten the patient","The patient might touch the sterile field","The patient could move the monitors","Theatre rules say only the surgeon may be alone with the patient"],"a":0,"e":"The patient may be scared in the strange environment."},{"id":"l05-12wc9jo","s":23,"q":"How does the nurse promote the patient's safety and comfort during surgery?","o":["By removing the patient's gown to reach the site and leaving the body uncovered","By strapping the patient tightly to the table","By helping to position the patient and applying safety measures","By giving analgesics before incision"],"a":2,"e":"Positioning and safety measures protect the patient."},{"id":"l05-8j5wxp","s":24,"q":"What is the limit of a theatre nurse's role when assisting a surgeon in a district hospital?","o":["She may cut and suture minor wounds when the surgeon is tired or busy elsewhere","She may give anaesthesia while assisting the surgeon","She may close the abdomen if the surgeon is called away","She may hold forceps and suction, but does not perform cutting or suturing"],"a":3,"e":"The nurse does not perform the cutting or suturing in this role."},{"id":"l05-12pd1dk","s":24,"q":"Why does the circulating nurse count instruments and swabs before the incision is closed?","o":["To record the cost of the items used in the operation for the hospital accounts office","To decide which items need re-sterilising","To prevent a swab being left in the abdomen and causing complications","To check that the surgeon has used enough swabs"],"a":2,"e":"A retained swab causes post-operative complications."},{"id":"l05-1v0284q","s":25,"q":"What does the nurse document during surgery?","o":["The surgical procedure and any events that occurred","The anaesthetic agent used and the cost of surgery","The patient's diet and the visiting hours","The surgeon's qualifications and the theatre schedule"],"a":0,"e":"The nurse documents specific procedures and events."},{"id":"l05-1imiqtp","s":26,"q":"Which tasks does the nurse perform after the procedure?","o":["Remove the sutures, send the patient home and write the discharge summary for the outpatient clinic records","Give the patient's relatives the operation report","Reorder anaesthetic supplies for the hospital","Label specimens for histology, decontaminate and sterilise instruments, and prepare the theatre"],"a":3,"e":"Specimens are labelled and instruments processed before the next case."},{"id":"l05-170o1jd","s":28,"q":"Why must the nurse know the multidisciplinary team's observations?","o":["To take over the observations from the anaesthetist when the anaesthetist leaves theatre","To replace the monitors when they fail","To teach the patient after surgery","To alert the team to potential problems and help avoid mortalities"],"a":3,"e":"Nurses must recognise problems early to avoid mortality."},{"id":"l05-1v9lvd7","s":29,"q":"How often should heart rate and blood pressure be monitored during surgery?","o":["Once every hour","At least every 5 minutes","At the start and end of surgery only","Only when the patient wakes"],"a":1,"e":"The anaesthetist monitors them at least every 5 minutes."},{"id":"l05-1ayjotz","s":29,"q":"How is the patient's hydration managed during surgery?","o":["The patient is given water by mouth at regular intervals to keep the mouth moist","IV fluid rate is adjusted and blood is transfused when required","Fluids are withheld to prevent bleeding","Only the surgeon decides to give fluids after the operation"],"a":1,"e":"Corrective measures include increasing the IV rate or transfusion."},{"id":"l05-v2lbg7","s":30,"q":"Why does the anaesthetist control the patient's level of consciousness until surgery is over?","o":["So the patient can answer questions during surgery and tell the surgeon about any pain","If the patient regains consciousness early there would be intra-operative pain","So the patient can move to help the surgeon","To make wound closing faster"],"a":1,"e":"Under general anaesthesia an awake patient would feel pain."},{"id":"l05-1e0n0fa","s":30,"q":"Which method is recommended to monitor oxygen saturation?","o":["Pulse oximetry","Arterial blood sampling every minute","Checking the colour of the nails alone","Counting the respiratory rate only"],"a":0,"e":"Pulse oximetry gives the data needed to give the right oxygen amount."},{"id":"l05-16kh26r","s":31,"q":"Who observes for bleeding and ensures bleeding points are controlled during surgery?","o":["The scrub nurse","The surgeon","The circulating nurse","The anaesthetist"],"a":1,"e":"The surgeon controls bleeding."},{"id":"l05-n6phgu","s":31,"q":"What does the nurse do if a patient becomes restless as anaesthetic is given?","o":["Assists the anaesthetist to ensure the patient's safety","Leaves the patient to the surgeon and waits at the side of the table","Increases the dose of the anaesthetic","Restrains the patient firmly with straps"],"a":0,"e":"The nurse supports the anaesthetist in keeping the patient safe."},{"id":"l05-8lx74r","s":33,"q":"Which infection prevention precautions are used in the theatre?","o":["Outdoor clothing allowed in the corridor and in the sterile areas, with fumigation done only once a year","No outdoor clothing, hair covered and masks worn, proper sterile fields, and regular fumigation","Sterile fields only for major cases, with masks optional","Hair left uncovered, but staff with infections excluded permanently"],"a":1,"e":"These precautions help prevent surgical infection."},{"id":"l05-ormihf","s":33,"q":"Why does failure to prevent surgical infection raise hospital costs?","o":["It lowers the number of staff needed in theatre and frees bed space for new patients","It prolongs stay and increases cost, and there is no bed space for new patients","It reduces the price of drugs","It shortens the operation time"],"a":1,"e":"Infection prolongs stay and reduces revenue."},{"id":"l05-1nkzcx","s":34,"q":"Why is regular equipment maintenance with back-up equipment and power needed?","o":["To lower the price of surgery and reduce the cost of staff training","To reduce the number of nurses in theatre","To make use of old machines","To avoid complications and intra-operative mortality"],"a":3,"e":"Reliable equipment protects patients."},{"id":"l05-1to6yph","s":35,"q":"What should be available to deal with life-threatening emergencies in theatre?","o":["A supply of oral analgesics, dressings and a ward trolley for minor emergencies in theatre","A fire extinguisher and a ward trolley","A second operating table and spare gowns","Emergency intubation facilities, a cardiac life-support machine and staff trained in CPR"],"a":3,"e":"The unit must be equipped and staff trained for resuscitation."},{"id":"l05-1d7zu03","s":35,"q":"What is done when the diathermy machine stops working?","o":["The operation is stopped until the machine has been repaired by the technician","A standby machine is used, for example to control bleeding","The surgeon uses only sutures for bleeding","The patient is moved to another theatre"],"a":1,"e":"Standby equipment ensures work continues."}]},{"id":"l06","title":"Postoperative Assessment","emoji":"🔍","recap":["The post-operative period runs from the last suture to the final follow-up; assess against normal values and pre-operative baselines.","Prepare equipment and charts, and get a handover from the anaesthesia provider (agents, blood loss, fluids, complications, pain plan).","Assess every system on arrival: respiratory, cardiovascular, skin and wound, digestive, musculoskeletal, nervous, renal.","Watch for bleeding, shock, wound infection, pulmonary problems, UTI and hydration problems; keep the recovery environment safe and quiet."],"slides":34,"questions":[{"id":"l06-17ry68m","s":7,"q":"When does the post-operative period begin and end?","o":["From the last suture in theatre to the last follow-up when the patient has recovered","From admission to the ward until the patient is discharged home with a follow-up appointment","From the end of anaesthesia until the first meal is taken","From the patient's arrival in recovery until the wound has healed"],"a":0,"e":"The period spans the last suture to the final follow-up visit."},{"id":"l06-1t995nk","s":7,"q":"What does post-operative assessment compare findings with?","o":["The surgeon's expected recovery chart for that operation only","The values of other patients in the ward","Normal values and baseline data collected pre-operatively","The values taken during the operation only"],"a":2,"e":"Findings are compared with normal values and pre-operative baselines."},{"id":"l06-16ufiol","s":8,"q":"Which equipment is used to check respiratory rate and measure urine?","o":["A pulse oximeter for breaths, and a thermometer for urine","A sphygmomanometer for breaths, and a stethoscope for urine","A thermometer for breaths, and a watch for urine","A watch for counting breaths, and measuring jars for urine"],"a":3,"e":"A watch times respiration over a minute and jars measure body fluids."},{"id":"l06-mhzlr9","s":8,"q":"Which device measures both pulse rate and oxygen saturation?","o":["Sphygmomanometer","Thermometer","Stethoscope","Pulse oximeter"],"a":3,"e":"The pulse oximeter measures pulse and oxygen saturation."},{"id":"l06-42f7bn","s":9,"q":"Which chart is used to check level of consciousness?","o":["Fluid balance chart","Glasgow Coma Scale","Pain chart","Vital signs chart"],"a":1,"e":"The Glasgow Coma Scale assesses consciousness."},{"id":"l06-7oeb3n","s":10,"q":"What information is confirmed with the anaesthesia provider on handover?","o":["Cost of the operation, billing arrangements, insurance status and the planned discharge date from the ward","Anaesthetic and reversal agents, blood and fluid loss and replacement, and significant vital sign problems","Surgeon's training, theatre number and list order","Visiting hours, diet and the family's address"],"a":1,"e":"These details help identify deviations from the intra-operative state."},{"id":"l06-f74292","s":11,"q":"Which items are also confirmed in the handover?","o":["Complications, pre-operative diagnoses like diabetes and allergies, pain plans, language barriers and family location","The patient's religion, bank details, the surgeon's phone number and the type of gown used for the case","The surgeon's phone number and theatre cleaning schedule","The type of gown used and the number of instruments"],"a":0,"e":"These considerations guide immediate post-operative care."},{"id":"l06-dw7spq","s":13,"q":"Which finding on respiratory assessment may be due to pain or anaesthetic effects?","o":["Shallow respiration","Barrel chest","Clubbing of the fingers","Hoarse voice before surgery"],"a":0,"e":"Shallow breathing may be due to pain or anaesthesia."},{"id":"l06-1x2swyu","s":14,"q":"What may noisy respiration after surgery suggest?","o":["Accumulation of secretions","Dehydration from the anaesthetic","Fever","Wound infection"],"a":0,"e":"Noisy breathing may be due to secretions."},{"id":"l06-2lshun","s":15,"q":"Which combination of findings suggests shock or internal haemorrhage?","o":["Slow bounding pulse with raised systolic pressure and warm, flushed, dry skin","Shallow, fast pulse and a consistently falling systolic blood pressure","Normal pulse with slightly raised temperature","Regular pulse with a raised diastolic pressure"],"a":1,"e":"A fast weak pulse with dropping systolic BP signals shock."},{"id":"l06-1vs07dp","s":16,"q":"Why are skin temperature, moisture and colour assessed?","o":["To estimate the patient's age","To decide on the anaesthetic type","To detect allergies to the dressing","To identify signs of shock early"],"a":3,"e":"Cold clammy or warm moist skin may signal shock."},{"id":"l06-a0btii","s":16,"q":"Why are wound drainage amount and colour documented over time?","o":["To help identify haemorrhage early","To decide the diet to be given","To measure the patient's temperature","To plan the discharge date"],"a":0,"e":"Changing drainage can reveal bleeding."},{"id":"l06-1iw2vuh","s":17,"q":"Why are IV sites assessed after surgery?","o":["To measure blood pressure on the same arm and check the patient's pulse","To prevent the patient moving the arm","To decide when to take out the sutures","To detect phlebitis and ensure correct rate and solution"],"a":3,"e":"IV sites are checked for patency, phlebitis, rate and solution errors."},{"id":"l06-1tebk4o","s":18,"q":"Why does the nurse auscultate bowel sounds after surgery?","o":["To decide when to give analgesics and when to start walking","To check for lung expansion","To know when to start clear fluids and a fluid diet","To test wound healing"],"a":2,"e":"Bowel sounds guide when feeding can begin."},{"id":"l06-mtyuol","s":18,"q":"What may cause muscle cramps or soreness after surgery?","o":["Dehydration from the anaesthetic","Infection of the wound","Pressure from the dressing","Positional injury from prolonged lying during the procedure"],"a":3,"e":"Long operations in one position may injure muscles."},{"id":"l06-gaej1z","s":19,"q":"Which nervous system aspects are assessed after surgery?","o":["Taste, smell, skin colour, temperature and capillary refill of the fingers and toes","Consciousness level, speech, orientation, movement of extremities and pain","Urine colour, bowel sounds and swallowing","Hearing, vision and the colour of the nails"],"a":1,"e":"These measure neurological recovery and baseline pain."},{"id":"l06-1u57mqv","s":19,"q":"Why does the nurse note the time, type and route of the last analgesic dose?","o":["It tells the surgeon how long the operation took and how much was given","It gives a baseline for judging how effective pain management is","It helps decide the next meal time","It shows when the wound was last dressed"],"a":1,"e":"It provides a baseline for assessing pain management."},{"id":"l06-1fz09y","s":20,"q":"Why are uncatheterised patients assessed for the urge to urinate and bladder distension?","o":["To identify urinary retention early","To measure the colour and smell of the urine","To check for kidney stones","To plan the discharge date"],"a":0,"e":"Distension and inability to pass urine suggest retention."},{"id":"l06-1g1fk2l","s":20,"q":"What is documented for patients having genitourinary surgery with irrigation?","o":["The colour of the dressing only, noted once a day","The number of voids per week","The patient's bowel movements","The amount of irrigant set up and drained"],"a":3,"e":"Irrigant in and out is recorded."},{"id":"l06-wuio9r","s":22,"q":"What may be done for bleeding caused by inadequate haemostasis?","o":["The bleeding is ignored until it stops naturally and the dressing is simply changed once every day by the ward nurse","The patient may be returned to theatre for exploration and ligation of the bleeding vessel","The patient is given an anticoagulant","The wound is washed with normal saline each hour"],"a":1,"e":"Bleeding from inadequate haemostasis may need re-exploration."},{"id":"l06-15wr0k1","s":23,"q":"What should the nurse do on noticing signs of shock?","o":["Give oral fluids and observe the patient for an hour before reporting","Raise the head of the bed fully","Wait for the next ward round","Promote circulation to the head and report immediately"],"a":3,"e":"Promote circulation to the head and report immediately."},{"id":"l06-1t0mwix","s":23,"q":"Which signs suggest wound infection?","o":["Pallor, coldness, numbness, dry dressing and slightly dry skin around the wound","Clear thin drainage with no pain","Itching and a flat scar","Redness, warmth, tenderness or pain, increased discharge and dehiscence"],"a":3,"e":"These are local signs of inflammation."},{"id":"l06-l7f6l6","s":24,"q":"Which measures help prevent atelectasis and pneumonia?","o":["Deep breathing and coughing, incentive spirometer, pain control and early ambulation","Bed rest and sedation until the patient is discharged, with no movement out of the bed at all","Restricting fluids and avoiding coughing","Lying flat with a pillow under the head"],"a":0,"e":"Pulmonary expansion is promoted by these measures."},{"id":"l06-18df0x4","s":25,"q":"Why does adequate pain control reduce the risk of urinary tract infection?","o":["It increases urine concentration and shortens the time the patient has to drink","It keeps the catheter in place longer","It promotes complete bladder emptying and early catheter removal","It reduces fluid intake"],"a":2,"e":"Pain control allows full emptying and early catheter removal."},{"id":"l06-xyhfi4","s":26,"q":"Which urine output is described as oliguria?","o":["More than 1,500 mL per day of dilute urine","About 800 mL per day","Less than 300 to 500 mL per day","No urine for one week"],"a":2,"e":"The lecture defines oliguria as under 300-500 mL a day."},{"id":"l06-18phk85","s":26,"q":"What does the nurse do to prevent over-hydration or dehydration after surgery?","o":["Give all fluids quickly in the first hour after surgery and then stop recording the intake and output","Stop IV fluids once the patient wakes","Estimate intake without recording","Give the correct drop rate, keep strict intake and output records and assess regularly"],"a":3,"e":"Strict monitoring prevents hydration problems."},{"id":"l06-76dgwi","s":28,"q":"Where is the stable post-operative patient transferred for further management?","o":["The recovery ward or post-anaesthesia care unit","The antenatal clinic or the general outpatient waiting area","The outpatient department","The X-ray department"],"a":0,"e":"The PACU provides the best setting, equipment and personnel for monitoring recovery."},{"id":"l06-1ahzpk1","s":28,"q":"What does adequate pre-operative preparation help with post-operatively?","o":["It removes the need for observation and routine assessment by the nurse after the operation","It shortens the operation","It prevents all complications","It prepares the patient for the changes in their post-operative environment and care"],"a":3,"e":"Pre-operative preparation prepares patients for the post-operative setting."},{"id":"l06-ayyjgu","s":30,"q":"Why should the recovery environment be free from noise and loud laughter?","o":["They may be misinterpreted by a subconscious or recovering patient","They can disturb other staff working in the recovery ward at the same time","They damage the equipment","They reduce the anaesthetic effect"],"a":0,"e":"Sounds may be misinterpreted by recovering patients."},{"id":"l06-8uw7lz","s":30,"q":"Which measures prevent falls in the recovery ward?","o":["Lower the side rails and raise the bed","Raise the side rails and lower the bed","Remove the side rails and use pillows","Raise the bed and use restraints"],"a":1,"e":"Side rails up and bed low reduce falls and injuries."},{"id":"l06-106b77z","s":31,"q":"Why are patients' hearing aids and glasses offered when stable?","o":["They help the patient read the notes and follow the surgeon's instructions","Impaired vision, hearing and speech raise the risk of injury","They are part of the theatre dress code","They calm relatives"],"a":1,"e":"Impaired senses increase risk of injury."},{"id":"l06-o5wwzr","s":31,"q":"Where should used needles and blades be discarded?","o":["Into the ordinary waste bin","Promptly into sharps boxes","Back into the instrument tray","Onto a clean towel until counted"],"a":1,"e":"Sharps go into sharps boxes to prevent injuries."},{"id":"l06-mphrk7","s":32,"q":"How should restraints be used for aggressive patients?","o":["Routinely for all patients recovering from anaesthesia","Only when required and guided by facility protocols","As soon as the patient wakes","Whenever visiting hours begin"],"a":1,"e":"Restraints are used only when needed and as protocol guides."},{"id":"l06-5vhupp","s":32,"q":"Why should the recovery ward temperature be modulated?","o":["To prevent infection of the wound and the airway","To keep the patients awake","To protect the equipment","To prevent post-operative hypothermia"],"a":3,"e":"Controlled warmth prevents hypothermia."}]},{"id":"l07","title":"Postoperative Nursing Care","emoji":"🛏️","recap":["Post-operative care has two phases: immediate care until anaesthesia wears off, then general care to prepare for discharge.","Immediate care: lift as a unit, nurse unconscious patients recumbent with head to the side, keep the airway clear, give oxygen and IV fluids, watch for bleeding and shock, and give pain relief before the patient asks.","General care: observations, aseptic wound care, pain control, exercises and early walking, hygiene, rest, staged feeding.","Discharge planning starts when surgery is decided; day cases go home within 12 hours if criteria are met."],"slides":46,"questions":[{"id":"l07-1gjver9","s":8,"q":"Where is post-operative care provided?","o":["Only on the surgical ward, ending as soon as the wound has been dressed and checked","Only in the recovery room, ending when the patient is handed over to relatives","In the theatre and outpatient department only, stopping once the patient leaves the ward","Theatre, ICU or recovery ward and surgical ward, and it continues after discharge"],"a":3,"e":"Care runs through the theatre, recovery or ICU, ward and beyond discharge."},{"id":"l07-1k2typ9","s":9,"q":"What are the aims of post-operative care?","o":["Reduce the cost of surgery, shorten theatre lists and increase the number of cases done daily","Confirm the original diagnosis, plan any further operations and update the surgical records","Hand control of the patient's care to relatives and arrange early transfer to a clinic","Facilitate recovery, minimise complications and prepare the patient for discharge"],"a":3,"e":"Post-operative care promotes recovery, prevents complications and prepares for discharge."},{"id":"l07-bj18po","s":9,"q":"How is post-operative care divided into two phases?","o":["Care in theatre until the wound is closed, then care at home with a community nurse only","Care in the first 24 hours after surgery, then routine care continuing after the first week","Immediate care until anaesthesia wears off, then general care aimed at discharge","Care on the general ward until stitches are removed, then care in the intensive care unit"],"a":2,"e":"The first phase is until anaesthesia wears off; the second prepares the patient for discharge."},{"id":"l07-vi6oty","s":10,"q":"What determines the length of stay in the recovery ward?","o":["The type of surgery and the patient's condition","The seniority of the surgeon and the time the operation started that day","The number of free beds on the surgical ward and the visiting hours set","The patient's own preference and the wishes of relatives waiting outside"],"a":0,"e":"Length of stay depends on the type of surgery and condition."},{"id":"l07-1zzmf7","s":12,"q":"How is the immediate post-operative phase characterised?","o":["By complete safety as soon as the patient wakes, so that observation can be relaxed","By potential risks that need skilled assessment and observation","By a need for rest alone, with observation delayed until the next morning round","By routine care that follows a fixed schedule, with no skilled monitoring required"],"a":1,"e":"This phase carries potential risks, so skilled observation is essential."},{"id":"l07-19s6lfe","s":13,"q":"How should the post-operative patient be transferred onto the ward bed?","o":["Gently, lifting as a unit on the count of three, to prevent wound gaping","Quickly, with one nurse pulling the draw sheet across so the patient is not disturbed","Slowly, asking the patient to roll over unaided to build strength after the operation","By asking the patient to climb across the trolley gap, which encourages early independence"],"a":0,"e":"Gentle lifting together prevents the wound from gaping."},{"id":"l07-25in0h","s":13,"q":"Which accessories should the post-operative bed have ready?","o":["Hot water bottles, extra blankets and an electric fan for temperature control","Extra pillows, a footstool and a bedside table for meals and personal items","A reading lamp, a radio and a visitor's chair for the patient's family","Drip stand, suction apparatus and oxygen source"],"a":3,"e":"These supports are needed for immediate care."},{"id":"l07-1o9ylor","s":14,"q":"How is an unconscious post-operative patient positioned?","o":["Sitting upright supported by two or three pillows to ease breathing and drainage","Recumbent, without pillows, with the head turned to the side","Head down with the legs raised on pillows to keep blood flowing to the brain","Lying flat on the back with the head kept straight and a pillow under the shoulders"],"a":1,"e":"Recumbent with head turned to the side protects the airway."},{"id":"l07-i214d0","s":15,"q":"What oxygen dose is usually given to adults after surgery?","o":["1 to 2 litres per minute for every patient regardless of the saturation reading","10 to 12 litres per minute for every patient to keep the saturation at 100 percent","4 to 6 litres per minute, as prescribed or as guided by saturation","None, because oxygen is only given after surgery when the patient is cyanosed at rest"],"a":2,"e":"The adult dose is usually 4 to 6 litres per minute."},{"id":"l07-znbpvm","s":15,"q":"When should the absence of urine be reported to the surgeon?","o":["About 4 to 8 hours after surgery","After 24 hours, once the patient has been seen by the surgeon on ward rounds","After 48 hours, when the catheter would normally be removed from the patient","After one week, if the patient is still passing very little urine at home"],"a":0,"e":"If there is no urine 4 to 8 hours after surgery, report."},{"id":"l07-1j83ep7","s":16,"q":"When should analgesics be given in the immediate post-operative period?","o":["Only when the patient asks for them and rates the pain above five out of ten","Without waiting for the patient to complain of pain","Only after the patient has eaten a first meal and is able to tolerate fluids","Only once the patient is fully awake, talking clearly and able to describe the pain"],"a":1,"e":"Do not wait for the patient to complain of pain."},{"id":"l07-1e3jirh","s":16,"q":"What should the nurse do if the surgical site is bleeding?","o":["Remove the dressing to inspect the wound, then replace it and report during the next round","Wait and observe the site for one hour before deciding whether to report to the surgeon","Wash the wound with saline until the bleeding stops, then apply a fresh light dressing","Reassure the patient, reinforce the dressing and inform the surgeon immediately"],"a":3,"e":"Reinforce the wound and report at once."},{"id":"l07-z0wtop","s":17,"q":"How often are vital signs taken in the immediate post-operative period?","o":["Every 12 hours throughout the first day, then once a day until discharge from hospital","Once a day at the morning round, then twice weekly until the wound has fully healed","Only when the patient looks unwell or complains, then at the end of each shift","Every 15 to 30 minutes until stable, then 1 to 4 hourly"],"a":3,"e":"Frequency is 15-30 minutes then spaced to 1-4 hourly."},{"id":"l07-1xnlgho","s":17,"q":"Which two vital signs may first indicate internal bleeding?","o":["Temperature and respiration","Pain score and urine output","Pulse and blood pressure","Oxygen saturation and weight"],"a":2,"e":"Pulse and blood pressure change first with internal bleeding."},{"id":"l07-1bunos8","s":18,"q":"Why are padded side rails used for post-operative patients?","o":["They prevent the patient from eating or drinking while the intravenous line is running","They hold the drip stand and the suction apparatus steady beside the bed","Some react to anaesthesia and become confused and restless","They stop the bed from rolling when the nurse is changing the dressings"],"a":2,"e":"Confused patients risk falling."},{"id":"l07-lfx8yk","s":19,"q":"Why must the patient not swallow the water used to rinse the mouth?","o":["It would dilute the anaesthetic still circulating in the blood and shorten its action","It would trigger vomiting from the acid already collected in the empty stomach","It may cause complications such as paralytic ileus","It makes the wound heal slowly because the fluid is taken from the tissues"],"a":2,"e":"Swallowing water too early risks paralytic ileus."},{"id":"l07-dnbekc","s":21,"q":"Which observations are made on the fully conscious post-operative patient?","o":["Vital signs and dietary intake only, with all other findings left to the surgeon's review next morning","Wound appearance and dressings only, with the remaining systems checked only at the time of discharge","Vital signs, wound, hydration, drug effects, sleep, drains, ambulation, pain and bowel sounds","Urine output and bowel sounds only, since the other observations are done by the doctors on their rounds"],"a":2,"e":"Many aspects are observed and abnormalities reported."},{"id":"l07-2yvojc","s":22,"q":"Which wound care practice is correct?","o":["Cleaning with any available tap water and leaving the dressing as tight as the patient tolerates","Leaving the first tight dressing in place unchanged until the day the stitches are removed","Aseptic dressing with an appropriate lotion such as normal saline, not too tight","Letting the patient touch and press on the wound regularly to check that it is healing well"],"a":2,"e":"Aseptic technique and non-restrictive dressings are required."},{"id":"l07-1xnxd3b","s":22,"q":"How should the patient be advised about the wound?","o":["To scratch it if it itches","Not to touch it","To wet it daily","To tighten the dressing"],"a":1,"e":"Patients are taught not to touch the wound."},{"id":"l07-1s6ce0s","s":23,"q":"What is dressed first when a sutured wound and a drainage site are both present?","o":["The drainage site before the sutured wound","Both at the same time with one swab","The sutured wound before the drainage site","Whichever is nearer to the nurse"],"a":2,"e":"Dress the clean sutured wound first to prevent infection."},{"id":"l07-aqg0og","s":23,"q":"Which pain management principle is correct?","o":["Give only one analgesic even when two are prescribed, and wait until the patient reports severe pain","Withhold analgesics until the patient asks, and choose the drug according to the nurse's own preference","Give all prescribed analgesics, do not wait for complaints, and involve the patient","Give analgesics at the surgeon's convenience and avoid involving the patient in decisions about doses"],"a":2,"e":"Maintain individuality and administer as prescribed."},{"id":"l07-zwq4z4","s":24,"q":"What should the nurse do when patient-controlled analgesia or epidural analgesia is available?","o":["Avoid it altogether because these techniques are considered unsafe on a general ward","Let the relatives operate the device so that the patient can rest without interruption","Use it and educate the patient on its effective use","Give additional morphine by injection as well to make sure the pain stays controlled"],"a":2,"e":"Advanced pain techniques are used and patients are educated."},{"id":"l07-1o8zy63","s":25,"q":"Why are exercises and early ambulation encouraged after surgery?","o":["To make the wound heal faster by gently stretching the edges of the incision each day","To prevent deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism","To reduce the need for intravenous fluids by improving the patient's oral intake early","To improve the appetite and so shorten the period of staged feeding after the operation"],"a":1,"e":"Movement helps prevent DVT and pulmonary embolism."},{"id":"l07-1ivaoon","s":25,"q":"Which drug is used to prevent deep vein thrombosis?","o":["Paracetamol","Heparin","Diazepam","Atropine"],"a":1,"e":"Heparin therapy helps prevent DVT."},{"id":"l07-818dh5","s":26,"q":"How does effective pain management support early ambulation?","o":["It delays ambulation","It prevents the patient from sleeping","It reduces the need for exercises","It makes early ambulation possible"],"a":3,"e":"Controlled pain allows early walking."},{"id":"l07-1qciu0z","s":27,"q":"Which advice supports personal hygiene after surgery?","o":["Soak the wound daily in warm water to soften the dressing and loosen debris","Keep the wound dry to prevent infection","Use heavily perfumed soaps and powders around the wound to keep the area fresh","Avoid bathing for at least two weeks so that the dressing is never disturbed"],"a":1,"e":"Educate patients to keep the wound dry."},{"id":"l07-swfxxh","s":28,"q":"How is rest and sleep promoted after surgery?","o":["Waking the patient every hour for observations so that early complications are not missed overnight","Playing soft music through the night and allowing relatives to stay with the patient at all times","Keeping the ward lights on and giving all nursing procedures separately at regular hourly intervals","Grouping nursing procedures, restricting visitors, comfortable positions and good pain control"],"a":3,"e":"These measures conserve rest."},{"id":"l07-ca67wr","s":29,"q":"Which activities are part of general post-operative care?","o":["Delivering the anaesthesia and monitoring the patient's response until the operation has finished","Observing elimination, giving privacy with bedpans, and sending theatre specimens to the laboratory","Measuring the temperature only and leaving all other observations to the surgeon on the ward round","Leaving all specimens in theatre and giving bedpans in the open ward to save the nurse's time"],"a":1,"e":"These are listed among general care activities."},{"id":"l07-5aphns","s":30,"q":"How is feeding progressed after abdominal surgery?","o":["A normal diet on the same day of surgery, with intravenous fluids only if the patient vomits afterwards","Intravenous fluids only for the first two weeks, then a sudden change to a full normal diet","IV fluids until bowel sounds return, then oral sips, fluid diet, semi-solid and normal diet","Solid food first to restart the bowel, and then fluids and sips once the patient is hungry"],"a":2,"e":"Feeding is staged as bowel sounds return."},{"id":"l07-1ewct9l","s":30,"q":"Which nutrients and foods are encouraged after surgery?","o":["Fatty foods and sweets for energy, with extra salt to replace the losses from surgery","Only clear fluids for the first week, with no solid foods until the wound has closed","Spicy and highly seasoned foods to stimulate the appetite and speed up bowel movement","Vitamins and protein for healing, and fibre to prevent constipation"],"a":3,"e":"Vitamins, protein and fibre are recommended."},{"id":"l07-13s4vxj","s":32,"q":"When does discharge planning begin?","o":["On the day of discharge, once the surgeon has signed the discharge summary form","As soon as the decision for surgery is made","After the wound has healed, when the stitches or staples have been removed","After the first dressing change, when the nurse has assessed the patient's progress"],"a":1,"e":"Discharge planning begins at the decision for surgery."},{"id":"l07-15drg8z","s":32,"q":"When may a patient leave the recovery room for the ward?","o":["As soon as the last stitch is placed and the surgeon has finished writing the operation notes in the chart","When anaesthesia has worn off and blood pressure, breathing and oxygen saturation are satisfactory","When the surgeon's operating list has ended for the day and the theatre staff are free to transport the patient","When the relatives arrive at the hospital and the ward has a vacant bed ready to receive the patient"],"a":1,"e":"Recovery indicators must be satisfactory."},{"id":"l07-1oi6mo0","s":33,"q":"Which instructions are given to the patient and caregiver at discharge?","o":["Verbal instructions only, given once to the patient at the time of leaving the ward","Written instructions only, handed to the relatives without any follow-up appointment details","Verbal and written instructions with follow-up information","No instructions are needed once the surgeon has certified the patient as fit to go home"],"a":2,"e":"Both forms plus follow-up information are given."},{"id":"l07-6hf6qh","s":34,"q":"Which are examples of adjuvant therapies mentioned for post-operative patients?","o":["Blood transfusion, peritoneal dialysis and intravenous antibiotic infusion therapy","Radiotherapy, routine vaccination and nutritional supplement therapy for the patient","Oxygen therapy, nebulisation and suction delivered while still in the theatre","Warm sitz baths, chemotherapy and physiotherapy"],"a":3,"e":"These therapies can accompany post-operative care."},{"id":"l07-1my6vw6","s":35,"q":"What should patient and caregiver be able to state about complications?","o":["Early or late signs and how to seek urgent care","The name of the anaesthetic used and the dose that was given in theatre","The cost of the operation and the expected date of the next clinic review","The surgeon's phone number and the name of the nurse who dressed the wound"],"a":0,"e":"They need to know signs and where to get urgent help."},{"id":"l07-y715nf","s":36,"q":"Why are patients given return demonstration for self-care such as stoma care?","o":["To prove to the surgeon that the nurse is able to carry out the procedure","To allow the nurse to give constructive feedback","To save time at discharge by shortening the amount of teaching that is needed","To teach the family members only, since the patient cannot manage the procedure"],"a":1,"e":"Return demonstration confirms skill."},{"id":"l07-qost9u","s":38,"q":"How is a day surgical case defined?","o":["Admitted, operated and discharged home within 12 hours of the same day","Admitted for three days with an early discharge before the stitches have been removed","Admitted overnight for observation and discharged after the first morning ward round","Operated in the clinic under local anaesthesia with no admission or recovery period"],"a":0,"e":"Day cases go home within 12 hours."},{"id":"l07-13xs8ui","s":38,"q":"What is the term when a patient needs an overnight stay?","o":["23-hour stay","Short stay","Elective admission","Extended stay"],"a":0,"e":"The lecture calls it a '23 hour stay'."},{"id":"l07-nwx0gi","s":39,"q":"Which measure helps reduce unplanned overnight admissions?","o":["Schedule the procedure early on the list and ensure the home can support recovery","Schedule it last on the list so that the patient has more time to rest before surgery","Use general anaesthesia for all day cases so that the patient sleeps through the procedure","Discharge without teaching, so that the patient is not overwhelmed by too much information"],"a":0,"e":"Early scheduling and home support minimise unplanned stays."},{"id":"l07-6j3xv","s":40,"q":"Which procedures can be done as day cases?","o":["Heart transplant, emergency laparotomy for trauma and major reconstruction of the leg arteries","Hernia repair, haemorrhoidectomy, laparoscopic cholecystectomy and breast lesion biopsy","Liver resection, gastrectomy, oesophagectomy and any other major cancer operation in the abdomen","Brain tumour removal, spinal fusion and other long operations needing intensive care afterwards"],"a":1,"e":"Examples are listed under abdominal and breast."},{"id":"l07-1uf3eap","s":41,"q":"What is the main requirement for day surgery?","o":["Overnight observation in the ward so that any complications are picked up early on","A large family able to supervise the patient closely at home for the first week","General anaesthesia, since it ensures the patient is not aware during the procedure","Suitable pain control and ability to drink and eat within a reasonable time"],"a":3,"e":"Pain control and oral intake are key."},{"id":"l07-4g07qp","s":42,"q":"How long should vital signs be stable before a day-case patient is discharged?","o":["Only 10 minutes, as long as the readings have been recorded once","At least 6 hours, with readings taken hourly by the ward nurse","A full 24 hours, with readings taken every 15 minutes through the night","At least 1 hour"],"a":3,"e":"Vital signs should be stable for at least an hour."},{"id":"l07-18zf176","s":43,"q":"Before discharge the day-case patient should be able to do what?","o":["Dress and walk, with minimal nausea, vomiting or dizziness and able to take oral fluids","Lie flat for six hours after the operation, with no nausea and no attempt to stand or walk","Eat a full meal of solid food and be completely free of every sensation of pain and discomfort","Pass urine, empty the bowels and sign the discharge forms without any help from the nurse"],"a":0,"e":"These are discharge criteria."},{"id":"l07-14rkgki","s":44,"q":"After a genitourinary procedure like prostate biopsy, what must the nurse confirm?","o":["That the patient has passed urine","That the patient has eaten a full meal and kept it down without vomiting","That the patient has bathed and changed into clean clothes before leaving","That the patient has slept for at least two hours since the biopsy was done"],"a":0,"e":"Passing urine is confirmed."},{"id":"l07-1c4pt6u","s":44,"q":"Who must take the day-case patient home?","o":["A responsible adult","The patient alone","A taxi driver","A neighbour who has met the nurse"],"a":0,"e":"A responsible adult must accompany the patient."}]},{"id":"l08","title":"Wound and Burns Care","emoji":"🩹","recap":["A wound is a break in tissue continuity; healing runs through inflammatory (4-5 days), proliferative (to 21 days) and maturation (day 21 to 2 years) phases.","Healing is impaired by poor oxygen supply, smoking, malnutrition and infection; closure may be primary, secondary or tertiary.","Wound care: assess edges, sutures, drainage; keep dressings aseptic; use only prescribed cleaning solutions; remove stitches on time.","Burns: classify by cause and depth, estimate area (rule of nines or palms), prevent fluid loss and infection, manage pain and nutrition."],"slides":46,"questions":[{"id":"l08-43bpoc","s":7,"q":"Which statement best describes a wound?","o":["A localised collection of pus formed after bacterial infection of the skin","A temporary colour change of the skin caused by pressure on a limb","A break in the continuity of tissue caused by injury or surgical incision","A swelling of tissue that follows an allergic reaction to a drug or food"],"a":2,"e":"A wound is a break in tissue continuity with disruption of structure and function."},{"id":"l08-1q73uy6","s":7,"q":"Which term describes a wound that fails to heal?","o":["Chronic","Acute","Avulsion","Laceration"],"a":0,"e":"A chronic wound fails to heal."},{"id":"l08-14ov6og","s":8,"q":"Which of these are physical agents that can cause wounds?","o":["Toxins, acids and other corrosive chemical substances in contact with the skin","Bacteria, viruses and fungi invading the skin through a small break","Blunt or penetrating trauma, cold, heat and irradiation","Hypersensitivity reactions to drugs, latex or certain foods in the body"],"a":2,"e":"Trauma, cold, heat and irradiation are physical agents."},{"id":"l08-1n5ij26","s":10,"q":"What is an ulcer?","o":["A break in the continuity of mucous membrane with sloughing of necrotic tissue","A clean surgical cut made through the skin and underlying fat that is closed with sutures at once","A raised collection of fluid under the skin that develops after friction or a minor burn injury","A tear within a muscle belly that follows heavy lifting or a sudden sporting injury to the limb"],"a":0,"e":"An ulcer is a lesion with sloughing of necrotic tissue, usually linked to poor perfusion."},{"id":"l08-1l0l70k","s":10,"q":"Which can cause an ulcer?","o":["Rapid epithelial healing, excessive collagen formation in the wound bed and a brisk blood supply","High vitamin C intake, good blood supply to the limb and early use of correctly applied moist dressings","Arterial or venous stasis, caustic agents, extreme temperature, or pressure on the skin","Regular moist dressings changed by the nurse in charge, plus adequate protein and early walking"],"a":2,"e":"Stasis, caustic agents, extreme temperatures and traction or pressure cause ulcers."},{"id":"l08-2czdau","s":14,"q":"Which features characterise the inflammatory phase of healing?","o":["Redness, heat, pain and swelling lasting about 4 to 5 days","Beefy red tissue and new capillary growth lasting up to 21 days","Increase in tensile strength continuing for as long as 2 years","A sealed epithelial layer that protects against bacteria and fluid loss"],"a":0,"e":"The inflammatory phase lasts about 4 to 5 days with the classic signs."},{"id":"l08-1xz1dq2","s":14,"q":"What does platelet activity do in the inflammatory phase?","o":["Stops bleeding and triggers the immune response","Forms new capillaries that supply oxygen and nutrients to the developing tissue","Builds the collagen matrix that later gives the healed wound its tensile strength","Seals the wound surface with a new layer of epithelium that blocks bacteria"],"a":0,"e":"Platelets stabilise the wound and start the immune response."},{"id":"l08-dg3494","s":15,"q":"When does the proliferative phase begin and how long can it last?","o":["At day 21 after injury and continuing for as long as two years afterwards","After five days of inflammation and ending within two days of starting","Within 24 hours of injury and up to 21 days","Within one hour of injury and lasting for no more than four days in total"],"a":2,"e":"The proliferative phase begins within 24 hours and may continue for up to 21 days."},{"id":"l08-16gkco0","s":15,"q":"Which three events characterise the proliferative phase?","o":["Inflammation, haemostasis and scar contraction occurring together during repair","Bleeding, clotting and wound maturation occurring one after the other in sequence","Epithelialisation, granulation and collagen synthesis","Debridement, drainage and wound closure carried out by the surgical team together"],"a":2,"e":"Those three events define the proliferative phase."},{"id":"l08-1yjgyai","s":16,"q":"What is granulation tissue like?","o":["Beefy red tissue with new capillaries that bleeds easily","Pale dry tissue with no capillary growth that does not bleed when it is touched","Black dead tissue that has to be cut away by the surgeon before healing can occur","Yellow tissue covered with pus and a bad smell that spreads onto the nearby skin"],"a":0,"e":"Granulation tissue is beefy red, fed by new capillaries, and bleeds easily."},{"id":"l08-r3qxrh","s":17,"q":"Why is a moist environment important for the wound?","o":["It speeds up the formation of pus so that the wound can drain more quickly","It stops the body from forming new capillaries until the wound has been cleaned","It makes the dressing stay on better and for longer before it needs replacing","It fosters growth of the fragile epithelial layer"],"a":3,"e":"A moist environment promotes epithelial growth."},{"id":"l08-2ixvno","s":17,"q":"What can easily destroy the new epithelial layer?","o":["Gentle dressing of the wound with warm normal saline at the usual intervals","Covering the wound with a moist dressing that is changed only when needed","Aggressive wound irrigation or cleansing","Resting the affected area and keeping it raised above the level of the heart"],"a":2,"e":"The epithelial layer is fragile and can be destroyed by aggressive cleansing."},{"id":"l08-4qzb1t","s":18,"q":"Which nutrients are vital for collagen synthesis?","o":["Calcium, sodium, vitamin K, potassium and fat only","Fibre, water, vitamin B12, iodine and carbohydrate only","Alcohol, caffeine, sugar, salt and saturated fat","Oxygen, iron, vitamin C, zinc, magnesium and protein"],"a":3,"e":"These are the nutrients named in the lecture for collagen synthesis."},{"id":"l08-ja4p0s","s":19,"q":"What is true of the maturation phase of healing?","o":["It begins on the first day after injury and ends within one week, with full tissue strength restored","It begins at day 5 and lasts about 3 weeks, with the wound reaching full tensile strength again","It begins around day 21 and may last up to 2 years, reaching about 80% of pre-injury strength","It begins at day 21 and ends within 2 months, with the scar reaching 120% of normal strength"],"a":2,"e":"Tensile strength reaches only about 80% of pre-injury strength."},{"id":"l08-1sqcds","s":20,"q":"Which factors impair wound healing?","o":["Increased epidermal turnover, good capillary strength, high oxygen delivery and a thick layer of tissue","Balanced nutrition, a strong immune response, plenty of dermal tissue and a well oxygenated blood supply","Reduced vascularisation and oxygen flow, altered nutrition, impaired immunity and reduced tissue mass","Adequate fluid intake, a good blood supply, increased subcutaneous mass and vigorous immune activity"],"a":2,"e":"These are factors that impair healing."},{"id":"l08-13i5axz","s":22,"q":"Why is oxygen delivery needed for wound healing?","o":["To kill all the bacteria inside the wound at once and keep the area sterile","For cellular respiration and hydroxylation of proline and lysine","To dry the wound surface quickly so that a hard protective scab forms over it","To make the pain at the wound site less severe by numbing the exposed nerves"],"a":1,"e":"Oxygen is needed for cellular respiration and hydroxylation of proline and lysine."},{"id":"l08-mv3u58","s":22,"q":"How does smoking impair wound healing?","o":["It increases blood flow to the wound so strongly that new cells are washed out of it","It raises vitamin C levels in the blood and makes newly formed collagen too stiff to heal","Vasoconstriction, atherosclerosis and carboxyhaemoglobin reduce oxygen delivery","It speeds up formation of the epithelial layer and so causes excessive scarring later"],"a":2,"e":"Smoking reduces oxygen delivery through those mechanisms."},{"id":"l08-1bg1syh","s":23,"q":"How does vitamin C deficiency affect wound healing?","o":["It increases the speed of collagen production and so causes thick, raised scars in the wound","It prevents platelets from clumping together and so causes severe bleeding at the site","It lowers the body temperature and so slows the immune response to wound bacteria","It diminishes hydroxylation of lysine and proline, so collagen is poorly made"],"a":3,"e":"Vitamin C deficiency affects hydroxylation of lysine and proline."},{"id":"l08-1b4y8pj","s":23,"q":"Which pairing of deficiency and effect is correct?","o":["Zinc - impaired bone healing; vitamin D - inhibited cell proliferation","Zinc - inhibited cell proliferation; vitamin D - impaired bone healing","Zinc - excess bleeding; vitamin D - weakened immunity","Zinc - raised blood sugar; vitamin D - delayed clotting"],"a":1,"e":"Zinc deficiency inhibits proliferation and deficient granulation; vitamin D affects bone."},{"id":"l08-1n40faa","s":24,"q":"What is primary wound closure?","o":["Immediate sealing of the wound by suturing, skin graft or flap","Spontaneous healing by contraction with no active attempt to close","Delayed closure after repeated debridement to control infection","Packing the wound open until granulation tissue fills it"],"a":0,"e":"Primary closure (first intention) is immediate sealing."},{"id":"l08-vc05vq","s":24,"q":"What is secondary wound closure?","o":["Spontaneous closure by re-epithelialisation and contraction, with no active intent to seal","Immediate suturing of a clean surgical incision using sterile materials under aseptic technique","Delayed suturing of the wound after the infection has been controlled and the wound cleaned","Closure of the wound using only steri-strips and plaster, applied within the first hour"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary intention healing is spontaneous, used in highly contaminated wounds."},{"id":"l08-1224dki","s":24,"q":"What is tertiary wound closure?","o":["Delayed primary closure of a contaminated wound after infection is controlled","Immediate closure of a clean surgical wound with staples soon after the injury occurs","Spontaneous healing by contraction of the wound edges with no suturing of any kind done","Closing the wound with a simple dressing and no sutures, leaving the edges to meet"],"a":0,"e":"Tertiary closure follows debridement and infection control."},{"id":"l08-9g65g9","s":26,"q":"Which are complications of wounds?","o":["Hypertrophy, hyperplasia and metaplasia","Embolism, shock and cyanosis","Constipation, nausea and headache","Contractures, abscess and infection"],"a":3,"e":"Wound complications include contractures, abscess and infection."},{"id":"l08-1twaatm","s":28,"q":"What does assessment of a surgical wound include?","o":["Wound edge approximation, suture or staple integrity, redness, warmth, swelling, tenderness and drainage","Only the colour of the outer dressing, the size of the gauze that was applied and the type of tape used","Only the patient's temperature, blood pressure and pulse, recorded at the time of each dressing change","Only the date of the operation, the type of suture used and the name of the surgeon who performed it"],"a":0,"e":"Assessment covers approximation, integrity, signs of inflammation and drainage."},{"id":"l08-5e36sg","s":29,"q":"Why are surgical wounds covered with gauze dressings?","o":["To keep the wound moist with fresh saline at all times, flush out debris and cool the tissue below","To hide the stitches and the scar from the patient and relatives so that they are not distressed","To provide a healing environment, absorb drainage, splint the wound and protect new epithelium","To make the wound heal by secondary intention, stimulate granulation and reduce the need for sutures"],"a":2,"e":"Dressings protect and support healing."},{"id":"l08-o64y7y","s":29,"q":"What are wound drains?","o":["Tubes or corrugated sheets that channel secretions out of the wound","Plastic covers that seal the wound edges tightly together and so prevent any leakage","Special sutures that slowly dissolve inside the wound and release antibiotics as they go","Bandages that absorb the blood from the wound surface and hold the dressing in place"],"a":0,"e":"Drains channel secretions into a drainage system or dressing."},{"id":"l08-huaeup","s":30,"q":"Who is usually present at the first change of a surgical dressing?","o":["The patient's relatives, who will continue the dressing care later on at home","A laboratory technician, who will take a swab from the wound for culture","The ward porter, who will carry the dressing trolley to the bedside for the nurse","A member of the surgical team, to assess the wound's integrity"],"a":3,"e":"The first dressing is done with a surgical team member present."},{"id":"l08-o5x1wu","s":30,"q":"Which precautions are taken before changing a wound dressing?","o":["Hand washing and use of sterile gloves, instruments and supplies","Wearing a mask only and using clean tap water","Using sterile gloves but reusing instruments from the last dressing","Washing the patient's hands and using paper towels"],"a":0,"e":"Infection prevention measures include hand washing and sterile items."},{"id":"l08-vc2gyb","s":31,"q":"Which cleaning lotion is used for open wounds?","o":["Methylated spirit","Normal saline","Chlorhexidine","Hydrogen peroxide"],"a":1,"e":"Normal saline is used for open wounds; spirit and chlorhexidine for closed incisions."},{"id":"l08-b1g00y","s":31,"q":"Within how many days should staples and non-absorbable sutures be removed?","o":["Within 7 days","Within 3 months","Within 24 hours","After 30 days"],"a":0,"e":"Staples and non-absorbable sutures are removed within 7 days."},{"id":"l08-1i2972p","s":32,"q":"When may alternate stitches be removed according to facility protocol?","o":["On the day of surgery","At the end of the second week","Between the 20th and 30th day","Between the 3rd and 5th day"],"a":3,"e":"Alternate stitches can be removed between the 3rd and 5th day."},{"id":"l08-1tzdwwl","s":32,"q":"What should be done about deeply embedded non-absorbable stitches that cannot be removed?","o":["Leave them without making any note, because they will dissolve on their own with time","Pull them out forcefully with forceps and then dress the area with a fresh gauze","Cut down through the skin over them at the bedside using a sterile blade and forceps","Document them and tell other surgical team members for prompt action"],"a":3,"e":"They must be documented and communicated."},{"id":"l08-jce1bn","s":34,"q":"Which are functions of the skin?","o":["Production of bile, storage of glucose, filtration of blood and digestion of dietary proteins and fats","Protection from infection, sensation, fluid control, temperature regulation and insulation from trauma","Gas exchange in the lungs, voice production, hearing and the maintenance of balance when walking","Hormone production, blood clotting, protein digestion and the growth and repair of long bones"],"a":1,"e":"These are the skin functions listed."},{"id":"l08-1hk47fl","s":35,"q":"How does a burn differ from a scald?","o":["A burn is caused by liquid heat such as boiling water; a scald is caused by dry heat","A burn affects only the deepest tissues; a scald affects only the superficial layers of skin","A burn is caused by chemical substances; a scald is caused by electrical shock to the skin","A burn is caused by dry heat such as flames; a scald is caused by liquid heat"],"a":3,"e":"Burns come from dry heat sources; scalds from liquid sources."},{"id":"l08-ifnrwh","s":36,"q":"Which pairing of burn type and cause is correct?","o":["Electrical - sunburn; chemical - flames; radiation - electrocution from live wires","Electrical - acids and alkalis; chemical - sunburn; radiation - flames from a fire","Electrical - flames; chemical - radiation exposure; radiation - acids from the lab","Electrical - electrocution; chemical - corrosive agents; radiation - sunburn"],"a":3,"e":"Each burn type has a specific cause."},{"id":"l08-1lhbcd3","s":37,"q":"Which are the categories of burn depth?","o":["First stage, second stage and third stage, graded by the size of the burn","Superficial, partial thickness and full thickness","Mild, moderate and severe, graded by how much pain the patient reports","Thermal, chemical and electrical, graded according to the source of the burn"],"a":1,"e":"Burn depth is classified as superficial, partial thickness or full thickness."},{"id":"l08-1q3flob","s":38,"q":"Which method is best for large burns, and which for burns under 10% BSA?","o":["Rule of palms for large burns; rule of nines for burns under 10%","Rule of nines for large burns; rule of palms for burns under 10%","Rule of nines for both large and small burns","Rule of palms for both large and small burns"],"a":1,"e":"The rule of nines suits large areas and the rule of palms suits small burns."},{"id":"l08-vv6y6n","s":40,"q":"Which systemic complications may follow a burn?","o":["Hypertension from fluid retention in the kidneys and hyperthermia from the heat of the burn","Hypothermia from loss of thermoregulation and hypovolaemia from fluid and protein shift","Hyperglycaemia from the stress response and constipation from prolonged bed rest in hospital","Polycythaemia from dehydration and bradycardia from severe pain in the burned area of skin"],"a":1,"e":"Skin loss impairs temperature control and fluids shift into the burn."},{"id":"l08-oedu4a","s":41,"q":"What is the greatest risk of a burn?","o":["Infection","Hypothermia","Pain","Scarring"],"a":0,"e":"Infection is the greatest risk of a burn."},{"id":"l08-1r06fjd","s":41,"q":"Which groups are at higher risk of physical abuse that may cause burns?","o":["Athletes and adults who are in good health and living independently at home","Only married women and widows who are living alone without family around them","Teenagers and healthcare workers who are exposed to chemicals at their workplace","The elderly, the infirm and the young"],"a":3,"e":"Physical abuse risk is higher in the elderly, infirm or young."},{"id":"l08-q4131s","s":42,"q":"Which signs suggest airway involvement in a burn?","o":["Rash, itching, sneezing and a runny nose that appear soon after the injury","Constipation, nausea and abdominal pain that begin a few hours after the burn","Hoarseness, dysphagia, dyspnoea and adventitious sounds","Joint pain, stiffness and swelling of the legs that develop within the first day"],"a":2,"e":"Hoarseness, swallowing and breathing difficulty and abnormal sounds suggest airway injury."},{"id":"l08-vdlboc","s":43,"q":"Why are burn patients given adequate fluids orally and by the parenteral route?","o":["They are unable to eat solid food after the burn","They need fluids to cool the burnt area from inside","They are at high risk of losing serum from the burn sites","They need fluids to wash out the antibiotics"],"a":2,"e":"Burn patients lose serum from burn sites."},{"id":"l08-1cd8tsa","s":43,"q":"Why is infection prevention vital in burn care?","o":["The skin barrier has been broken and is open to pathogens","The burn site produces too much heat for the body","Burn patients cannot take antibiotics","Infection is not a risk for burns"],"a":0,"e":"Burned skin cannot protect against pathogens."},{"id":"l08-1edln10","s":44,"q":"Which medicines and measures form part of burn management?","o":["Anticoagulants for clots, insulin for sugar and diuretics for swelling","Laxatives for constipation, sedatives for sleep and steroids for strength","Analgesics for pain, antibiotics against infection, balanced diet and debridement surgery if needed","Vaccines for prevention, vitamins only and bed rest only"],"a":2,"e":"These are the drugs, nutrition and surgery measures listed."},{"id":"l08-gdzdtw","s":44,"q":"What is the aim of rehabilitation for the burn patient?","o":["To keep the patient in hospital for longer","To prevent the patient from going home","To resume normal life activities such as work","To reduce the need for any follow-up"],"a":2,"e":"Rehabilitation helps the patient to resume normal activities like work."}]},{"id":"l09","title":"Pain Management in Surgery","emoji":"💊","recap":["Pain is what the patient says it is; 'total pain' has physical, psychological, social, spiritual, emotional and financial parts.","Unrelieved pain causes shallow breathing, delayed walking, a stress response and slower recovery.","Assess intensity, timing, location, quality, meaning and relieving factors with a consistent scale (0 to 10 or faces).","Treat with drug and non-drug methods: pre-emptive, multimodal, around-the-clock analgesia; by mouth, by the clock, by the WHO ladder for chronic pain."],"slides":41,"questions":[{"id":"l09-h3jmbx","s":7,"q":"How is pain defined by McCaffery and Pasero?","o":["A measurable reaction whose existence is proven by changes in pulse and blood pressure","A sensation that exists only when the nurse can see visible signs of discomfort","An experience whose intensity is decided by the nurse using clinical judgement","It is whatever the patient says it is and exists whenever he or she says it does"],"a":3,"e":"Pain is subjective and the patient's report is the standard."},{"id":"l09-uy7r5u","s":7,"q":"Which statement matches the definition of pain by Merskey and Bogduk?","o":["An unpleasant sensory and emotional experience from actual or potential tissue damage","A purely physical reaction of the nerves to an injury, which ends once the tissue has healed","A feeling that occurs only after tissue has been cut or burned by an external physical agent","A mental state that is unrelated to any physical cause, tissue change or underlying disease process"],"a":0,"e":"Pain is a sensory and emotional experience linked to actual or potential tissue damage."},{"id":"l09-1wsq2e4","s":8,"q":"Which components make up the concept of 'total pain'?","o":["Acute, chronic, referred, phantom and visceral components that the patient reports to the nurse","Mild, moderate, severe and unbearable components that are scored by the nurse on a pain scale","Physical, psychological, emotional, social, spiritual and financial components","Cutaneous, deep somatic and visceral components that arise from the tissues of the body"],"a":2,"e":"Total pain includes physical, psychological, emotional, social, spiritual and financial aspects."},{"id":"l09-4yfprd","s":9,"q":"What does social pain involve?","o":["Fear of death, thoughts about life closure, guilt and unresolved questions about the meaning of life","Loss of function from the disease and its treatment, including loss of work, sleep and independence","Challenges from language barriers during hospital care, and insensitivity of staff to the patient's culture","Poor relationships, lack of support, feeling a burden, stigma, and financial or legal issues"],"a":3,"e":"Social pain relates to interpersonal relations and support."},{"id":"l09-15zsbbw","s":9,"q":"What does cultural pain reflect?","o":["Pain that arises from tribal marks and traditional treatments","Pain that is expressed openly in some ethnic groups","Challenges from language barriers and cultural insensitivity","Pain from a lack of money to pay for treatment"],"a":2,"e":"Cultural pain arises from language barriers and insensitivity."},{"id":"l09-1n9pfry","s":10,"q":"Which are natural pain modulators in the body?","o":["Endorphins, dynorphins, enkephalins, serotonin and noradrenaline","Insulin, glucagon, thyroxine and cortisol released by the pancreas and the adrenal glands","Adrenaline, aldosterone, oxytocin and prolactin released by the pituitary and adrenal glands","Histamine, bradykinin, substance P and acetylcholine released from the injured and inflamed tissues"],"a":0,"e":"These are the body's own pain-modulating substances."},{"id":"l09-nv1n3r","s":12,"q":"How does acute pain affect breathing after surgery?","o":["Patients breathe deeply and often, which clears the secretions from the lungs more quickly","Patients breathe shallowly, leading to fatigue and poor lung expansion","Patients breathe faster than usual but gain better oxygen exchange in the lung bases","Patients breathe normally but cough more often, which loosens the secretions in the chest"],"a":1,"e":"Patients in acute pain do not breathe deeply enough."},{"id":"l09-ad31c9","s":12,"q":"How does pain influence early post-operative ambulation?","o":["It encourages walking because the patient tries to move away from the source of the pain","It has no effect on mobility at all, but only changes the patient's mood and appetite","It forces the patient to walk much faster than advised, which increases the risk of falls","It delays ambulation despite the patient's education and will to cooperate"],"a":3,"e":"Pain delays early ambulation."},{"id":"l09-14rhlvb","s":13,"q":"What happens to the body's stress response when pain is poorly controlled?","o":["Insulin levels rise and the metabolic rate drops in order to conserve energy for healing","Cortisol and other stress hormones rise and the metabolic rate increases","Immunity improves because the stress response speeds up the repair of the damaged tissues","Cardiac output rises while all of the stress hormones disappear from the circulating blood"],"a":1,"e":"Stress hormones like cortisol rise and the metabolic rate goes up."},{"id":"l09-pqoz97","s":13,"q":"Poorly managed chronic pain may lead to which problems?","o":["Improved sleep, stronger immunity and a better mood once the patient has adapted to it","Depression, disability, lifestyle changes and lowered immunity","Faster healing of wounds, a shorter hospital stay and fewer visits to the pain clinic","Increased appetite and steady weight gain, with greater energy for daily activities"],"a":1,"e":"Chronic pain may lead to depression, disability and lowered immunity."},{"id":"l09-176dsxw","s":15,"q":"Which is a clinician-related barrier to good pain management?","o":["A stoic attitude that is usually cultural","Opioids not being available for outpatients","Fear of creating addiction or of killing the patient","Worry that treatment will distract the doctor from the disease"],"a":2,"e":"Clinicians may fear addiction or overdose."},{"id":"l09-omm3ly","s":15,"q":"Which is a patient-related barrier to pain management?","o":["A stoic attitude, often cultural, or fear that treatment diverts attention from the disease","Concern about diversion of drugs under health system laws and about regulatory scrutiny of prescribing","Lack of knowledge of the principles of pain treatment among the doctors who write the orders","Opioids not being available at outpatient clinics because of weak supply and storage systems"],"a":0,"e":"Stoicism and fear of diverting the doctor's attention are patient barriers."},{"id":"l09-1yx8hjx","s":17,"q":"Why is the patient's own description of pain so important?","o":["The patient is always exaggerating, so the words must be checked against the nurse's estimate","Vital signs alone give a complete picture, so the patient's description adds no useful detail","The nurse's own estimate is more accurate than the patient's, so the words are only a backup","Pain is subjective, so the patient's words guide how to relieve it"],"a":3,"e":"Because pain is subjective, the patient describes it and the nurse assesses it objectively as far as possible."},{"id":"l09-1d1ndmo","s":18,"q":"Which set lists characteristics of pain to assess?","o":["Pulse, blood pressure, temperature and respiration, recorded only at the start of each shift","Age, sex, occupation, religion and family size, recorded once at the time of admission","Posture, intensity, timing, location, quality, meaning and relieving or aggravating factors","Appetite, sleep, bowel pattern and weight, recorded only at the end of each day on the ward"],"a":2,"e":"These characteristics describe the patient's pain."},{"id":"l09-19hhck9","s":18,"q":"What is meant by the 'quality' of pain?","o":["How severe the pain is when it is scored by the patient on a scale of 0 to 10","How often the pain occurs and at what time of the day it usually begins","Which part of the body feels the pain and where it spreads or radiates to","The patient's description such as burning, crushing or throbbing"],"a":3,"e":"Quality is the patient's description of the pain."},{"id":"l09-1gpc5u9","s":19,"q":"How does a visual analogue pain scale work?","o":["The nurse scores the pain from 0 to 10 by watching the expression on the patient's face","The patient names the drug that has relieved the pain best during earlier episodes of pain","The patient counts the hours since the last analgesic and the nurse converts this into a score","The patient marks intensity from 0 (no pain) to 10 (worst possible pain)"],"a":3,"e":"Patients mark the intensity on a 0 to 10 line."},{"id":"l09-1nabq3v","s":19,"q":"What is the rule for using a pain scale?","o":["Change the scale at every assessment so that the patient does not become bored with it","Use the chosen scale consistently, and give it verbally if the patient cannot write","Use a scale only for patients who can speak English and are able to hold a pen steady","Use the scale only before surgery, since afterwards the patient is too drowsy to respond"],"a":1,"e":"The same scale must be used consistently, verbally if needed."},{"id":"l09-gxc7q1","s":21,"q":"What does the Revised Faces Pain Scale for children describe?","o":["How the child's face looks while the nurse is examining the wound or changing the dressing","How the parent feels about the child's pain and how well the parent is coping at the bedside","How long the pain has lasted and how many times the child has needed analgesia that day","How the child feels inside in relation to pain, not their facial expression"],"a":3,"e":"The scale describes how the child feels, not how the face looks."},{"id":"l09-1ogakic","s":23,"q":"On what does effective pain management rest?","o":["Strong opioid drugs","The surgeon's orders alone","Effective pain assessment","Early discharge from hospital"],"a":2,"e":"Pain management rests on effective assessment."},{"id":"l09-1ifuvvx","s":24,"q":"Which are non-pharmacologic pain measures?","o":["Morphine, pethidine and tramadol given on time and adjusted to how the patient responds","Suppositories, tablets and injections given by the nurse at the prescribed hours of the day","Epidural and patient-controlled analgesia delivered through a pump after the operation","Realistic reassurance, emotional support and massage"],"a":3,"e":"Reassurance, support and massage are non-drug measures."},{"id":"l09-evr1f5","s":25,"q":"Which list contains only non-drug pain relief methods?","o":["Pethidine, diclofenac, tramadol and paracetamol given by mouth, injection or suppository","Epidural, spinal and local anaesthetic injections given by the anaesthetist near the nerves","Antiemetics, antibiotics and antacids that are prescribed together with the main analgesics","Diversional therapy, music, hot or cold applications, exercise and hypnosis"],"a":3,"e":"These are all non-pharmacologic methods."},{"id":"l09-1wy38oa","s":26,"q":"What oral intake rule applies when analgesia is given parenterally after surgery?","o":["Nil per os until bowel sounds return","Sips of water immediately after the injection","A full meal one hour after the injection","Normal diet from the same evening"],"a":0,"e":"The patient stays nil per os until bowel sounds return."},{"id":"l09-1dw3meh","s":26,"q":"Which side effects should be watched for with epidural or patient-controlled analgesia?","o":["Constipation and hunger","Hypertension and headache","Cough and sneezing","Pruritus and vomiting"],"a":3,"e":"Watch for pruritus and vomiting and treat with drugs such as Kytril, promethazine or dexamethasone."},{"id":"l09-4vi3eg","s":27,"q":"Which pairing of route and drug is correct?","o":["Injection - tramadol; suppository - morphine; tablet - pethidine, each given at the same dose","Injection - paracetamol; suppository - tramadol; tablet - morphine, given as prescribed by clock","Injection - pethidine or morphine; suppository - diclofenac or paracetamol; tablet - tramadol","Injection - diclofenac; suppository - pethidine; tablet - morphine, with the dose adjusted by weight"],"a":2,"e":"These are the common analgesics by route."},{"id":"l09-pxcqvs","s":28,"q":"Which analgesic is preferred over diclofenac for asthmatic and ulcer patients?","o":["Aspirin, since it is gentler on the stomach lining than other anti-inflammatory drugs","Ibuprofen, because it has a weaker effect on the airways than diclofenac has","Paracetamol","Ketorolac, because it is a stronger analgesic that acts for a longer time than diclofenac"],"a":2,"e":"Diclofenac is avoided in asthmatics and ulcer patients; paracetamol is better."},{"id":"l09-1nvjo0a","s":28,"q":"What is required for elderly and frail patients on opioids?","o":["Regular assessment","Doubling of the dose","Withholding of the next dose","Discontinuation without review"],"a":0,"e":"They need regular assessment."},{"id":"l09-1toqsxy","s":29,"q":"Which are guidelines for post-operative pain management?","o":["Patient and family education, teamwork, management support and evidence-based recommendations","Treat pain only when the patient asks for it, and record the response once at the end of each shift","Each nurse decides independently on the drug and dose without consulting the rest of the team","Use only the oldest and best-known drugs, because newer approaches have not yet been proven safe"],"a":0,"e":"These are the four guidelines."},{"id":"l09-d8uxnr","s":30,"q":"What is pre-emptive analgesia?","o":["Giving analgesics only after severe pain","Giving analgesics before pain starts","Giving analgesics only at bedtime","Withholding analgesics until the doctor reviews"],"a":1,"e":"Pre-emptive analgesia gives analgesics before pain starts."},{"id":"l09-t4h49d","s":30,"q":"What is multimodal analgesia?","o":["Giving one strong analgesic only","Giving analgesics by injection only","Giving analgesics only for chronic pain","Giving more than one type of analgesic"],"a":3,"e":"Multimodal means using combination analgesia."},{"id":"l09-11v04yn","s":34,"q":"Which principles guide the management of chronic pain?","o":["By injection, as needed, a standard dose for everyone and the strongest drug available","By mouth, by the clock, individualised dose and the analgesic ladder","By suppository, when the patient complains, one fixed dose for all and no use of any ladder","By mouth, as needed, a small starting dose and the largest drug first without using a ladder"],"a":1,"e":"Oral administration, giving by the clock, individualising and using the ladder."},{"id":"l09-wu7rfk","s":35,"q":"When should the dose be increased or the next ladder step used?","o":["If pain is fully controlled and the patient has been comfortable for at least a day","After one week without any review of the patient by the doctor or pharmacist","If pain is not 90% controlled after 24 hours","Only if the patient requests morphine and the family has agreed to the change"],"a":2,"e":"The lecture says increase if not 90% controlled after 24 hours."},{"id":"l09-1psc2lv","s":36,"q":"Which drugs belong on step 1 (mild pain) of the WHO analgesic ladder?","o":["Morphine given by mouth, with or without adjuvants such as antidepressants or steroids","Non-opioids such as aspirin, paracetamol and NSAIDs, with or without adjuvants","Codeine and tramadol, with or without step 1 drugs and adjuvants for neuropathic pain","Corticosteroids and anticonvulsants only, used together with the strong opioid drugs"],"a":1,"e":"Step 1 uses non-opioids."},{"id":"l09-1gjy3lw","s":36,"q":"Which drugs belong on step 3 (severe pain) of the ladder?","o":["Aspirin and paracetamol only, given by mouth at regular times throughout the day","Codeine and tramadol only, with or without non-opioid drugs and adjuvant medicines","Strong opioids such as morphine, with or without step 1 drugs and adjuvants","NSAIDs alone, given at higher doses and at shorter intervals than are normally used"],"a":2,"e":"Step 3 uses strong opioids like morphine."},{"id":"l09-dlj8sm","s":37,"q":"Which are adjuvant analgesics?","o":["Amitriptyline, carbamazepine, phenytoin, dexamethasone and prednisolone","Morphine, codeine and pethidine given together with non-opioid drugs at regular times","Aspirin, paracetamol and ibuprofen given together with an opioid in a fixed combination","Heparin, warfarin and aspirin given together with the analgesics to lower the risk of clots"],"a":0,"e":"Antidepressants, anticonvulsants and corticosteroids act as adjuvants."},{"id":"l09-zjw1uv","s":38,"q":"Which practice is correct for oral morphine?","o":["Keep it open on the ward trolley with the other syrups so that the nurse can reach it quickly at any time","Label the bottle, keep it cool and dark away from children, and use different colours for each strength","Use the same colour for every strength so that all the bottles look alike and are easy to store on one shelf","Share a single bottle between several patients on the ward so that none of the morphine is wasted at all"],"a":1,"e":"These points about oral morphine are listed in the lecture."},{"id":"l09-mi72ga","s":39,"q":"What should be done for a nonverbal patient whose pain behaviour changes after an intervention?","o":["Continue the treatment","Stop treatment and review","Repeat the assessment tomorrow","Double the dose"],"a":0,"e":"If an intervention modifies pain behaviour, continue it."},{"id":"l09-14rh38k","s":39,"q":"What should be done if a nonverbal patient's pain behaviour persists?","o":["Assume the patient is not in pain","Give another dose of the same drug only","Rule out other causes such as delirium, drug side effects or disease and treat appropriately","Ignore it until the patient speaks"],"a":2,"e":"Look for other causes of the behaviour."}]},{"id":"l10","title":"Neoplasm","emoji":"🧬","recap":["Non-neoplastic growth patterns (hypertrophy, hyperplasia, metaplasia, dysplasia) are not cancer but may come before it; anaplasia is the hallmark of cancer.","Benign tumours are encapsulated, slow, expansive and never metastasise; malignant ones infiltrate, recur and spread.","Carcinogenesis has three stages (initiation, promotion, progression) and many causes: chemicals, hormones, viruses, radiation, weak immunity and heredity.","Names follow the tissue: -oma is benign (with exceptions), carcinoma is epithelial, sarcoma is connective tissue; tumours spread directly, by blood and by lymph."],"slides":60,"questions":[{"id":"l10-ypa5xv","s":8,"q":"A weightlifter's muscle fibres grow larger with training. Which growth pattern is this?","o":["Hyperplasia, an increase in the number of cells","Hypertrophy, an increase in the size of cells","Metaplasia, one adult cell type replaced by another","Dysplasia, cells varying in size and shape"],"a":1,"e":"Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size, commonly from increased workload or hormonal stimulation."},{"id":"l10-1k367hz","s":8,"q":"Which situation is a normal physiological example of hyperplasia?","o":["Scar tissue forming over a healed wound","Breast tissue growth during pregnancy","Cells changing type after long irritation","Cells turning abnormal after radiation"],"a":1,"e":"Hyperplasia is a reversible rise in cell number during rapid growth, such as pregnancy or adolescence."},{"id":"l10-iswgza","s":9,"q":"Which statement about metaplasia is correct?","o":["It is reversible if the stimulus is removed but may progress to dysplasia","It is irreversible once the first adult cell type has been replaced by another type","It is always cancerous from the time it first appears in the affected tissue","It is only a rise in the size of the cells present"],"a":0,"e":"Metaplasia can reverse if the stimulus goes, but persistent stimulus may lead to dysplasia."},{"id":"l10-kg3vgf","s":9,"q":"Which site is a common place for metaplasia?","o":["The skeletal muscle of the limbs","The uterine cervix","The heart valve leaflets","The cornea of the eye"],"a":1,"e":"The lecture names the uterine cervix as a common area."},{"id":"l10-rpav99","s":10,"q":"In dysplasia, what happens to the affected cells?","o":["They only increase in size while keeping a normal structure and arrangement","They are replaced by cells of the same adult type after chronic irritation","They stop dividing and are removed by the immune system","They vary in size, shape or organisation, or become less mature"],"a":3,"e":"Dysplasia alters normal adult cells so they vary from their normal size, shape or organisation."},{"id":"l10-10m4pfl","s":10,"q":"Which statement about hyperplasia, metaplasia and dysplasia is correct?","o":["They are all forms of cancer that appear after treatment","They only occur in children and never in adults","They always return to normal once the stimulus goes","They are not neoplastic but may come before cancer"],"a":3,"e":"These three are non-neoplastic but may precede the development of cancer."},{"id":"l10-fjf9ro","s":12,"q":"Which feature is typical of a benign tumour but rare in a malignant one?","o":["It frequently metastasises","It is poorly differentiated","It is usually encapsulated","It recurs frequently"],"a":2,"e":"Benign tumours are usually encapsulated; malignant ones rarely are."},{"id":"l10-dwwzz9","s":12,"q":"Which pairing of tumour type and growth mode is correct?","o":["Malignant - expansive only; benign - infiltrative","Malignant - slow and encapsulated; benign - invasive","Malignant - absent vascularity; benign - marked vascularity","Malignant - infiltrative and expansive; benign - expansive"],"a":3,"e":"Malignant tumours grow by both infiltration and expansion, benign tumours by expansion only."},{"id":"l10-4l57vi","s":12,"q":"How do the cells of a benign tumour compare with its parent cells?","o":["They are fairly normal and similar to the parent cells","They are abnormal, with marked variation in size and shape from the parent cells","They are poorly differentiated and unpredictable in how they grow and behave","They are more primitive than the original tissue and have lost their function"],"a":0,"e":"Benign cells stay fairly normal and resemble the parent cells."},{"id":"l10-vzo6q6","s":13,"q":"What does anaplasia mean?","o":["An irreversible change in which adult cells regress to a more primitive level","A reversible rise in the number of cells in a tissue, usually after a hormonal stimulus","One adult cell type being replaced by another adult type after chronic irritation","A rise in cell size caused by a heavier workload or hormonal stimulation"],"a":0,"e":"Anaplasia means 'without form' and is a hallmark of cancer."},{"id":"l10-3o9h09","s":13,"q":"What happens to the function of anaplastic cells?","o":["They gain extra specialised functions over time","They keep all their original functions unchanged","They produce more mature cells of the same type","They lose the capacity for specialised functions"],"a":3,"e":"Anaplastic cells lose specialised function and become disorganised."},{"id":"l10-1ehbwry","s":14,"q":"Cancer cells that differ greatly in size and shape, some very large and some tiny, show which microscopic property?","o":["Pleomorphism","Aneuploidy","Hyperchromatism","Translocation"],"a":0,"e":"Pleomorphism describes variation in cell size and shape."},{"id":"l10-1xcu5nc","s":14,"q":"Which microscopic property describes unusual numbers of chromosomes?","o":["Pleomorphism","Polymorphism","Aneuploidy","Hyperchromatism"],"a":2,"e":"Aneuploidy is an unusual number of chromosomes."},{"id":"l10-1q92h2t","s":14,"q":"What is seen in hyperchromatism?","o":["The nucleus is larger and varies in its shape from one cell to the next","Cells vary in size and have more than one nucleus in a single cell body","Chromosomes are unusual in number and pattern when they are counted","Nuclear chromatin appears more pronounced on staining"],"a":3,"e":"Hyperchromatism is more pronounced staining of nuclear chromatin."},{"id":"l10-z3dkto","s":15,"q":"What is a translocation in cancer cells?","o":["An exchange of genetic material between two different chromosomes","The loss of a section of a chromosome during cell division","An exchange of material between chromosomes","An extra copy of a whole chromosome in every cell of the body"],"a":2,"e":"Translocation is exchange of material between chromosomes; deletion is loss of sections."},{"id":"l10-132yetw","s":15,"q":"Which pairing of chromosome abnormality and meaning is correct?","o":["Deletion - extra chromosomes in the cell; fragile site - lost section of a chromosome","Deletion - exchange of material between chromosomes; fragile site - extra copy of a chromosome","Deletion - loss of chromosome sections; fragile site - weak section","Deletion - weak section of a chromosome; fragile site - exchange of material between chromosomes"],"a":2,"e":"Deletions are loss of sections and fragile sites are weak sections on chromosomes."},{"id":"l10-vsqmfj","s":16,"q":"Which list shows kinetic properties of cancer cells?","o":["Strong cell adhesion, high differentiation, slow steady growth, and a surrounding capsule","Loss of proliferative control, loss of ability to differentiate, and capacity to metastasise","Contact inhibition, anchorage dependence, normal chromosomes, and controlled division","Stable chromosomes, tight control of division, encapsulation, and a low mitotic rate"],"a":1,"e":"These are the kinetic properties listed in the lecture."},{"id":"l10-7k0z7e","s":17,"q":"What does 'immortality' of transformed cells mean?","o":["Cells can go through unlimited doublings if nutrition and growth factors are available","Cells stop dividing once they have been in contact with their neighbours for some time","Cells die quickly when the growth factors in the surrounding area run out completely","Cells can only divide a fixed number of times before they age and eventually die"],"a":0,"e":"Cancer cells can double an infinite number of times given enough nutrition and growth factors."},{"id":"l10-42r3r9","s":17,"q":"How do normal cells behave when they meet a neighbouring cell?","o":["They stop moving and arrange themselves around each other in an orderly way","They keep moving and pass over the neighbouring cell without any pause","They release enzymes and invade the neighbouring cell wall straight away","They stop moving and arrange themselves around each other"],"a":3,"e":"Normal cells show contact inhibition of movement; cancer cells ignore it."},{"id":"l10-1su6bje","s":18,"q":"What is the effect of decreased contact inhibition of cell division?","o":["Cells continue dividing and may pile on top of one another","Cells stop dividing as soon as the nutrients in the surrounding fluid are used up","Cells divide more slowly as the waste products build up around them in the tissue","Cells divide only when they stay attached to a firm surface and to the matrix"],"a":0,"e":"Cancer cells keep dividing even when surrounded, and can pile up."},{"id":"l10-wuu0m1","s":19,"q":"What may cause cancer cells to be less adhesive?","o":["A gain of extra collagen and elastin","Increased contact with neighbouring cells","Stronger links to the basement membrane","Loss of extracellular fibronectin"],"a":3,"e":"Fibronectin is a glycoprotein that helps cells stick together, so its loss reduces adhesion."},{"id":"l10-ti8ewt","s":20,"q":"What is loss of anchorage dependence?","o":["Cancer cells can grow only when they are firmly attached to the underlying tissue","Cancer cells stop growing once the surface has been covered by a single layer","Cancer cells attach much more strongly to the surrounding cells and matrix","Cancer cells can grow without needing a surface to attach to"],"a":3,"e":"Cancer cells do not need a surface on which to attach and proliferate."},{"id":"l10-1gprtpo","s":20,"q":"What does loss of restrictive point control allow cancer cells to do?","o":["Stop dividing as soon as nutrients start to run low or the cells become crowded","Divide only when growth factors are supplied to them from an outside source","Keep proliferating despite poor nutrition and high cell density","Return to normal function once the density of cells in the tissue is high"],"a":2,"e":"Normal cells are blocked from the cell cycle by such conditions; cancer cells are not."},{"id":"l10-13qhq3l","s":22,"q":"What is carcinogenesis?","o":["The spread of cancer cells from one organ to another through blood and lymph","The surgical removal of a tumour followed by a course of chemotherapy","The grading of a tumour by the stage of its spread through the body","The process by which normal cells are changed into cancer cells"],"a":3,"e":"Carcinogenesis is the transformation of normal cells into cancer cells."},{"id":"l10-17m84k7","s":22,"q":"What does the lecture say about the exact cause of most human cancers?","o":["It is known and caused by one agent in all cases","It is still unknown and no single theory is accepted","It is always inherited from the parents of the patient","It is always caused by viruses in the tissues affected"],"a":1,"e":"No single unifying hypothesis has been accepted."},{"id":"l10-11ow4tg","s":23,"q":"What does an initiating agent do?","o":["Alters how the genetic information of a cell is expressed","Blocks the action of the agents that promote cancer","Permanently and irreversibly changes the DNA of a cell","Destroys carcinogens by stimulating cell metabolism"],"a":2,"e":"An initiating agent is a carcinogen that changes the DNA permanently."},{"id":"l10-6lsjbg","s":23,"q":"Which agent alters expression of genetic information and so enhances cell transformation?","o":["An initiating agent (carcinogen)","A reversing agent such as a vitamin","A promoting agent (cocarcinogen)","A complete carcinogen with no promoter"],"a":2,"e":"Hormones, plant products and drugs may act as promoters."},{"id":"l10-1fflxnr","s":23,"q":"What is a complete carcinogen?","o":["One that only changes the DNA of the cell but cannot support growth","One with both initiating and promoting properties","One that only promotes growth in cells that have already been changed","One that blocks the action of other carcinogens inside the cell"],"a":1,"e":"A complete carcinogen has both properties."},{"id":"l10-1y2v4my","s":24,"q":"Which statement about a reversing agent is correct?","o":["It inhibits promoting agents; examples include drugs, enzymes and vitamins","It inhibits initiating agents; examples include drugs, enzymes and antibodies","It permanently alters the DNA of the cell; examples include drugs and radiation","It speeds up the growth of cancer cells; examples include hormones and toxins"],"a":0,"e":"Reversing agents inhibit the effect of promoters."},{"id":"l10-1olya8h","s":24,"q":"What is an oncogene?","o":["A gene that is found only in cells after they have become cancerous in the body","A gene that destroys carcinogens as they enter the cell and protects the DNA","A gene that is inherited only through the mother and is never acquired later","A gene that controls growth and repair of tissues, acting as on and off signals"],"a":3,"e":"Oncogenes send the 'on' and 'off' signals that control reproduction."},{"id":"l10-1r8owlw","s":25,"q":"What is heterogeneity in a tumour?","o":["Similarity among all cells within the tumour mass","The speed at which the tumour spreads to distant sites","Differences among individual cells within the tumour","The presence of a capsule around the tumour"],"a":2,"e":"Heterogeneity refers to differences among individual cells in a tumour."},{"id":"l10-1l8zayq","s":25,"q":"Why does progression occur in a tumour?","o":["A cell type grows or metabolises faster than the others in the mass","All cells in the mass slow down as they become crowded and short of nutrients","The immune system removes the faster growing cells and leaves the slower ones","The capsule around the tumour thickens and stops any further growth of the mass"],"a":0,"e":"Faster growing cell types change the microscopic structure as the tumour proliferates."},{"id":"l10-1de6sdn","s":26,"q":"What is transformation?","o":["A single step in which cells become mature after repeated exposure to a promoter","A multistep process by which cells become progressively dedifferentiated after an initiating agent","A process in which cancer cells slowly become normal again once the carcinogen is gone","A process in which cancer cells move through the lymph to reach distant organs"],"a":1,"e":"Transformation is the multistep process of progressive dedifferentiation."},{"id":"l10-47wot2","s":27,"q":"Berenblum's theory of carcinogenesis has evolved into what?","o":["A three-stage theory in which a normal cell becomes a cancer cell through changes in its DNA","A two-stage theory in which a normal cell is changed by viruses and then by hormones","A single-step theory in which one carcinogen turns a normal cell into a cancer cell at once","A four-stage theory in which immune cells first fail and then the tumour spreads to other organs"],"a":0,"e":"Berenblum's theory evolved into the three-stage theory: three distinct phases, with several substages, all occurring in the cell's DNA."},{"id":"l10-168ti0o","s":28,"q":"In Berenblum's three-stage theory, what happens in the initiation stage?","o":["A cocarcinogen causes reversible damage to the proliferation mechanism of the cell","Cells become invasive, resist drugs and begin to metastasise to other organs","A carcinogen damages DNA by altering a specific gene","The immune system recognises the cell as foreign and destroys it at once"],"a":2,"e":"Initiation is DNA damage by a carcinogen; the gene may repair, change permanently or transform."},{"id":"l10-179cqzr","s":28,"q":"After initiation, a gene may be permanently changed but cause no cancer. When can cancer then arise?","o":["If the cell is later exposed to vitamin C or to another protective nutrient","If the cell is later exposed to a cocarcinogen","If the cell is repaired by the immune system and then returns to its former state","If the cell is surrounded by normal cells that keep its growth in check"],"a":1,"e":"A permanently changed gene may need a later cocarcinogen to cause cancer."},{"id":"l10-1kv34ze","s":29,"q":"What happens in the promotion stage?","o":["Cocarcinogens cause reversible or irreversible damage to the proliferation mechanism","Carcinogens change a gene directly by damaging the DNA of the cell nucleus","Cells gain the ability to invade tissue and to resist drugs given to treat them","Cells are repaired and return to their former state once the stimulus ends"],"a":0,"e":"Irreversible damage in promotion results in cancer cell transformation."},{"id":"l10-12zk6w","s":29,"q":"Which factors may inhibit the effect of cocarcinogens?","o":["High hormone levels, heavy drinking, and a long period of exposure to the agent","Chronic irritation, malnutrition, and repeated exposure to radiation over years","Vitamin C, an effective immune system, and limited time or dose of exposure","A weak immune system, advancing age, and a high dose of exposure to the agent"],"a":2,"e":"Reversing agents and host factors such as immunity can inhibit cocarcinogens."},{"id":"l10-1ugbx49","s":30,"q":"What characterises the progression stage?","o":["Reversible DNA damage that the cell is able to repair by itself without help","Exposure to a cocarcinogen with no lasting change in the cell or its behaviour","Return of the cells to normal growth after the stimulus has been removed","Morphologic change and more malignant behaviour such as invasion and metastasis; it is irreversible"],"a":3,"e":"Progression is irreversible and leads to invasion, metastasis and drug resistance."},{"id":"l10-13woh5p","s":31,"q":"Which four cancers occur mostly in hormone-responsive tissue?","o":["Skin, liver, bone, lung and other tissues that do not respond to hormones","Colon, stomach, kidney and bladder, which depend on hormone signals to grow","Cervix, thyroid, eye and tongue, where hormones control cell growth","Prostate, brain, breast and endometrium"],"a":3,"e":"Those four cancers occur in target tissue for hormones."},{"id":"l10-1rpqj4v","s":32,"q":"Which group of workers was the first to show a link between chemicals and cancer?","o":["Miners, who showed a high rate of lung cancer after working underground","Chimney sweeps, with a high rate of scrotal cancer","Farmers, who showed a high rate of skin cancer after years in the sun","Dyers, who showed a high rate of bladder cancer after handling dyes"],"a":1,"e":"The link was first documented in chimney sweeps."},{"id":"l10-ovlpx1","s":33,"q":"What does 'sufficient evidence of carcinogenicity' mean?","o":["A link is seen between exposure and cancer but chance and bias cannot be ruled out","Research is too weak or inconsistent in design to allow any firm conclusion","Several good studies across all exposure levels show no association with cancer","A causal relationship is established and chance, bias and confounding are ruled out with reasonable confidence"],"a":3,"e":"This is the strongest category of evidence."},{"id":"l10-1mfh51u","s":33,"q":"Which category means a positive association is seen but chance, bias or confounding cannot be ruled out?","o":["Limited evidence","Sufficient evidence","Inadequate evidence","Evidence of lack of carcinogenicity"],"a":0,"e":"Limited evidence has a credible but not firmly proven link."},{"id":"l10-8wkvu","s":34,"q":"Which category applies when research is of poor quality or no human data exist?","o":["Inadequate evidence of carcinogenicity","Limited evidence of carcinogenicity","Sufficient evidence of carcinogenicity","Evidence suggesting lack of carcinogenicity"],"a":0,"e":"Inadequate evidence means studies cannot support a conclusion."},{"id":"l10-1rv8hnz","s":35,"q":"How are viruses thought to contribute to cancer?","o":["By producing chemicals that directly destroy the immune system of the host","By infecting host DNA, causing proto-oncogenic changes and cell mutation","By blocking the blood vessels that supply the normal tissues of the body","By releasing hormones that raise the rate of cell growth in target tissue"],"a":1,"e":"Viruses infect host DNA, leading to changes and mutation."},{"id":"l10-v9tofn","s":36,"q":"Which virus has a firmly established link with hepatocellular cancer?","o":["Herpes simplex type 2 virus","Hepatitis B virus","Cytomegalovirus","Human T-cell lymphotropic virus"],"a":1,"e":"The HBV-hepatocellular cancer association is firmly established."},{"id":"l10-1yx4a1s","s":36,"q":"Cytomegalovirus has been linked to which cancer?","o":["Cervical cancer","Hepatocellular cancer","Kaposi's sarcoma","Retinoblastoma"],"a":2,"e":"CMV has been linked to Kaposi's sarcoma; HSV-2 is believed linked to cervical cancer."},{"id":"l10-11v2qyj","s":37,"q":"What did the first documented evidence of radiation carcinogenesis show?","o":["Lung cancer in mining workers after long exposure to smoke and dust underground","Skin cancer after chronic exposure to radioactive chemicals and x-rays","Liver cancer after exposure to viruses carried in contaminated food supplies","Breast cancer after long exposure to hormones given as medicines"],"a":1,"e":"Skin cancer after chronic exposure to radioactive materials was first documented."},{"id":"l10-8mqxfz","s":38,"q":"How does radiation appear to start carcinogenesis?","o":["By raising hormone levels in the tissues of the body over many years","By damaging susceptible DNA through strand breaks or cross-linking","By infecting the cells with viral genetic material that alters their growth","By blocking the immune cells that normally remove abnormal cells from tissues"],"a":1,"e":"Radiation causes single and double strand breaks or cross-linking in DNA."},{"id":"l10-qrvegg","s":38,"q":"Which is a source of ionising radiation exposure?","o":["Mobile phone use for long periods","Infrared lamps used for warming","Diagnostic or therapeutic x-rays","Ordinary household electric lighting"],"a":2,"e":"Sources include radioactive ground minerals, x-rays and radioisotopes."},{"id":"l10-18ydfyy","s":39,"q":"What is the normal role of the immune system in relation to cancer?","o":["It screens and eliminates potential cancer cells before a tumour forms","It causes the cancer cells to grow faster in tissues that are chronically inflamed","It only acts after a tumour has already spread to distant sites in the body","It plays no part in the control of abnormal cells before a tumour forms"],"a":0,"e":"Potentially cancerous cells arise constantly but are removed by the immune system."},{"id":"l10-1ef38qp","s":40,"q":"When does cancer arise, according to the immune theory?","o":["When the immune system is very strong and starts to attack the normal cells","When the person has been vaccinated against several different viruses","When the person is young and has no chronic illness or other stress","When the immune system is overwhelmed, as in malnutrition, chronic disease, age and stress"],"a":3,"e":"Cancer should arise only when immunity is overwhelmed."},{"id":"l10-1yy0766","s":41,"q":"What are the main means used to identify families with hereditary cancer syndrome?","o":["Clinical data and family history","Routine blood sugar tests","Chest x-ray of all family members","Examination of the nails and hair"],"a":0,"e":"Many gene locations are unknown, so history is the primary tool."},{"id":"l10-2vm08x","s":43,"q":"What does the suffix '-oma' usually indicate?","o":["A malignant tumour of the connective tissue only, as in sarcoma","A tumour that has already spread to other organs through the blood","An inflammation of the tissue named by the prefix, as in fibroma","A benign tumour when it follows a tissue prefix, as in fibroma"],"a":3,"e":"Benign tumours usually end in -oma, for example fibroma and adenoma."},{"id":"l10-vc9x3w","s":44,"q":"Which two tumours have names ending in -oma but are malignant?","o":["Fibroma and adenoma, which are both benign despite sounding similar","Lipoma and osteoma, which both arise from connective tissue","Melanoma and hepatoma","Chondroma and myoma, which both arise from supporting tissue"],"a":2,"e":"Melanomas and hepatomas are exceptions to the naming rule."},{"id":"l10-1h8d0n","s":45,"q":"Which root names malignant tumours of epithelial origin?","o":["Sarc, meaning flesh, which names tumours of the connective tissue","Carcin, meaning crab-like","Blast, meaning embryonic, which names tumours of immature cells","Oma, meaning tumour, which names most benign growths"],"a":1,"e":"Carcinomas arise from epithelium, sarcomas from connective tissue."},{"id":"l10-tr4jz8","s":46,"q":"A tumour of malignant fat cells is called what?","o":["Osteosarcoma","Chondrosarcoma","Liposarcoma","Rhabdomyosarcoma"],"a":2,"e":"Lipo means fat."},{"id":"l10-1hkmvbr","s":46,"q":"Which pairing of sarcoma and tissue of origin is correct?","o":["Rhabdo - smooth muscle; leiomyo - skeletal muscle","Rhabdo - skeletal muscle; leiomyo - smooth muscle","Rhabdo - cartilage; leiomyo - bone","Rhabdo - bone; leiomyo - fat"],"a":1,"e":"Rhabdomyosarcoma is from skeletal muscle and leiomyosarcoma from smooth muscle."},{"id":"l10-13r2xre","s":47,"q":"Which suffix is used for tumours that appear to come from embryonal tissue?","o":["Blastoma","Sarcoma","Carcinoma","Teratoma"],"a":0,"e":"Neuroblastoma and retinoblastoma are examples."},{"id":"l10-e0kjbs","s":47,"q":"Which tumour is a mixed tumour arising from the three germ layers?","o":["Retinoblastoma","Hepatoblastoma","Teratoma","Nephroblastoma"],"a":2,"e":"Teratomas arise from totipotential germ cells and may be benign or malignant."},{"id":"l10-1rtcq0y","s":48,"q":"What is leukaemia?","o":["A diffuse cancer of the blood system with abnormal proliferation of white cell precursors","A solid tumour of the bone marrow that grows and stays in one place in the marrow","A benign growth of red blood cells in the spleen that rarely spreads","A cancer of the lymph nodes that does not enter the blood circulation"],"a":0,"e":"Leukaemia is a diffuse rather than solid tumour."},{"id":"l10-1hfw905","s":49,"q":"How does acute leukaemia differ from chronic leukaemia?","o":["Acute shows mature white cells; chronic shows primitive cells","Acute affects bone marrow only; chronic affects lymph only","Acute is a solid tumour; chronic is a diffuse tumour","Acute shows primitive white cells; chronic shows mature cells"],"a":3,"e":"The level of maturity of the predominant cell separates acute and chronic forms."},{"id":"l10-13gfqmi","s":49,"q":"Which prefix describes leukaemia of lymphoid origin?","o":["Lympho","Myelo","Granulo","Blast"],"a":0,"e":"Lympho refers to lymphoid, myelo or granulo to myeloid origin."},{"id":"l10-1iu3mbh","s":51,"q":"What does tumour angiogenesis factor do?","o":["Stops blood vessels from reaching the tumour and so slows its growth and spread","Breaks down the tissue barriers around the tumour cells so they can invade","Helps cancer cells to attach to the surface of organs far from the primary site","Stimulates new capillary formation so the tumour grows and invades faster"],"a":3,"e":"Once vascularised, the tumour grows faster and invades local tissue more easily."},{"id":"l10-72on6n","s":51,"q":"How does rapid tumour growth help direct invasion?","o":["It reduces pressure so that cells fall away from the mass into nearby tissue","It creates pressure that forces finger-like projections into nearby tissue","It makes the capsule thicker and so stops the spread to nearby structures","It stops cell division in the centre of the tumour and shrinks the mass"],"a":1,"e":"Intratumour pressure pushes projections of cells along natural fracture lines."},{"id":"l10-1u15j43","s":52,"q":"Which tumour-related factors promote direct spread?","o":["Strong cell adhesion, slow movement, thick tissue barriers and low enzyme levels","Cell motility, loss of adhesiveness and enzymes such as plasminogen activator","Low enzyme levels, tight adhesion, encapsulation and a fixed cell position","Fixed cell position, high contact inhibition, small tumour size and slow growth"],"a":1,"e":"Motility, slipperiness and enzymes that destroy tissue barriers all help invasion."},{"id":"l10-yw3k3w","s":53,"q":"What is serosal seeding?","o":["Tumour cells travel in veins to a distant organ and grow there as new tumours","Tumour cells enter the lymph channels that drain the primary tumour site","Tumour cells enter a body cavity and attach to the surface of organs there","Tumour cells spread to the skin from a surgical instrument during an operation"],"a":2,"e":"Serosal seeding happens commonly with lung and ovarian tumours."},{"id":"l10-1n4uqgq","s":54,"q":"How can surgery itself spread cancer cells?","o":["Biopsy or manipulation of a tumour may contaminate normal tissue or release cells into the blood","Anaesthesia makes the cancer cells more active and mobile in the surrounding tissue","Antibiotics given before surgery help the tumour cells to spread more easily","Sutures used to close the wound cause cancer cells to grow along the scar"],"a":0,"e":"Instrumentation provides a direct pathway for spread."},{"id":"l10-1w47vrd","s":55,"q":"What does the term 'metastasis' describe?","o":["Growth of a tumour into the tissue next to the primary site by direct extension","Return of the same tumour to its original site after it has been removed","Change of a benign tumour into a normal tissue as the cells mature again","Release of cells from the primary site and their attachment at distant sites"],"a":3,"e":"Metastasis comes from 'meta', meaning change, and means distant spread."},{"id":"l10-1eakjmq","s":56,"q":"Which sequence correctly starts the metastatic process by blood?","o":["Growth of the primary tumour, angiogenesis, detachment, circulation, then arrest on vessel lining","Circulation, detachment, arrest on vessel lining, angiogenesis, then primary growth","Detachment, arrest on vessel lining, primary growth, circulation, then angiogenesis","Angiogenesis, arrest on vessel lining, circulation, detachment, then primary growth"],"a":0,"e":"The lecture lists these steps in order."},{"id":"l10-1iu0wwb","s":57,"q":"What is site predilection?","o":["All tumour cells settle in the first organ they reach after leaving the primary site","Certain tumour cells have an affinity for specific organs","Tumour cells survive in any organ with equal ease whatever its blood supply","Tumour cells settle only in the organ they came from and never in others"],"a":1,"e":"Site and survival of tumour cells depend on their own unique properties."},{"id":"l10-sp5ie7","s":58,"q":"Which statement about lymphatic spread is correct?","o":["Cells enter arteries only, and the mechanism is already well understood","Cells penetrate lymphatic channels draining the site; this is less understood than blood spread","Cells spread through the skin surface to reach nearby organs and lymph nodes","Cells spread along nerves to reach the brain and no other organ"],"a":1,"e":"Much less is known about lymphatic than haematogenous spread."},{"id":"l10-1fbrgny","s":58,"q":"What does 'metastases from metastasis' mean?","o":["A secondary implant may release cells into the circulation and form tertiary implants","A primary tumour is removed and the secondary tumours then disappear on their own","A secondary tumour turns into a benign tumour over time and stops growing","A metastasis can only be formed by the first primary tumour and never by a secondary"],"a":0,"e":"Secondary implants have new blood vessels and can shed cells too."},{"id":"l10-mmi81l","s":58,"q":"The lecture notes that metastatic cells can lie dormant. What example is given?","o":["Lung cancer cells found in the liver after a dormant period of about 2 years","Colon cancer cells found in the brain after a dormant period of about 5 years","Skin cancer cells found in the bone after a dormant period of about 10 years","Breast cancer cells in the vertebrae after more than 30 years"],"a":3,"e":"Dormancy of metastatic cells for years is given, breast to vertebrae over 30 years."}]},{"id":"l11","title":"Nursing Management of Cancer","emoji":"🎗️","recap":["Common cancers in Ghana include breast, cervical, prostate, colorectal, head and neck, skin, retinoblastoma, Wilms' tumour, Burkitt's lymphoma and Kaposi sarcoma.","Treatment is medical (chemotherapy, hormonal, radiation), surgical (diagnostic, curative, palliative, prophylactic) and nursing.","Nursing care covers psychological care, assessment, hygiene, nutrition, elimination, safe drug handling, education and home visits for palliation.","Survivorship care covers prevention, surveillance, intervention for effects of treatment, and coordination with other providers."],"slides":34,"questions":[{"id":"l11-1tffevs","s":7,"q":"Which of these cancers is listed as common in Ghana?","o":["Pancreatic adenocarcinoma","Thyroid papillary carcinoma","Burkitt's lymphoma","Bladder transitional carcinoma"],"a":2,"e":"Burkitt's lymphoma is on the lecture's list, along with Kaposi sarcoma, Wilms' tumour and retinoblastoma."},{"id":"l11-mjwtd7","s":7,"q":"Which list contains only cancers named as common in Ghana?","o":["Brain, bone, ovarian, gastric and skin cancers of the common types","Breast, cervical, prostate and colorectal","Liver, pancreatic, thyroid, bladder and nasopharyngeal cancers","Lung, oesophageal, testicular, renal and laryngeal cancers"],"a":1,"e":"Breast, cervical, prostate and colorectal cancers are on the lecture's list."},{"id":"l11-i90da","s":9,"q":"What are the three medical treatments for cancer in the lecture?","o":["Chemotherapy, hormonal therapy and radiation therapy","Chemotherapy, surgery and physiotherapy given in a rehabilitation unit","Radiation therapy, diet therapy and prayer provided at the bedside","Hormonal therapy, surgery and nursing care given on the ward"],"a":0,"e":"The medical modalities are chemotherapy, hormonal and radiation therapy."},{"id":"l11-18zai21","s":9,"q":"A surgeon takes a biopsy to confirm the diagnosis of cancer. What type of surgery is this?","o":["Curative","Palliative","Prophylactic","Diagnostic"],"a":3,"e":"Diagnostic surgery takes samples to confirm the diagnosis."},{"id":"l11-1buta2f","s":9,"q":"Surgery is done to relieve nerve compression by a tumour. Which type is this?","o":["Curative","Palliative","Diagnostic","Prophylactic"],"a":1,"e":"Palliative surgery prevents suffering, for example from nerve compression."},{"id":"l11-sfuvtb","s":9,"q":"Which surgery removes tissue that is at risk of developing cancer?","o":["Curative surgery to remove the tumour","Prophylactic","Palliative surgery to relieve symptoms","Diagnostic surgery to obtain a tissue sample"],"a":1,"e":"Prophylactic surgery removes at-risk tissue, for example the breast."},{"id":"l11-rdfl8","s":11,"q":"Which list gives areas of general nursing care for cancer patients?","o":["Psychological care, assessment, diet, physiotherapy, surgery and radiology, with no education or home visit","Hygiene, nutrition, surgery, radiology, counselling and discharge planning, without drug administration","Psychological care, assessment, hygiene, nutrition, elimination, drug administration, education and home visit","Assessment, medication, surgery, discharge and follow-up only, with no psychological care"],"a":2,"e":"These are the eight areas listed in the lecture."},{"id":"l11-ak24nj","s":13,"q":"How should the nurse deal with a cancer patient's worries about the prognosis and treatment?","o":["Avoid the topic and talk about the patient's usual daily routine instead","Allow the patient to express worries and fears, and give reassurance","Tell the patient to speak only with the doctor about such worries","Give brief answers and move on to the next patient on the ward round"],"a":1,"e":"Psychological care includes letting patients express worries about condition, prognosis and treatment."},{"id":"l11-9e5z80","s":13,"q":"Which approach is part of psychological care of a cancer patient?","o":["Limited information on the treatment so that the patient is not frightened","Information given only to the relatives of the patient during visiting hours","Education on the condition and on the treatment at all stages","Education given only after the treatment has been completed in full"],"a":2,"e":"Education on the condition and on the available treatment at all stages is included."},{"id":"l11-142l3ml","s":14,"q":"Which activity helps cancer patients cope according to the lecture?","o":["Staying away from other patients with cancer in order to avoid added stress","Avoiding any discussion about spiritual needs while in the ward","Receiving exactly the same routine care as every other patient on the ward","Meeting other survivors to share experiences and joining cancer groups"],"a":3,"e":"Survivor meetings and cancer associations support coping."},{"id":"l11-1m0fr7k","s":14,"q":"How should care for cancer patients be planned?","o":["It follows one standard routine to treat patients equally","It is based on the nurse's own preferences for the patient","It is individualised and shows respect for every client","It is decided by the family of the patient alone"],"a":2,"e":"Individualised care and respect for all clients are listed."},{"id":"l11-1im4r","s":15,"q":"Which laboratory tests are part of the assessment of a cancer patient?","o":["Urine pregnancy test, blood glucose, sputum culture and a throat swab","Full blood count, urea and creatinine, and liver function tests","Stool culture, throat swab, skin scrapings and a urine dipstick test","Chest x-ray, ECG, eye examination and a hearing test only"],"a":1,"e":"Haematology and biochemistry tests are listed."},{"id":"l11-1bw5au9","s":15,"q":"What body measures show the nutritional status of a cancer patient?","o":["Skin colour and hair length","Height and shoe size","Pulse and respiratory rate","Weight and body mass index"],"a":3,"e":"Weight and BMI are used for nutritional assessment."},{"id":"l11-k2a7ij","s":16,"q":"Which group of findings is assessed in a patient receiving cancer treatment?","o":["Tumour colour, food preferences, hair length, shoe size, the number of visitors and the bed position","Tumour size, response and side effects of treatment, sleep, pain, infection and metastasis","Appetite and body weight only, with sleep, pain, infection and metastasis left out of the check","Visitors, ward routine, bed position, meal times and the weather in the ward"],"a":1,"e":"These are assessment items on the slide."},{"id":"l11-13zzs56","s":16,"q":"Why must the nurse assess for side effects of treatment?","o":["Side effects can be immediate or long term and need to be managed","Side effects only occur after the treatment has ended, so they cannot be seen earlier","Side effects always mean the treatment has failed and must be stopped","Side effects are only a concern in children and the very elderly"],"a":0,"e":"The lecture lists immediate and long-term side effects."},{"id":"l11-132iw6q","s":17,"q":"Which hygiene need is specific to patients on radiotherapy?","o":["Skin care during radiotherapy","Hair care for alopecia during chemotherapy","Colostomy care after bowel surgery","Nail care for the hands and feet"],"a":0,"e":"Skin care during radiotherapy is listed under personal hygiene."},{"id":"l11-t9g62h","s":17,"q":"Alopecia in a cancer patient refers to what?","o":["Mouth ulcers","Skin burns","Nail loss","Hair loss"],"a":3,"e":"Alopecia is hair loss and appears under hair care."},{"id":"l11-1591j08","s":18,"q":"Which advice on nutrition is given to cancer patients?","o":["Avoid fruits and vegetables, and drink as little water as possible each day","Eat only soft foods and avoid all fluids at mealtimes to prevent nausea","Eat fruits, vegetables and roughage and drink plenty of water","Eat fatty foods and avoid fibre in the diet to protect the bowel lining"],"a":2,"e":"The lecture advises healthy diets, roughage and copious fluids."},{"id":"l11-yttbgs","s":18,"q":"When may parenteral nutrition be needed for a cancer patient?","o":["When the patient is eating a balanced diet and drinking enough fluids","When the patient has just started chemotherapy and feels well enough","When the patient cannot meet nutritional needs by eating","When the patient has completed treatment and has returned to normal eating"],"a":2,"e":"Parenteral nutrition is listed as an option under nutrition."},{"id":"l11-1kr2nga","s":19,"q":"Which problem is looked for when assessing the bowel pattern of a cancer patient?","o":["Diarrhoea, constipation and changes in stool colour, odour and amount","Bleeding gums, mouth sores, dry skin and hair loss","Headache, dizziness, blurred vision and ringing in the ears","Cough, wheeze, chest pain and shortness of breath"],"a":0,"e":"Bowel pattern and stool characteristics are assessed."},{"id":"l11-ssq209","s":20,"q":"Which pair are examples of cytotoxic drugs mentioned in the lecture?","o":["Paracetamol and tramadol, which are analgesics","Heparin and aspirin, which are anticoagulants","Morphine and pethidine, which are opioids","Cyclophosphamide and cisplatin"],"a":3,"e":"Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent, cisplatin a platinum compound."},{"id":"l11-1lg6ia6","s":20,"q":"Which is a precaution before giving chemotherapy?","o":["Read the patient's notes and ensure the patient has eaten","Give the drug on an empty stomach, whether or not the patient has eaten, for better absorption","Skip the notes if the patient has had the same drug before without problems","Give the full dose quickly in order to shorten the time of exposure to it"],"a":0,"e":"The lecture advises reading the notes and making sure the patient eats first."},{"id":"l11-rpmg35","s":21,"q":"Which equipment protects the nurse when handling chemotherapy?","o":["Plain cotton gloves only","A cap and shoe covers only","A mask only with no gloves","Face shields and gloves"],"a":3,"e":"The slide shows safe handling with face shields and gloves."},{"id":"l11-1z08lwq","s":21,"q":"How should chemotherapy be given?","o":["Slowly and patiently at the specified infusion or bolus rate","Quickly, to reduce the time the patient is exposed to the drug and its effects","At any rate that suits the schedule of the ward on that particular shift","In one dose regardless of the infusion or bolus rate stated for the drug"],"a":0,"e":"Drugs are given according to the specified time or rate."},{"id":"l11-14yfk5m","s":22,"q":"What should the nurse watch for during and after chemotherapy?","o":["Immediate drug reactions","Only delayed side effects after one week","Only changes in the weight of the patient","Visitors who come with the patient"],"a":0,"e":"Observing for immediate drug reaction is stated."},{"id":"l11-1r1kta5","s":23,"q":"Which topics are covered in education and counselling of cancer patients and relatives?","o":["Surgery techniques, anaesthesia, theatre routine and recovery room care","Hospital finances, insurance claims, bed charges and payment plans","Ward layout, visiting times, parking rules and the hospital canteen","The condition, treatment, nutrition and personal hygiene"],"a":3,"e":"These topics are listed on the slide."},{"id":"l11-16tclxr","s":24,"q":"What else should the patient and family be taught?","o":["How the family may change the drug doses at home when symptoms improve","Follow-ups, signs of infection and metastasis, stress management, and social and employment issues","How to read laboratory results by themselves and decide on the treatment","How to give the chemotherapy at home to the patient without supervision"],"a":1,"e":"Education includes follow-up, signs of infection and metastasis, stress, social and employment issues."},{"id":"l11-16hzmxh","s":25,"q":"Which patients mostly receive home visits?","o":["Patients who are newly diagnosed and fit to walk","Patients who have completed treatment and are well","Patients who missed their last clinic review","Patients with end-stage cancer needing palliation"],"a":3,"e":"Home visits are mostly for end-staged cancers."},{"id":"l11-1gesrk8","s":25,"q":"How often are home visits made?","o":["Once a year for every patient who is stable, as soon as the condition settles","Daily for all patients in the community, regardless of the condition","About two weekly, as the condition demands, or when the patient calls","Only when the doctor requests a visit in writing for that patient"],"a":2,"e":"The lecture says two weekly or as the condition demands, and on call."},{"id":"l11-pie1xp","s":27,"q":"How is cancer survivorship defined?","o":["The first phase of care, from the time of diagnosis up to the surgery","The period of chemotherapy and radiation therapy only, while treatment goes on","The period after a patient is declared completely cured and discharged","The phase of care after primary treatment until recurrence or end of life"],"a":3,"e":"Survivorship follows primary treatment and lasts until recurrence or end of life."},{"id":"l11-14w4wt8","s":30,"q":"Why is survivorship care needed?","o":["Cancer cells always return in the first month after treatment, so close care is required","Survivors cannot be cared for by primary care providers in the community","Short and long-term and late effects of cancer and its treatment may have many physical and psychosocial effects","Survivors no longer need any contact with a nurse or a doctor after treatment"],"a":2,"e":"Effects of cancer and its treatment need to be acknowledged, planned for and addressed."},{"id":"l11-j43tjz","s":31,"q":"Which are the first two components of survivorship care suggested by the American Cancer Society?","o":["Intervention for treatment effects, and coordination of care among providers","Prevention and detection of new cancer, and surveillance for recurrence","Surgery for the tumour, and chemotherapy for the cancer cells that remain","Counselling for the family, and discharge from the clinic with a letter"],"a":1,"e":"The first two are prevention and detection, and surveillance."},{"id":"l11-h2lgnc","s":31,"q":"Which test is used for surveillance after prostate cancer?","o":["Papanicolaou smear of the cervix","Mammography of the breast tissue","Prostate specific antigen","Colonoscopy of the large bowel"],"a":2,"e":"PSA is used after prostate cancer; mammography after breast cancer."},{"id":"l11-cq1j04","s":32,"q":"Which is an example of 'intervention for consequences of cancer and its treatments'?","o":["Pap smears of the cervix","Smoking cessation counselling","Lymphoedema therapy","Colonoscopy of the large bowel"],"a":2,"e":"Lymphoedema therapy, pain management and fertility care are examples."},{"id":"l11-1720ckw","s":32,"q":"What does coordination between specialists and primary care providers address?","o":["Only the surgical wound of the survivor and how well it has healed","Only the cost of treatment for the survivor and the family at home","Health needs such as comorbid hypertension and diabetes","Only the screening of the family members of the survivor for cancer"],"a":2,"e":"Coordination meets needs such as care for comorbidities."},{"id":"l11-1yob8bo","s":32,"q":"What is the nurse's role in survivorship care?","o":["Leave the survivorship planning entirely to the oncologist in the hospital","Discharge the survivor without any plan or follow-up arrangement at all","Help develop the survivorship care plan and educate and care for survivors","Only give the drugs that have been prescribed for the survivor by the doctor"],"a":2,"e":"The nurse assists in developing the plan and provides education and care."}]},{"id":"l12","title":"Ethical and Legal Issues in Surgical Care","emoji":"⚖️","recap":["Surgery is lawful only because the team intends treatment, not harm; ethics (code of conduct) and law (rules with penalties) govern what the team may do.","Seven principles: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, veracity, fidelity, justice and paternalism.","Informed consent comes from autonomy; patients may refuse life-saving care, advance directives must be respected, and euthanasia is not allowed in Ghana.","Confidentiality: share only what bears on treatment, never photograph patients without consent, and anonymise data for research."],"slides":35,"questions":[{"id":"l12-kc5x7b","s":7,"q":"Why would an ordinary person who cuts another be charged with assault, while a surgeon is not?","o":["The surgical team is protected by law from every kind of legal claim made against it","The surgical team intends treatment of illness, not deliberate harm","The patient is unconscious and so cannot make any complaint about what was done","The surgical team works only in government-owned hospitals where the law differs"],"a":1,"e":"In surgery there is no deliberate harm, but the team is offering treatment."},{"id":"l12-hkh8m4","s":8,"q":"Which feature makes ethics and law especially important in surgery?","o":["Patients lose self-control under anaesthesia, yet face a risk of injury or death","Surgery is only done on patients who agree with every single step in advance","Patients lose self-control under anaesthesia and face risk of injury or death","Surgery by fallible humans always follows safe fixed rules, so errors do not occur"],"a":2,"e":"Surgical procedures are done by fallible humans and carry risk, with the anaesthetised patient unable to protect themselves."},{"id":"l12-1itz4hw","s":9,"q":"What does the word 'ethos', the root of ethics, mean?","o":["Custom, meaning the habitual rules of a community","Honour, meaning the esteem a person earns from others","Character","Duty, meaning the tasks a person is paid to carry out"],"a":2,"e":"Ethics comes from the Greek word ethos, meaning character."},{"id":"l12-nb2coc","s":9,"q":"How do ethics and law differ?","o":["Ethics is enforced with penalties by courts; law is a code of conduct for professionals","Ethics applies only to doctors in hospitals; law applies only to nurses on wards","Ethics is a code of conduct; laws are rules enforced with penalties","Ethics is written by the courts of the land; law is written by the professions"],"a":2,"e":"Laws are recognised rules with penalties; ethics forms the code of conduct of professions."},{"id":"l12-ow7ep7","s":9,"q":"When may charges of malpractice be brought against a nurse?","o":["When the nurse follows the hospital's written procedures","When the nurse acts contrary to the laws guiding practice","When the nurse asks for help from a more senior colleague","When the nurse refers a patient to another specialist"],"a":1,"e":"Malpractice charges follow actions contrary to the laws guiding practice."},{"id":"l12-1b6mdgy","s":11,"q":"Which list gives the seven ethical principles in the lecture?","o":["Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, veracity, fidelity, justice and paternalism","Autonomy, honesty, loyalty, kindness, equality, consent, privacy and respect","Beneficence, caution, truth, honour, fairness, duty, law and compassion","Justice, privacy, consent, safety, rights, duty, respect and accountability"],"a":0,"e":"These seven principles are listed."},{"id":"l12-9g0t4s","s":12,"q":"What does the principle of autonomy mean?","o":["Rule by the surgical team, with the patient following the advice that is given","Rule by the family, with the patient simply agreeing to the choice they make","Self-rule by the patient, with the right to make informed choices free from outside pressure","Rule by the law, with the patient following the orders that a court has given"],"a":2,"e":"Autonomy comes from autos (self) and nomos (law or rule)."},{"id":"l12-bv3oc8","s":12,"q":"How do surgical staff respect autonomy before an operation?","o":["They obtain the signature first and explain the procedure to the patient afterwards","They decide for the patient when the decision seems clear to the whole team","They seek informed consent after giving the education needed for the decision","They give the information only to the family members of the patient"],"a":2,"e":"Informed consent must follow the education that helps the patient decide."},{"id":"l12-1nazv4z","s":13,"q":"A patient refuses an operation that would save his life, even after good education. What is the correct view?","o":["The nurse must persuade him again and again until he finally agrees to the operation","He has the right to refuse, and only a recognised court can overturn it","The surgeon may operate once the family gives written consent on his behalf","The nurse may override him if the whole team agrees that it is in his interest"],"a":1,"e":"Patients can refuse life-saving surgery; only a recognised law court may overturn that right."},{"id":"l12-17reiz4","s":14,"q":"Which point must be discussed with the patient when seeking informed consent?","o":["The cost of the surgeon's training","The names of other patients on the list","The consequences of no treatment at all","The nurse's personal opinion of the surgeon"],"a":2,"e":"Alternatives and the consequences of no treatment are part of informed consent."},{"id":"l12-y8zyml","s":14,"q":"Which list is part of informed consent discussion?","o":["The surgeon's fee, the theatre number, the date of discharge and the cost of drugs","The nurse's name, the ward number, the visiting hours and the meal times","The anaesthetic brand, the suture type, the blade size and the gown colour","The condition, type of surgery, expected side effects, unexpected hazards and alternatives"],"a":3,"e":"These are listed on the consent slide."},{"id":"l12-1acbklm","s":15,"q":"What does beneficence require of the nurse?","o":["Ensuring that all interventions are for the patient's overall benefit","Avoiding all interventions that cause any discomfort or pain to the patient","Following the wishes of the family in every decision that is made for the patient","Giving exactly the same care to every patient regardless of individual need"],"a":0,"e":"Beneficence is the duty to do good for the patient."},{"id":"l12-1t69dt7","s":16,"q":"A nurse raises the side rails for a patient at risk of falling. Which principle is she following?","o":["Beneficence, which means doing good for the patient","Non-maleficence","Veracity, which means telling the truth to the patient","Fidelity, which means keeping promises made to the patient"],"a":1,"e":"Non-maleficence is refraining from harm, including preventing anticipated harm."},{"id":"l12-1ixyt2f","s":17,"q":"What does veracity require?","o":["Telling the family everything first, before the patient has been informed at all","Telling the truth to patient and family in a polite, responsible manner","Hiding bad news from the patient until the patient asks directly about it","Telling the truth only when it is good news for the patient and the family"],"a":1,"e":"Veracity is the obligation not to lie or deceive, communicated with courtesy."},{"id":"l12-1iy2ekj","s":18,"q":"Which action shows fidelity?","o":["Measuring vital signs accurately, rather than copying the previous values in the chart","Measuring vital signs accurately rather than charting previous values","Telling a patient the truth about the prognosis in clear and simple terms","Treating all patients alike regardless of their social status or wealth"],"a":1,"e":"Fidelity is keeping promises and commitments, including accurate charting."},{"id":"l12-r5ynwo","s":18,"q":"Which document reminds nurses about fidelity to patients?","o":["The consent form","The theatre register","The nurses' pledge","The ward diary"],"a":2,"e":"The nurses' pledge reminds nurses of their fidelity."},{"id":"l12-1h2mek8","s":19,"q":"What does the principle of justice suggest?","o":["Every patient must get exactly the same treatment at all times, whatever their need","Patients with higher status must be treated before all the others on the ward","Like cases should be treated alike, with fair treatment free from bias","The oldest patients on the ward are always seen first, before any other patient"],"a":2,"e":"Justice means fair treatment without bias, though equity, need and entitlement may influence it."},{"id":"l12-197y068","s":19,"q":"Triaging and managing the most critical cases first is an example of which influence on justice?","o":["Legal entitlement","Equality","Individual needs","Veracity"],"a":2,"e":"Triage serves individual needs; children first is equity; special wards reflect legal entitlement."},{"id":"l12-16h4s0k","s":20,"q":"What is paternalism?","o":["The duty to tell the truth to a patient at all times, however painful it may be","The obligation to keep the promises that were made to the patient earlier","The intentional limiting of another's autonomy, justified by their welfare","The practice of treating all patients in exactly the same way on the ward"],"a":2,"e":"Paternalism limits autonomy by appeal to beneficence or welfare."},{"id":"l12-b1rlk8","s":20,"q":"Which act is given as an example of paternalism?","o":["Seeking consent for an operation","Telling a patient the diagnosis","Restraining an aggressive patient","Keeping a patient's records private"],"a":2,"e":"Restraint limits autonomy to prevent harm."},{"id":"l12-1ugtkb","s":21,"q":"Why must the nurse be careful before restraining a patient?","o":["Restraints are always forbidden under the law of Ghana in every health facility","Restraints suggest imprisonment and can cause harm or death","Restraints are never needed once the patient has become calm and quiet","Restraints are used only on children who are admitted to the ward"],"a":1,"e":"The risks of limiting autonomy must be weighed against the risk of injury."},{"id":"l12-64ceu5","s":21,"q":"Restraint should be guided by which of the following?","o":["The personal preference of the nurse in charge on that particular shift","The wishes of the patient's relatives who are waiting at the bedside","The advice of the other patients who share the ward with that patient","The health facility's protocols or legal department guidelines"],"a":3,"e":"The process must be justified, safe and guided by documented guidelines."},{"id":"l12-z1kjub","s":22,"q":"Which alternative should be tried before restraining a patient?","o":["Giving sedatives to the patient without a doctor's prescription to calm him quickly","A trained sitter or a willing family member to monitor continuously","Moving the patient at once to a different ward away from other patients","Leaving the patient alone in a quiet room until the behaviour stops"],"a":1,"e":"Other strategies include a dedicated sitter or a willing family member."},{"id":"l12-1ifffw6","s":23,"q":"What is an ethical dilemma?","o":["A clear conflict between two or more moral principles","A situation in which the law has been broken by a nurse","A situation in which the patient refuses to give consent","A disagreement between the nurse and the surgeon on a drug"],"a":0,"e":"A dilemma is a clear conflict between moral principles."},{"id":"l12-jpdr6b","s":23,"q":"Facing an ethical dilemma, what should the nurse consult?","o":["Only her own personal values and her own religious beliefs about the case","Laws, the code of ethics, facility policy, legal unit guidelines and best practice","Only the views of the patient's family and neighbours in the community","Only the nurse in charge of the ward on that shift, and nobody else"],"a":1,"e":"The lecture lists these sources; principles stay the same though situations vary."},{"id":"l12-1ocsma2","s":25,"q":"A patient who cannot decide for himself needs a decision on a life-saving treatment. Who decides?","o":["A guardian or a court","The nurse caring for him","The hospital's cleaning staff","The patient's other doctors in the unit"],"a":0,"e":"If incapable, a guardian or court makes the decision."},{"id":"l12-txi6vo","s":25,"q":"What are advance directives?","o":["Instructions given by the nurse after the operation is completed and the patient wakes","Rules set by the hospital about visiting hours, diet and dress for patients","Wishes stated ahead of time about care such as resuscitation or transfusion","Records kept by the court about previous cases involving the patient"],"a":2,"e":"Advance directives state a patient's wishes in advance."},{"id":"l12-1r3fyqs","s":26,"q":"What is a durable power of attorney for health care?","o":["A court order that allows a nurse to override the patient's own wishes about care","A form signed by a surgeon before every operation to protect the hospital","One person names another to make health care decisions for them","A hospital rule that lets relatives make all decisions for the patient"],"a":2,"e":"It is a type of advance directive in which another person decides on one's behalf."},{"id":"l12-d4xc47","s":26,"q":"Why should the nurse ask about advance directives?","o":["So that the patient can be discharged earlier from the ward","So that care does not go against the patient's wishes","So that the family can claim the patient's property later","So that the surgeon can avoid explaining the procedure"],"a":1,"e":"The nurse must enquire so as not to go contrary to them."},{"id":"l12-i5ag11","s":27,"q":"What is the legal position on euthanasia in Ghana?","o":["It is allowed if the patient and doctor agree to it","It is allowed if the family and the nurse agree to it","It is allowed if the patient has an incurable cancer","It is not allowed, even if the patient pleads for it"],"a":3,"e":"Euthanasia (mercy killing) is not allowed under Ghanaian law."},{"id":"l12-ndcgau","s":27,"q":"Why must nurses be cautious when giving opioids?","o":["A court may find intent to kill if an injection was virtually certain to kill","Opioids are never allowed to be given after surgery under any circumstances","Opioids can only be given by a doctor and never by a nurse in the ward","Opioids have no effect on pain in elderly patients who have had surgery"],"a":0,"e":"If an analgesic is virtually certain to kill the patient, a court might conclude intent to kill."},{"id":"l12-15s66kb","s":29,"q":"Which statement about confidentiality is correct?","o":["Information may be shared freely with relatives and friends who ask for it","Information from treatment must not be shared with anyone else without consent","Information may be shared with employers who request it in writing","Information may be shared once the patient has been discharged from care"],"a":1,"e":"The team must respect privacy and not share information without consent."},{"id":"l12-lb2chl","s":30,"q":"Under what circumstance may confidentiality be breached?","o":["By a relative's request","By a friend's request","By an employer's letter","By a court order"],"a":3,"e":"The lecture states that confidentiality may be breached by court orders."},{"id":"l12-wqa0ih","s":30,"q":"With whom may a nurse privately discuss a patient's care?","o":["Any colleague on the ward who is interested in the case and wants to learn from it","Friends who work in another hospital in the town and ask out of concern","Relatives of the patient who ask for an update by telephone at any time","Team members, only on information with a direct bearing on treatment"],"a":3,"e":"Discussion is limited to team members and relevant treatment information."},{"id":"l12-1kgbmbk","s":31,"q":"What must be done with photographs taken with consent for education or publication?","o":["They must show the patient's face clearly","They must be kept in the nurse's own phone","They must hide the patient's identity","They must be shared with the ward group chat"],"a":2,"e":"Consented photographs for education must hide identity."},{"id":"l12-1v2rwnn","s":31,"q":"What is required before information from patients is used for research or service improvement?","o":["It must be shown to the patient's relatives first","It must be anonymised and ethically approved","It must be kept in the ward until the patient is discharged","It must be signed by the nurse in charge only"],"a":1,"e":"Research information must be anonymised and ethically approved."}]}]};
